export const simulationReports=[{"id": "report-sunhours", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Direct sun hours on ground, roofs and facades: a ray-cast method checked step for step against Ladybug", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Direct sun hours", "Solar position", "Ray casting", "Overshadowing", "Ladybug", "Verification"], "highlights": ["Sun hours counts the time steps in which a ray from a point to the sun meets no geometry.", "Solar position follows the NOAA implementation of Meeus; it differs from NREL SPA by at most 0.015°.", "Against an exact shadow solution the ground hours agree to 0.000 h on 44,686 samples.", "Against ladybug-core's Direct Sun Hours procedure every point agrees to 32-bit precision.", "The result depends on the chosen day, time step and grid; no weather or cloud is involved."], "abstract": "Direct sun hours, the number of hours a point receives unobstructed beam sunlight on a chosen day, is one of the oldest and most widely used overshadowing metrics in architecture and planning [1, 2]. SimuSite's Sun hours study computes it on ground cells, roofs and facades by casting a ray from each sample point towards the sun at regular time steps and counting the steps in which the ray is not blocked by the model or its surroundings. This report sets out the model, lists every parameter with its source, and summarises the verification. The solar position (NOAA's implementation of Meeus's algorithms [3, 4]) was compared with the NREL Solar Position Algorithm [5] at 17,014 positions: the largest error is 0.015° in altitude. Shadow casting was compared with an exact ray–box solution for a 200 m wall at Vancouver on 44,686 ground samples (difference 0.000 h), and the whole procedure was reproduced with ladybug-core's Direct Sun Hours [6] in four scenes, where every point agrees to the 32-bit floating-point precision in which results are stored. The metric itself has known limits: it is weather-independent, it weights a minute of low winter sun the same as a minute of high summer sun, and its value depends on the time step and grid spacing chosen by the user.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nAccess to direct sunlight is regulated or recommended in many jurisdictions. In the United Kingdom, BRE Report 209 recommends that at least half of an amenity area receive two hours of sun on 21 March [1]; Chinese residential codes set minimum hours of sunlight on the \"great cold day\" or winter solstice for dwellings [2]. Architects also use sun hours informally, to place gardens, terraces and play areas or to compare massing options. The metric is simple to state: for a point and a day, count the time during which the sun is above the horizon and the line from the point to the sun is not obstructed.\n\nThis report documents how SimuSite computes it, where the numbers come from, and how far the implementation can be trusted. It is one of a series of reports, one per SimuSite simulation, written after an audit of each engine's inputs on 20 November 2026.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Solar position\n\nFor each time step the sun's altitude and azimuth are computed with the algorithm published by NOAA's Global Monitoring Laboratory, which implements the low-precision formulae of Meeus [3, 4]: the Julian century from the date and local standard time, the geometric mean longitude and anomaly of the sun, the equation of centre, apparent longitude with nutation and aberration corrections, the obliquity of the ecliptic, the solar declination and the equation of time. The hour angle follows from true solar time, and altitude and azimuth from the usual spherical-trigonometry relations. Atmospheric refraction is not applied. Times are local standard time; daylight saving is not applied, so that a day's result does not change with a clock change.\n\nThe Workspace version of the study (the one most users run) uses the Ladybug sun path (ladybug-core's `Sunpath`), which also follows the NOAA formulation; the two were compared and agree to 3 × 10⁻¹³ degrees in altitude over 567,648 cases (Section 4).\n\n### 2.2. Sampling and time steps\n\nThe analysis region is divided into square cells. Each ground cell is tested at its centre, raised 0.1 m along the surface normal so that the ray does not start inside the ground; roofs and facades are divided into squares in the same way. The day is divided into steps of 5 to 60 minutes. A sun vector more than 90° from a surface's normal counts as not received (the surface faces away from the sun), exactly as in Ladybug's Direct Sun Hours [6].\n\n### 2.3. Shadow test\n\nFor each sample and each sun vector, a ray is cast towards the sun through a bounding-volume hierarchy of the model's triangles (three-mesh-bvh). The ray is clear if it hits nothing; the hours at the sample are the number of clear steps divided by the steps per hour. All geometry is opaque: glass, trees and translucent canopies block the sun completely unless the user removes them. Terrain and the site's surrounding buildings are included when they are part of the model.\n\n### 2.4. Results\n\nThe study reports mean hours on the ground, the share of ground with at least two hours (the BRE amenity test [1]), and the corresponding figures for roofs and facades. A \"range\" mode accumulates hours over several days.\n\n**Sunshine from the weather (added 23 November 2026).** Sun hours assumes a clear sky. To show how often the sun is actually out, the study reads the project weather file for the month of the chosen day and counts the hours with the sun above the horizon at mid-hour, and among them the hours whose direct normal irradiance is at least 120 W/m². That is the World Meteorological Organization's threshold for sunshine: sunshine duration is the time during which direct solar irradiance exceeds 120 W/m² [7, 8]. The share of sunny hours is reported, and so are expected sun hours on the ground, the clear-sky hours multiplied by that share. The product assumes that sunshine is equally likely at every hour of the day, which is a simplification: in many climates mornings are cloudier than afternoons, or the reverse. The weather file gives hourly integrated irradiance (Wh/m² in each hour [9]), so an hour counts as sunny when its mean direct irradiance reaches the threshold; the WMO definition is instantaneous, so partly sunny hours are counted all or nothing.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs of the Sun hours study and the evidence behind each.\n\n| Parameter | Value in SimuSite | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Solar position | NOAA / Meeus formulation; Ladybug sun path in the Workspace | Meeus [3]; NOAA calculator [4]; ladybug-core [6] | Sourced and verified (Section 4) |\n| Time zone, latitude, longitude | From the project site | User's site; the city list holds rounded coordinates | Input data |\n| Time step | 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 min (user) | Ladybug timestep convention [6] | User choice; affects precision |\n| Ground offset | 0.1 m along the normal | Ladybug Direct Sun Hours default offset [6] | Sourced |\n| Back-facing rule | Sun > 90° from normal counts 0 | Ladybug Direct Sun Hours [6] | Sourced |\n| \"Two hours on 21 March\" | Summary threshold | BRE 209 [1] | Sourced (a UK guideline, not a universal rule) |\n| Geometry | Model triangles, opaque | — | Modelling assumption |\n| Refraction | Not applied | Meeus discusses it [3] | Known simplification (≤ about 0.6° near the horizon); kept off on 23 November 2026 so that results stay identical to Ladybug and the SPA comparison of Section 4 |\n| Sunshine threshold | Direct normal irradiance ≥ 120 W/m², hourly EPW values, month of the chosen day | WMO definition of sunshine duration [7, 8]; EPW fields [9] | Sourced threshold; hourly values approximate the instantaneous definition; the share is applied evenly through the day (added 23 November 2026) |\n\nNo parameter of this study was found to be unsourced. The one modelling assumption with a practical effect is that all geometry is opaque.\n\n## 4. Verification\n\nThree independent checks are run by the scripts in `tests/validation/` and were re-run for this audit.\n\n**Solar position against NREL SPA.** NREL's Solar Position Algorithm has a stated uncertainty of ±0.0003° [5]. Through pvlib's `spa_python`, the engine's sun was compared at 9 latitudes from 60° S to 70° N, 3 longitudes, every 7th day and every daylight hour of 2026: 17,014 positions. Mean error 0.003° in altitude and azimuth; maximum 0.015° in altitude and 0.012° in azimuth. At a 5-minute step the sun moves about 1.25°, so the position error is two orders of magnitude below the discretisation.\n\n**Shadows against an exact solution.** A wall 200 m long, 1 m thick and 10 m high stands east–west on open ground at Vancouver (49.28° N). For every ground sample the engine produced, the reference intersects the engine's own sun rays with the wall by an exact slab test. On 20 March (17,568 samples) and 21 December (27,118 samples) the mean and maximum differences are 0.000 h. As a hand check, at the equinox the noon sun stands 40.7° high and the wall casts an 11.6 m shadow; the engine gives 0.84 h 10 m north of the wall and 11.83 h at 15 m.\n\n**Procedure against Ladybug.** `lb_sunhours.py` rebuilds the LB Direct Sun Hours component step for step with ladybug-core, using an exact Möller–Trumbore ray–triangle test in place of Rhino's mesh ray, on a box-and-L scene in four cases (Vancouver 21 March at 15 min; 21 December at 5 min; 21 June at 1 h with north rotated 20°; Sydney 21 June at 10 min, 8:00–16:00). On 2,461 ground points and 203 surface squares per case, the largest difference is 3 × 10⁻⁷ h, the precision of the 32-bit floats in which results are stored.\n\n**Sunshine share.** `tests/sunshine-share.test.mjs` builds a March of hourly rows at Toronto with direct normal irradiance of 119 W/m² on days 1–10, 120 W/m² on days 11–20 and 800 W/m² on days 21–31 in daylight, and 500 W/m² at night. The function counts exactly the daylit hours of days 11–31 as sunny (120 W/m² is sunshine, 119 is not), ignores the night hours and other months, and finds about twelve daylit hours a day, as expected at the equinox. In the Workspace, `tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs` runs Sun hours on the hospital example for 21 March: the Toronto weather had sunshine in 222 of 362 daylit March hours (61 %), so the 10.2 clear-sky hours on the ground become 6.3 expected hours.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe checks establish that SimuSite computes what the metric defines, and computes it as Ladybug does. They do not make the metric itself a measure of solar access. Sun hours ignores cloud: a site in Vancouver and one in Phoenix with the same geometry get the same answer. The sunshine share from the project weather now states how far the clear-sky hours overstate the sun a site actually gets in that month. It counts a minute of sun at 5° altitude as much as a minute at 60°, although the energy received differs by an order of magnitude; for energy, the Solar radiation study (a companion report) is the appropriate tool. Results depend on the time step: a step of Δt minutes can shift a point's hours by up to Δt at each transition between sun and shade, so a 15-minute step can put a point on either side of a two-hour threshold. Grid cells smaller than the features of interest are needed near building edges. Finally, the solar position does not include refraction, which raises the apparent sun by up to about half a degree at the horizon [3]; this mostly affects the first and last minutes of the day.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nSun hours in SimuSite is a direct, exact-geometry implementation of a well-defined metric. Its sun position is accurate to 0.015° against NREL SPA, its shadow test reproduces an exact solution, and its procedure matches Ladybug's to floating-point precision. Results should be reported with the day, time step and grid spacing used, and read as access to direct sun on that day, not as energy or comfort.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [emb/sunhours.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/sunhours.html), [emb/modeler.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/modeler.html). Tests: [tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs), [tests/validation/sun_position.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/sun_position.py), [tests/validation/sun_shadow.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/sun_shadow.py), [tests/validation/lb_sunhours.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/lb_sunhours.py), [tests/sun-period.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/sun-period.cjs), [tests/sunshine-share.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/sunshine-share.test.mjs). This article's source: [sunhours.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/sunhours.md)."}], "references": ["P.J. Littlefair, Site layout planning for daylight and sunlight: a guide to good practice, BR 209, 2nd ed., BRE Press, Watford, 2011.", "Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development of the People's Republic of China, GB 50180-2018 Standard for urban residential area planning and design, China Architecture & Building Press, Beijing, 2018.", "J. Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms, 2nd ed., Willmann-Bell, Richmond, VA, 1998.", "NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory, Solar calculation details (NOAA Solar Calculator), [gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc](https://gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc/calcdetails.html).", "I. Reda, A. Andreas, Solar position algorithm for solar radiation applications, Sol. Energy 76 (2004) 577–589. [doi:10.1016/j.solener.2003.12.003](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.solener.2003.12.003)", "M.S. Roudsari, M. Pak, Ladybug: a parametric environmental plugin for Grasshopper to help designers create an environmentally-conscious design, in: Proc. Building Simulation 2013, IBPSA, Chambéry, 2013, pp. 3128–3135.", "World Meteorological Organization, Guide to Instruments and Methods of Observation (WMO-No. 8), Volume I, chapter on the measurement of sunshine duration, WMO, Geneva (definition adopted in 2003: direct solar irradiance exceeding 120 W/m²).", "Japan Meteorological Agency, CP7 Sunshine (training material on the CIMO Guide chapter on sunshine duration). [jma.go.jp](https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/jma-eng/jma-center/ric/Our%20activities/International/CP7-Sunshine.pdf)", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary (field Direct Normal Radiation, Wh/m²), EnergyPlus documentation, 2023."], "category": "research", "simulation": "sunhours", "summary": "How SimuSite counts hours of direct sun, where every input comes from (the NOAA/Meeus solar position, the Ladybug Direct Sun Hours procedure), and the checks against NREL SPA, an exact shadow solution and ladybug-core.", "image": "img/home/sunhours.webp", "imageAlt": "Sun hours on the ground around a building, warmer colours for more hours of direct sun"}}, {"id": "report-radiation", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Solar irradiation on ground, roofs and facades from an hourly weather file: Perez sky, Tregenza patches and a check against pvlib", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Solar irradiation", "Perez sky model", "Tregenza sky subdivision", "EPW weather data", "Photovoltaic potential"], "highlights": ["Hourly direct and diffuse irradiance from an EPW file is distributed over 145 Tregenza sky patches.", "The diffuse sky follows Perez et al. (1990): isotropic dome, circumsolar disc and horizon band.", "On six open planes the engine agrees with pvlib's Perez model within 0.2 %.", "Inter-building reflections are not modelled; ground reflection uses the weather file's hourly albedo, weighted by irradiance, and 0.2 when the file has none.", "The PV figure uses PVWatts V5's module, loss and inverter equations on the hourly weather (standard modules, about 15 %), for modules laid flat: a screening estimate."], "abstract": "The Solar radiation study estimates how much solar energy reaches each part of a site and building over a chosen period, in kWh/m², from an hourly weather file in EnergyPlus (EPW) format [1]. Beam irradiance is traced to the sun through the model; diffuse irradiance is split by the model of Perez et al. [2] into an isotropic part, a circumsolar part that is shaded like the sun, and a horizon band, and the sky is divided into the 145 patches of Tregenza [3]. Ground-reflected light uses an isotropic albedo of 0.2. This report describes the model and its inputs, and the verification against pvlib [4]: on a synthetic year at Toronto, irradiation on horizontal, vertical and tilted open planes agrees with pvlib's Perez transposition within 0.2 %, and grouping hourly sun positions into 415 clusters about 3° apart changes totals by less than 0.1 %. The rooftop PV estimate built on these totals rests on two assumed constants (20 % module efficiency, performance ratio 0.8) that are typical of current crystalline-silicon systems but not project-specific.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nIrradiation maps are used early in design to locate photovoltaic panels, to judge solar gains through facades and to find surfaces that need shading. The physically based way to compute them is to combine a weather record of beam and diffuse irradiance with a model of how diffuse light is distributed over the sky, and to trace both through the geometry [5]. SimuSite does this in the browser, within the Sun hours engine, so that the same sightlines that give sun hours also give energy.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Weather data\n\nThe study reads the project's EPW file: global horizontal (GHI), direct normal (DNI) and diffuse horizontal (DHI) irradiance for each of the 8,760 hours, as defined in the EnergyPlus weather file data dictionary [1]. Hour h covers (h − 1):00 to h:00 local standard time. Short runs of missing values are filled from neighbouring hours; radiation is zero at night. The sun position for each hour is taken at the middle of the hour.\n\n### 2.2. Sky model\n\nThe sky is divided into the 145 patches of Tregenza [3]: eight altitude bands of 12° (the last a 6° cap) with 30, 30, 24, 24, 18, 12, 6 and 1 patches. Diffuse horizontal irradiance is split with the Perez 1990 model [2] into three components whose weights depend on the hour's sky clearness ε and brightness Δ, using the \"allsitescomposite1990\" coefficient set as distributed with pvlib [4] and Radiance's gendaymtx: an isotropic dome (the same radiance in every patch), a circumsolar disc that travels with the sun, and a horizon band. The circumsolar part is shaded by buildings as the sun is, which matters for facades in clear climates.\n\n### 2.3. Grouping the sun\n\nTracing every daylight hour (about 4,400 a year) from every sample would be slow in a browser. Beam hours are therefore grouped into sun positions about 3° apart; each group keeps the vector sum of DNI·ŝ over its hours, so the energy is conserved exactly and only the direction of the sightline is approximated.\n\n### 2.4. Irradiation on a surface\n\nFor a sample with normal n, irradiation is the sum of (i) beam and circumsolar from the visible sun groups, projected on n; (ii) the isotropic dome through the visible sky patches; (iii) the horizon band, seen by vertical faces; and (iv) ground-reflected light from the lower half of the view at albedo ρ = 0.2, taken as unobstructed and isotropic.\n\n### 2.5. Rooftop PV estimate\n\nRoof samples that receive at least 80 % of what open level ground receives are summed as candidate PV area. Energy is estimated as area × irradiation × η × PR, i.e. modules laid flat on all of that area.\n\nUntil 23 November 2026, η was 0.20 and PR a fixed 0.80. Both now come from the PVWatts Version 5 manual [6]. η is 0.15, the nominal efficiency of its \"standard\" module type (Table 3: about 15 %, glass cover, temperature coefficient −0.47 %/°C). PR is computed for the site and period, hour by hour from the weather file, for an unshaded module laid flat:\n- DC power follows the manual's eq. (8), P_dc = (I/1000) P_dc0 [1 + γ (T_cell − 25)].\n- The default system losses of 14 % (Table 6, combined by eq. 9) are taken off the DC energy.\n- The inverter follows eq. (10): part-load efficiency η = (η_nom/η_ref)(−0.0162 ζ − 0.0059/ζ + 0.9858), with η_nom = 0.96 and η_ref = 0.9637. Output is clipped at the AC rating, eq. (11), with the default DC-to-AC ratio of 1.1.\n- PVWatts computes the cell temperature with Fuentes' transient heat-balance model and an installed nominal operating cell temperature (INOCT) of 45 °C for an open rack [6]. The study uses the steady-state relation T_cell = T_air + (INOCT − 20) · I/800 with that INOCT, the simplified form of the NOCT relation (Duffie and Beckman give it with an additional efficiency term [5]), which ignores wind and thermal lag.\n- The angle-of-incidence losses of the module cover are not applied.\n\nPR is then the period's AC energy divided by its irradiation on the plane in kWh/m², per kW of modules.\n\n**Ground albedo (added 23 November 2026).** EPW files carry an hourly albedo field [1]. In each hour the ground-reflected irradiance is proportional to ρ × GHI, so over a period the GHI-weighted mean albedo reproduces the hour-by-hour total exactly. The study uses it when at least 90 % of the period's GHI has an albedo value, and 0.2 otherwise. Snow-covered ground in winter months then reflects as the file records rather than at 0.2.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs of the Solar radiation study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Weather (GHI, DNI, DHI) | Project EPW | EnergyPlus data dictionary [1]; files from climate.onebuilding.org or user | Measured / typical-year data |\n| Sky subdivision | 145 patches | Tregenza 1987 [3] | Sourced |\n| Diffuse sky distribution | Perez 1990, allsitescomposite1990 | Perez et al. [2]; as in pvlib [4] | Sourced and verified |\n| Sun grouping | ≈ 3° clusters, energy-conserving | Engine design | Verified (< 0.1 % effect) |\n| Ground albedo | EPW hourly albedo, GHI-weighted; 0.2 when missing | EPW field Albedo [1]; 0.2 common default (pvlib, PVWatts [6]) | Measured / typical-year data where present (added 23 November 2026) |\n| Inter-reflection between buildings | Not modelled | — | Known simplification |\n| PV module efficiency | 15 % (standard module) | PVWatts V5 Table 3 [6] | Sourced (replaced 20 % on 23 November 2026) |\n| Temperature coefficient | −0.47 %/°C | PVWatts V5 Table 3 [6] | Sourced |\n| Cell temperature | T_air + (45 − 20)·I/800 | INOCT 45 °C from PVWatts V5 [6]; simplified NOCT relation [5] | Sourced constants; steady-state approximation of PVWatts' Fuentes model |\n| System losses | 14 % | PVWatts V5 Table 6, eq. (9) [6] | Sourced default |\n| Inverter | η_nom 0.96, η_ref 0.9637, eq. (10); DC/AC 1.1, clipping eq. (11) | PVWatts V5 [6] | Sourced |\n| Performance ratio | Computed per site and period from the above | PVWatts V5 equations [6]; PR as defined in IEC 61724-1 [7] | Derived (replaced the fixed 0.80 on 23 November 2026) |\n| \"Worth a look\" threshold | ≥ 80 % of open-ground irradiation | Engine heuristic | Assumption, flagged |\n\nThe audit found no fabricated constants. The PV efficiency and performance ratio, which were assumptions at the audit, now follow PVWatts V5; the \"worth a look\" threshold remains an assumption; the report and the interface should not present the PV figure as a yield prediction.\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/validation/climate_lib.py` runs the engine's own functions in Node against pvlib's `get_total_irradiance` [4] on a synthetic year at Toronto (Ineichen clear sky with day-to-day cloud), albedo 0.2.\n\nTable 2. Annual irradiation on open planes, engine against pvlib.\n\n| Plane | Engine (kWh/m²) | pvlib Perez | Difference | pvlib isotropic |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Horizontal | 1,112.7 | 1,111.9 | +0.07 % | 1,112.0 |\n| Vertical south | 974.6 | 974.7 | −0.01 % | 879.1 |\n| Vertical east | 701.9 | 703.0 | −0.16 % | 655.4 |\n| Vertical west | 700.6 | 701.8 | −0.18 % | 655.5 |\n| Vertical north | 313.8 | 314.1 | −0.09 % | 344.6 |\n| 30° tilt south | 1,352.0 | 1,351.6 | +0.02 % | 1,274.8 |\n\nEvery plane is within the 2 % tolerance fixed before the test. The isotropic column shows why the sky model matters: an even sky places 7–11 % less energy on sunlit facades and 9 % more on north facades. The cost of grouping the sun is at most 0.08 % (north facade). Shading by buildings uses the same ray test as Sun hours, which is verified against an exact solution and against Ladybug in the companion report.\n\n**Albedo and PVWatts.** `tests/pvwatts-albedo.test.mjs` checks the new functions by hand:\n- **Albedo:** two hours at 100 and 300 W/m² with albedos 0.2 and 0.8 give a weighted albedo of 0.65; a file with too little albedo data falls back to 0.2.\n- **PVWatts at standard conditions:** with the cell at 25 °C (air at −6.25 °C under 1,000 W/m²) and 14 % losses, the AC output matches eq. (10) computed independently, with an inverter efficiency of 0.961. Without losses, the same hour clips at the AC rating of 1/1.1 kW per kW of modules.\n- **PVWatts with a hot cell:** at 800 W/m² and 20 °C air the cell reaches 45 °C, the DC power is 0.8 × (1 − 0.0047 × 20) × 0.86 kW, and thin film, with its smaller temperature coefficient, yields more.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe engine reproduces the reference transposition model on unobstructed planes, so its errors on real buildings come from what it leaves out rather than from its arithmetic. Light reflected from neighbouring facades is not modelled; in dense streets this under-predicts irradiation on lower facades, typically by a few per cent for dark facades and more for light or glazed ones [5]. The ground is taken as unobstructed for reflected light and has a fixed albedo of 0.2, which under-predicts reflection over snow. Results are only as representative as the weather file: a typical meteorological year represents long-term average conditions, not any particular year.\n\nThe PV estimate multiplies flat-plate irradiation by a PVWatts-derived efficiency and performance ratio. It now accounts for temperature, system losses and the inverter as PVWatts V5 does, with a steady-state cell temperature in place of PVWatts' transient model, but it still neglects module tilt and row spacing, cover reflection losses and the shading pattern on each module. It is useful to compare options and to judge order of magnitude; a yield estimate should use a dedicated tool such as PVWatts [6].\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Solar radiation study implements a standard, documented sky model and agrees with pvlib within 0.2 % on open planes. Its outputs are suitable for comparing surfaces and massing options. The rooftop PV figure rests on assumed efficiency and performance ratio and should be labelled as an estimate.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [emb/lib/climate.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/climate.js), [emb/sunhours.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/sunhours.html). Tests: [tests/validation/climate_lib.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/climate_lib.py), [tests/climate-studies.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/climate-studies.cjs), [tests/pvwatts-albedo.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/pvwatts-albedo.test.mjs). This article's source: [radiation.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/radiation.md)."}], "references": ["U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: Weather Converter Program and EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "R. Perez, P. Ineichen, R. Seals, J. Michalsky, R. Stewart, Modeling daylight availability and irradiance components from direct and global irradiance, Sol. Energy 44 (1990) 271–289. [doi:10.1016/0038-092X(90)90055-H](https://doi.org/10.1016/0038-092X(90)90055-H)", "P.R. Tregenza, Subdivision of the sky hemisphere for luminance measurements, Light. Res. Technol. 19 (1987) 13–14.", "W.F. Holmgren, C.W. Hansen, M.A. Mikofski, pvlib python: a python package for modeling solar energy systems, J. Open Source Softw. 3 (2018) 884. [doi:10.21105/joss.00884](https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.00884)", "J.A. Duffie, W.A. Beckman, Solar Engineering of Thermal Processes, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ, 2013.", "A.P. Dobos, PVWatts Version 5 Manual, NREL/TP-6A20-62641, National Renewable Energy Laboratory, Golden, CO, 2014.", "IEC 61724-1:2021, Photovoltaic system performance – Part 1: Monitoring, International Electrotechnical Commission, Geneva, 2021."], "category": "research", "simulation": "radiation", "summary": "How the Solar radiation study turns an EPW weather file into kWh/m² on every surface, the sources of its sky model and constants, its agreement with pvlib's Perez model within 0.2 %, and the assumptions behind the rooftop PV estimate.", "image": "img/home/radiation.webp", "imageAlt": "Annual solar irradiation on the roofs and facades of a building, from blue for little to yellow for much"}}, {"id": "report-utci", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Outdoor thermal comfort with the Universal Thermal Climate Index: weather, sun and shade, and a SolarCal estimate of mean radiant temperature", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["UTCI", "Outdoor thermal comfort", "Mean radiant temperature", "SolarCal", "Urban microclimate"], "highlights": ["UTCI is computed with the official 6th-order polynomial; it matches pythermalcomfort to 1.4 × 10⁻¹² °C.", "Mean radiant temperature combines long-wave exchange with sky and ground and SolarCal short-wave gain.", "Buildings change radiation (sun, sky view) but not wind or air temperature in this study.", "Stress categories follow the published UTCI assessment scale.", "Surface temperatures of sunlit paving and walls are not modelled, so heat stress on hot afternoons is underestimated."], "abstract": "The Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI) expresses the combined effect of air temperature, humidity, wind and radiation on a person outdoors as the air temperature of a reference environment that would produce the same physiological response [1, 2]. SimuSite's Outdoor thermal comfort study computes the UTCI for every counted hour of an EPW weather year at each ground cell, in sun or in shade as the model dictates, and reports the share of hours with no thermal stress, heat stress and cold stress. Air temperature, humidity and wind come from the weather file; mean radiant temperature (MRT) is estimated from the sky temperature, the cell's sky view and the SolarCal model of short-wave gain on a standing person [3, 4]. This report sets out the model, the provenance of every constant, and the verification: the UTCI polynomial matches pythermalcomfort [5] to 1.4 × 10⁻¹² °C on 6,000 random states, and the short-wave part of MRT matches SolarCal to 0.027 K. The largest simplifications are that buildings do not change wind speed in this study and that sunlit surfaces are assumed to be at air temperature, which underestimates heat stress in sunny, sheltered places.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nOutdoor comfort influences whether squares, courtyards and terraces are used, and is increasingly assessed in planning submissions. Among the many indices proposed, UTCI was developed by COST Action 730 as a physiologically grounded index applicable across climates, based on the multi-node Fiala model of human thermoregulation and an adaptive clothing model [1, 2]. Because the full model is expensive, its authors published a sixth-order polynomial approximation as the operational procedure [2]. Design tools such as Ladybug use the same approximation.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Inputs from the weather file\n\nFor each counted hour, the study reads dry-bulb temperature, relative humidity, wind speed at 10 m, and the radiation fields of the EPW file [6]. Vapour pressure is computed from saturation pressure over water by Hardy's ITS-90 formulation, as in the official UTCI program [2]. The 10 m wind is used as UTCI expects (the index is defined for wind at 10 m), clamped to the polynomial's valid range of 0.5–17 m/s. Wind is not modified by buildings in this study; the Wind flow study addresses that separately.\n\n### 2.2. Mean radiant temperature\n\nMRT for a standing person is the sum of a long-wave and a short-wave part. The long-wave part assumes the upper half of the body exchanges with the sky in proportion to the cell's sky view factor, at a sky temperature derived from the hour's downwelling long-wave radiation in the EPW (or, where it is missing, from a clear-sky emissivity depending on dew point after Berdahl and Martin [7]); the rest of the surroundings, ground and walls, is taken at air temperature. The short-wave part is SolarCal, the model adopted in ASHRAE 55 Appendix C [3, 4]: direct beam on the projected area of the body (the projected-area factor f_p from the ASHRAE 55 table for a standing person, averaged over azimuth), diffuse sky in proportion to sky view, and ground-reflected light at albedo 0.2, with body short-wave absorptivity 0.7, long-wave emissivity 0.95, effective radiating area factor 0.725 and radiative heat transfer coefficient 6 W/m²K. Beam is applied only if the cell sees the sun in that hour, using the same ray test as Sun hours.\n\n### 2.3. UTCI and stress categories\n\nUTCI is then evaluated with the operational polynomial, transcribed from the open pythermalcomfort implementation of the official Fortran routine [2, 5]. Hours are classified on the UTCI assessment scale [1]: below −40 °C extreme cold stress, −40 to −27 very strong, −27 to −13 strong, −13 to 0 moderate, 0 to 9 slight cold stress, 9 to 26 no thermal stress, 26 to 32 moderate heat stress, 32 to 38 strong, 38 to 46 very strong, above 46 extreme heat stress. The study reports, per cell, the share of counted hours with no thermal stress, with heat stress (above 26 °C) and with cold stress (below 9 °C). To keep the run fast, UTCI is precomputed for each hour at 11 sky-view values in sun and in shade and interpolated for each cell.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Outdoor thermal comfort study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UTCI polynomial | 6th order, official coefficients | Bröde et al. [2]; pythermalcomfort [5] | Sourced, verified |\n| Valid range | Ta −50 to 50 °C; Tmrt − Ta −30 to 70 K; va 0.5–17 m/s | UTCI operational procedure [2] | Sourced; out-of-range hours are counted and reported |\n| Saturation vapour pressure | Hardy 1998 (ITS-90) | As in the UTCI program [2] | Sourced |\n| Stress categories | Ten classes, thresholds above | Jendritzky et al. / Błażejczyk et al. [1, 8] | Sourced |\n| Projected-area factor f_p | ASHRAE 55 standing table, mean over azimuth | ASHRAE 55-2020 App. C [3] | Sourced |\n| Absorptivity, emissivity, f_eff, h_r | 0.7, 0.95, 0.725, 6 W/m²K | SolarCal defaults [3, 4] | Sourced |\n| Ground albedo | 0.2 | Common default | Sourced default |\n| Sky temperature | From EPW horizontal IR; else Berdahl & Martin | EPW data dictionary [6]; [7] | Sourced |\n| Surroundings temperature | Equal to air temperature | Engine simplification | Assumption, flagged (see Discussion) |\n| Wind | EPW 10 m speed, not changed by buildings | Engine simplification | Assumption, flagged |\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/validation/climate_lib.py` runs the engine's functions in Node and compares them with pythermalcomfort [5]. UTCI on 6,000 random states inside the polynomial's range (air −45 to 48 °C, radiant minus air −25 to 65 K, wind 0.5–16 m/s, humidity 5–100 %): largest difference 1.4 × 10⁻¹² °C (tolerance 0.01 °C). Short-wave MRT gain for a sunlit standing person under an open sky, averaged over facing directions as the engine assumes, against `solar_gain` (SolarCal): 400 states, largest difference 0.027 K, mean 0.007 K (tolerance 0.05 K). The sun/shade test reuses the verified Sun hours ray casting.\n\nThe audit run on 20 November 2026 executed the study end to end on a two-storey test block with a Chicago O'Hare TMY3 weather file [9]; it completed and produced per-cell shares without errors.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe verification shows that SimuSite evaluates the published index correctly for the inputs it builds. The uncertainty therefore lies in the inputs, chiefly MRT and wind. Treating all surroundings as at air temperature ignores that sunlit asphalt and south-facing walls can be 10–20 K warmer than the air on summer afternoons, which raises MRT and UTCI by several degrees near them; conversely, shaded north walls and cool vegetation are not credited. Wind is the station's 10 m speed everywhere; in sheltered courtyards the real wind is lower (warmer in winter, more heat stress in summer), and in corners and gaps higher. Results are therefore most reliable for comparing sun and shade patterns between design options under the same weather, and least reliable for absolute heat stress in dense, paved settings, where a tool coupling surface temperatures and CFD wind is needed.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe study implements UTCI and SolarCal exactly as published and is suited to screening where sun and shade change comfort over a year. Its fixed-wind and air-temperature-surroundings assumptions should be stated with every result.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [emb/lib/climate.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/climate.js), [emb/sunhours.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/sunhours.html). Tests: [tests/validation/climate_lib.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/climate_lib.py), [tests/climate-studies.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/climate-studies.cjs). This article's source: [utci.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/utci.md)."}], "references": ["G. Jendritzky, R. de Dear, G. Havenith, UTCI—Why another thermal index?, Int. J. Biometeorol. 56 (2012) 421–428. [doi:10.1007/s00484-011-0513-7](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-011-0513-7)", "P. Bröde, D. Fiala, K. Błażejczyk, I. Holmér, G. Jendritzky, B. Kampmann, B. Tinz, G. Havenith, Deriving the operational procedure for the Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI), Int. J. Biometeorol. 56 (2012) 481–494. [doi:10.1007/s00484-011-0454-1](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-011-0454-1)", "ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 55-2020, Thermal Environmental Conditions for Human Occupancy, Appendix C, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2020.", "E. Arens, T. Hoyt, X. Zhou, L. Huang, H. Zhang, S. Schiavon, Modeling the comfort effects of short-wave solar radiation indoors, Build. Environ. 88 (2015) 3–9. [doi:10.1016/j.buildenv.2014.09.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.buildenv.2014.09.004)", "F. Tartarini, S. Schiavon, pythermalcomfort: A Python package for thermal comfort research, SoftwareX 12 (2020) 100578. [doi:10.1016/j.softx.2020.100578](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.softx.2020.100578)", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "P. Berdahl, M. Martin, Emissivity of clear skies, Sol. Energy 32 (1984) 663–664. [doi:10.1016/0038-092X(84)90144-0](https://doi.org/10.1016/0038-092X(84)90144-0)", "K. Błażejczyk, G. Jendritzky, P. Bröde, D. Fiala, G. Havenith, Y. Epstein, A. Psikuta, B. Kampmann, An introduction to the Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI), Geogr. Pol. 86 (2013) 5–10. [doi:10.7163/GPol.2013.1](https://doi.org/10.7163/GPol.2013.1)", "S. Wilcox, W. Marion, Users Manual for TMY3 Data Sets, NREL/TP-581-43156, National Renewable Energy Laboratory, Golden, CO, 2008."], "category": "research", "simulation": "utci", "summary": "How the Outdoor thermal comfort study computes UTCI for every hour of an EPW year in sun and shade, the published sources of the polynomial, the stress categories and the radiant-temperature model, its agreement with pythermalcomfort to 1e-12 °C, and what it leaves out.", "image": "img/home/utci.webp", "imageAlt": "Share of hours with no thermal stress on the ground around a building"}}, {"id": "report-skyview", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Sky exposure and sky view factor in urban design: Ladybug's View Percent method on ground and facades", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Sky view factor", "Sky exposure", "Urban canyon", "Ladybug", "Tregenza patches"], "highlights": ["Sky view is computed with Ladybug's View Percent (Sky View) method on 145, 577 or 1,297 sky patches.", "Each clear patch counts by the cosine of its angle to the surface normal (a cosine-weighted, horizontal-projected view).", "Against ladybug-core every ground point and facade square agrees within 4 × 10⁻⁶ percentage points.", "Open ground reads slightly below 100 % at low resolutions because of how patches are weighted.", "All geometry is opaque; trees and glass block the sky."], "abstract": "The sky view factor (SVF), the fraction of the radiation leaving a surface that reaches the sky, is a central geometric variable in urban climatology: it governs night-time long-wave cooling, diffuse daylight and the urban heat island [1, 2]. SimuSite's Sky exposure study computes it on ground cells and on the faces of the building by casting a ray from each sample to every patch of a subdivided sky dome and summing the clear patches with a cosine weight, following Ladybug's View Percent component in its \"Sky View\" mode [3]. The patch sets are the 145 Tregenza patches [4] and Reinhart's 577 and 1,297 subdivisions [5]. This report states the definitions, lists the parameters, and summarises a step-for-step comparison with ladybug-core at all three resolutions, in which the largest difference is 4 × 10⁻⁶ percentage points on the ground and 10⁻¹² on facades. An earlier environmental version of the study also reports equal-solid-angle \"sky exposure\" and a dated solar mask; the distinction between the measures is explained.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nTwo quantities are commonly called \"sky view\". The *sky exposure* (sometimes \"sky visibility\") is the fraction of the hemisphere's solid angle that is unobstructed, with every direction weighted equally. The *sky view factor* proper, as used in heat-transfer and urban climatology, weights each direction by the cosine of its angle to the surface normal, i.e. by its projection onto the surface; it is the fraction of diffuse radiation leaving a horizontal surface that reaches the sky [1, 2]. For a point at the bottom of a symmetric canyon of height-to-width ratio H/W, the cosine-weighted SVF is cos(arctan(2H/W)) [2]. The two definitions diverge strongly near the horizon: low obstructions remove much exposure but little view factor. Designers need to know which one a tool reports.\n\n## 2. Method\n\nFrom each sample (ground cell centres raised 0.1 m along the normal; facade and roof squares), a ray is cast towards the centre of each sky patch. The patch set depends on the chosen resolution: 145 patches (Tregenza [4]), 577 or 1,297 patches (Reinhart's subdivisions [5]), identical to the patches of Ladybug's `view_sphere` [3]. A clear patch within 90° of the surface normal contributes twice the cosine of its angle to the normal times its area weight; the sum is divided by the patch count and reported in per cent. This is exactly Ladybug's View Percent in Sky View mode [3]. Because the patches are finite and weighted by area, an unobstructed horizontal point reads slightly below 100 % at low resolution (Section 4) and an unobstructed vertical wall about 50 %.\n\nThe environmental version of the study (Climate & passive design group) also reports equal-solid-angle sky exposure, the horizontally projected sky view with cosine weighting, and a solar mask for a chosen date, using exact ray–triangle intersections through a bounding volume hierarchy with 96, 192 or 384 hemisphere rays.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs of the Sky exposure study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sky patches | 145 / 577 / 1,297 | Tregenza [4]; Reinhart [5]; as in Ladybug [3] | Sourced, verified |\n| Weighting | 2 cos θ × area weight / patch count | Ladybug View Percent, Sky View [3] | Sourced, verified |\n| Ground offset | 0.1 m | Ladybug default offset [3] | Sourced |\n| Geometry | Opaque triangles, model and surroundings | — | Modelling assumption |\n| Hemisphere rays (environmental version) | 96, 192, 384 | Engine design | Numerical setting |\n\nNo unsourced constants were found.\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/validation/lb_sunhours.py` computes LB View Percent (Sky View) with ladybug-core on the same scene and points (289 ground points, 203 surface squares) and compares point by point.\n\nTable 2. Sky view, SimuSite against ladybug-core.\n\n| Resolution | Patches | Mean, ground (SimuSite / Ladybug) | Worst ground difference | Mean, surfaces | Worst surface difference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 145 | 92.7377 / 92.7377 % | 3.8 × 10⁻⁶ pts | 49.8483 % | 1.1 × 10⁻¹² pts |\n| 2 | 577 | 95.4716 / 95.4716 % | 3.8 × 10⁻⁶ pts | 49.2222 % | 1.1 × 10⁻¹² pts |\n| 3 | 1,297 | 96.3462 / 96.3462 % | 3.8 × 10⁻⁶ pts | 49.0724 % | 1.1 × 10⁻¹² pts |\n\nThe patch sets were also checked to be identical to Ladybug's `view_sphere`. The study ran without error in the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe implementation reproduces Ladybug exactly, so its accuracy is that of the discretised method: at 145 patches an open point reads a few per cent below 100 %, and narrow gaps between buildings smaller than a patch may be missed; higher resolutions converge (Table 2). Users comparing with field measurements from fisheye photographs should note that photographic SVF is usually computed with an equal-area or cosine-weighted projection of a 180° image, and that trees, which the model treats as opaque or omits, have a large effect in the field [1]. SVF alone does not predict temperature; it is one input to the radiation balance used by the Outdoor thermal comfort and Solar radiation studies.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nSky exposure in SimuSite is a verified, exact reproduction of Ladybug's Sky View. Results should be reported with the resolution used and read as cosine-weighted sky view, not equal-angle sky exposure.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js), [emb/modeler.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/modeler.html), [mvp/js/environment/analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/analysis.js). Tests: [tests/validation/lb_sunhours.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/lb_sunhours.py), [tests/environment-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-analysis.cjs). This article's source: [skyview.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/skyview.md)."}], "references": ["T.R. Oke, Canyon geometry and the nocturnal urban heat island: comparison of scale model and field observations, J. Climatol. 1 (1981) 237–254. [doi:10.1002/joc.3370010304](https://doi.org/10.1002/joc.3370010304)", "G.T. Johnson, I.D. Watson, The determination of view-factors in urban canyons, J. Clim. Appl. Meteorol. 23 (1984) 329–335.", "M.S. Roudsari, M. Pak, Ladybug: a parametric environmental plugin for Grasshopper to help designers create an environmentally-conscious design, in: Proc. Building Simulation 2013, IBPSA, Chambéry, 2013, pp. 3128–3135.", "P.R. Tregenza, Subdivision of the sky hemisphere for luminance measurements, Light. Res. Technol. 19 (1987) 13–14.", "C.F. Reinhart, Tutorial on the use of Daysim simulations for sustainable design, Institute for Research in Construction, National Research Council Canada, Ottawa, 2006."], "category": "research", "simulation": "skyview", "summary": "The Sky exposure study computes the percentage of sky seen from ground cells and building faces with Ladybug's View Percent (Sky View) method. This report explains the two definitions in use, the geometry and constants, and the check that reproduces ladybug-core on every point.", "image": "img/home/skyview.webp", "imageAlt": "Sky view on the ground around buildings, darker where buildings hide more of the sky"}}, {"id": "report-shadebenefit", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Is this shade welcome? A degree-hour shade benefit method for the ground and facades", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Shade benefit", "Degree-hours", "Shading design", "Outdoor comfort", "Ladybug"], "highlights": ["Each sun-up hour in which the design blocks the sun scores (T − 26 °C)⁺ as benefit and (18 °C − T)⁺ as harm.", "Only shade added by the design counts: hours already shaded by the context are excluded.", "The method follows the temperature-based shade benefit idea of SHADERADE and Ladybug.", "The comfort band 18–26 °C is fixed in the Workspace; it is a design assumption, not a standard.", "Radiation intensity, wind and humidity are ignored."], "abstract": "Shade is beneficial in hot hours and costly in cold ones. Sargent, Niemasz and Reinhart's SHADERADE method evaluated each cell of a candidate shading device by the energy consequence of the sun it blocks [1], and Ladybug's thermal shade benefit component simplified this to a temperature criterion: each hour of shade counts as beneficial when air temperature is above a comfort band and harmful when it is below [2]. SimuSite's Shade benefit study applies that temperature criterion to the ground and building faces: for every sun-up hour of an EPW year, a sample is scored if the design (but not the existing context) blocks the beam, by the degrees above 26 °C as benefit and the degrees below 18 °C as harm. The net degree-hours map shows where the design's shade helps and where its sun is missed. This report sets out the method and its parameters and flags that the 18–26 °C band is a design assumption rather than a cited standard, and that the method does not account for radiation intensity, wind or humidity.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nShading devices and the shade of buildings themselves are designed by intuition more often than by analysis. A simple, defensible screen asks, hour by hour, whether a place would rather be in sun or shade. Olgyay's bioclimatic approach first framed shading periods by the air temperature at which people prefer shade [3]. SHADERADE quantified the benefit of each element of a shading device with energy simulation [1], and Ladybug offers both an energy-based and a temperature-based shade benefit [2]. SimuSite implements the temperature-based version for outdoor space and envelopes.\n\n## 2. Method\n\nFor each hour of the EPW with direct normal irradiance above zero and the sun above the horizon, the sun position is computed (the same NOAA/Ladybug sun path as Sun hours). Hours are grouped by sun direction into 15° azimuth by 10° altitude bins, and each bin keeps two sums: Σ max(0, T − T_upper) and Σ max(0, T_lower − T), where T is the hour's dry-bulb temperature. For each ground sample, a ray is cast towards each bin's direction twice, once through the existing context alone and once through the design; if the context already blocks the sun, the hour is not counted; if only the design blocks it, the bin's benefit sum is added as help and its harm sum as harm. The map shows help − harm in degree-hours. On facade and roof squares, the study reports the sun received in hot hours minus that in cold hours, in the same units, to show where shade on that face would help.\n\n**UTCI criterion (added 23 November 2026).** Air temperature alone does not say whether a person in the sun wants shade. The Workspace study can now score hours by the Universal Thermal Climate Index instead. For each hour with the sun up and direct normal irradiance above zero, UTCI is computed for a person standing in open sun: sky view factor 1, the station's 10 m wind (the height at which UTCI takes its wind input [8]), and the mean radiant temperature of the Outdoor thermal comfort study, whose short-wave part is the SolarCal model of ASHRAE Standard 55 Appendix C [6, 7]. The scoring band becomes the \"no thermal stress\" category of the UTCI assessment scale, 9 to 26 °C [5, 8]. An hour of shade counts for each degree of UTCI at or above 26 °C (moderate heat stress and above) and against for each degree below 9 °C (slight cold stress and below). The sun-direction bins and the comparison with and without the design are unchanged.\n\nIn the environmental version of the study, the candidate shade is any set of model objects the user selects; the same scoring compares the model with and without them on a sample grid of up to 512 points.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs of the Shade benefit study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Weather | Project EPW, hourly dry-bulb and DNI | EPW data dictionary [4] | Measured / typical-year data |\n| Lower and upper temperature | 18 °C and 26 °C (fixed in the Workspace; editable in the environmental version) | Engine default | Assumption, flagged: no standard is cited; 26 °C coincides with the upper bound of \"no thermal stress\" on the UTCI scale [5] but is applied here to air temperature |\n| UTCI criterion (Workspace option) | Shade wanted at UTCI ≥ 26 °C, unwanted below 9 °C, for a person in open sun | UTCI assessment scale [5, 8]; UTCI and its mean radiant temperature as in the Outdoor thermal comfort report [6, 7, 8] | Sourced band; radiant surfaces at air temperature, so heat stress near hot paving is underestimated (added 23 November 2026) |\n| Sun direction bins | 15° × 10° | Engine design | Numerical setting |\n| Scoring | Degree-hours above / below the band | Ladybug thermal shade benefit [2]; after SHADERADE [1] | Sourced method |\n| Sun position | NOAA / Ladybug sun path | See the Sun hours report | Verified |\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/environment-analysis.cjs`, re-run for this audit (all checks pass), verifies the environmental version's degree-hour scoring on a roof over a sample in a constant 30 °C month (benefit 31 days × 6 h × 4 K) and that hours already shaded by the context are not counted twice. The Workspace version uses the same scoring with sun-direction bins. There is no external reference implementation that uses exactly these bins and band, so the study is classed as a screening estimate. It ran without error in the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 with a Chicago TMY3 file.\n\nThe UTCI criterion reuses the UTCI polynomial and mean radiant temperature that the Outdoor thermal comfort report verifies against pythermalcomfort. `tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs` runs both criteria in the Workspace on the hospital example with its Toronto weather and checks that the method text and settings state the criterion. With air temperature the mean net benefit on the ground was −1 K·h; with UTCI in the sun it was −785 K·h. In Toronto a person standing in the sun is below 9 °C UTCI in many sunny hours, so shading the ground costs more warmth than it saves heat once radiation and wind are counted.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe method answers a narrow question well: given the climate, in which hours is shade wanted, and does this design provide it there? Its simplicity is also its limit. Air temperature alone does not determine whether shade is wanted: at 24 °C with strong sun and still air a person may prefer shade, while at 28 °C with wind and low sun they may not. A radiation-aware criterion is more defensible for outdoor space; the Workspace now offers one, based on UTCI in the sun (Section 2). For buildings, the energy consequence of shading depends on glazing, internal gains and HVAC, which the SHADERADE energy-based method captures and this one does not [1]. The fixed band should be visible in the interface and editable; the report recommends exposing it in the Workspace as it is in the environmental version.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nShade benefit is a transparent temperature-weighted screening of where a design's shade helps or hurts. Its band of 18–26 °C is an assumption; results should be compared between options with the same band and not read as energy or comfort predictions.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js), [mvp/js/environment/analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/analysis.js). Tests: [tests/environment-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-analysis.cjs), [tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs). This article's source: [shadebenefit.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/shadebenefit.md)."}], "references": ["J.A. Sargent, J. Niemasz, C.F. Reinhart, SHADERADE: combining Rhinoceros and EnergyPlus for the design of static exterior shading devices, in: Proc. Building Simulation 2011, IBPSA, Sydney, 2011, pp. 310–317.", "M.S. Roudsari, M. Pak, Ladybug: a parametric environmental plugin for Grasshopper to help designers create an environmentally-conscious design, in: Proc. Building Simulation 2013, IBPSA, Chambéry, 2013, pp. 3128–3135.", "V. Olgyay, Design with Climate: Bioclimatic Approach to Architectural Regionalism, Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, 1963.", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "K. Błażejczyk, G. Jendritzky, P. Bröde, D. Fiala, G. Havenith, Y. Epstein, A. Psikuta, B. Kampmann, An introduction to the Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI), Geogr. Pol. 86 (2013) 5–10. [doi:10.7163/GPol.2013.1](https://doi.org/10.7163/GPol.2013.1)", "ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 55-2020, Thermal Environmental Conditions for Human Occupancy, Appendix C (SolarCal), ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2020.", "E. Arens, T. Hoyt, X. Zhou, L. Huang, H. Zhang, S. Schiavon, Modeling the comfort effects of short-wave solar radiation indoors, Build. Environ. 88 (2015) 3–9. [doi:10.1016/j.buildenv.2014.09.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.buildenv.2014.09.004)", "P. Bröde, D. Fiala, K. Błażejczyk, I. Holmér, G. Jendritzky, B. Kampmann, B. Tinz, G. Havenith, Deriving the operational procedure for the Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI), Int. J. Biometeorol. 56 (2012) 481–494. [doi:10.1007/s00484-011-0454-1](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-011-0454-1)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "shadebenefit", "summary": "The Shade benefit study weighs each hour of shade a design casts by how hot or cold the air is, giving net degree-hours of benefit. This report traces the method to SHADERADE and Ladybug, states its fixed comfort band, and flags that it is a weather-based screening, not a load or comfort result.", "image": "img/home/shadebenefit.webp", "imageAlt": "Shade benefit on the ground, blue where the design's shade helps in hot hours and red where it takes sun in cold hours"}}, {"id": "report-solarenvelope", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "The solar envelope as a design constraint: computing the tallest building that does not overshadow its neighbours", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Solar envelope", "Solar rights", "Zoning", "Overshadowing", "Knowles"], "highlights": ["For each cell the envelope height is the lowest height at which a vertical line would start to shade a protected neighbour.", "Protected periods: equinoxes 9:00–15:00, winter solstice 10:00–14:00 or summer solstice 8:00–17:00, every 30 minutes.", "The method follows Knowles's solar envelope; heights are capped at 100 m.", "Protected hours are apparent solar time: local mean time at the site's meridian corrected by the equation of time (added after the audit).", "The envelope is a geometric constraint, not a planning approval."], "abstract": "The solar envelope, introduced by Ralph Knowles, is the largest volume that can be built on a site without casting shadow on neighbouring properties during specified hours [1, 2]. It turns the qualitative goal of solar access into a three-dimensional zoning constraint. SimuSite's Solar envelope study computes, on a grid over the site, the greatest height at each cell such that a building there would not shade any surrounding building for a set of sun positions in a protected period. This report describes the computation, the three preset protected periods, and the parameters, and records one approximation found in the audit and corrected on 21 November 2026: protected hours were taken at local mean solar time without the equation of time, which shifted the sun positions by about 7 minutes at the equinoxes. The envelope is a geometric constraint for comparison and massing studies and does not represent any jurisdiction's solar-access rules.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nKnowles and his students at the University of Southern California developed the solar envelope in the 1970s as a way to guarantee solar access in dense development while allowing as much building as possible [1]. The envelope is bounded by \"shadow fences\" on neighbouring properties and by a period of protected solar access; within it, any building leaves the neighbours in sun for that period [2]. The concept has informed zoning studies and is implemented in several environmental design tools.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Sun positions\n\nThe study uses one of three protected periods: both equinoxes (20 March and 22 September) from 9:00 to 15:00; the winter solstice from 10:00 to 14:00; or the summer solstice from 8:00 to 17:00. Sun positions are taken every 30 minutes, keeping those above 1° altitude, with the same NOAA sun position as the other solar studies. Times are apparent solar time: the sun position routine is given local mean time at the site's own meridian (time zone = longitude/15), less the equation of time for the date, so that 12:00 is solar noon, when the sun crosses the meridian.\n\n### 2.2. Envelope height\n\nThe surrounding buildings of the site (the context) are the receptors. For each grid cell and each sun direction, the study finds the height at which a vertical line at that cell would begin to intersect a ray towards the sun that reaches a receptor surface. The envelope height at the cell is the minimum over all sun directions, capped at 100 m. The environmental version of the study offers two modes: \"solar rights\", protecting a user-defined receptor rectangle, and \"solar collection\", finding the heights a receptor must reach to stay in sun; both use exact ray–triangle intersections on the original triangles.\n\n### 2.3. Results\n\nThe study reports the mean and range of envelope heights, the share of ground where the envelope is below 10 m, and, if the design is present, the share of its facade and roof squares that stand above the envelope and therefore shade a neighbour during the protected period.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs of the Solar envelope study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Concept | Solar envelope / shadow fences | Knowles [1, 2] | Sourced |\n| Protected periods | Equinoxes 9–15 h; winter solstice 10–14 h; summer solstice 8–17 h | Common practice in solar-access studies (e.g. Knowles's protected hours) [1, 2] | Sourced in principle; no jurisdiction's rule is implied |\n| Time step | 30 min | Engine design | Numerical setting |\n| Solar time | Apparent solar time (longitude/15 h offset less the equation of time) | Equation of time from the NOAA sun position routine | Corrected 21 November 2026; before, the equation of time was omitted |\n| Height cap | 100 m | Engine design | Numerical setting |\n| Receptors | Surrounding buildings from site data | OpenStreetMap or user geometry | Input data |\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/environment-analysis.cjs`, re-run for this audit (all checks pass), verifies the environmental version's solar-rights height against an independent geometric answer (a sun at 45° over a 1 m receptor gives a height of 1 m two metres away, the cap behind the receptor and zero over it), and the collection envelope against exact triangle cases including overhead and behind-receiver obstacles. In the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026, the audit site had no neighbouring buildings and the Workspace study stopped with a request to add context buildings, which is the intended behaviour, since the envelope protects neighbours' receptors. The Workspace version computes the same quantity in the modeler with its own ray code and has no separate reference test. A unit test added with the solar-time correction, `tests/sun-solar-time.test.mjs`, checks that at 12:00 apparent solar time the sun is due south on five dates through the year, within 0.05°, and that the equation of time stays between about −14.6 and +16.5 minutes.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe envelope is only as meaningful as its receptors and period. Real solar-access regulations protect specific surfaces (windows, gardens, solar collectors) and periods that vary by jurisdiction; the presets here are generic. At the time of the audit the equation of time was omitted. It ranges from about −14 to +16 minutes over the year; on the preset dates it is about −7 minutes on 20 March, +7 minutes on 22 September and 2 minutes or less at the solstices, so the protected window was off centre around solar noon by that much, with the ends (where the sun is lowest and the envelope most restrictive) affected most. The study now uses the equation of time already computed in the sun-position routine. Like Sun hours, the method treats all geometry as opaque.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Solar envelope study implements Knowles's concept directly and is useful for comparing massing options against neighbours' solar access. Its protected periods are generic and should be replaced by the rule of the jurisdiction before results are used to argue compliance.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js), [mvp/js/environment/analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/analysis.js). Tests: [tests/environment-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-analysis.cjs), [tests/sun-solar-time.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/sun-solar-time.test.mjs). This article's source: [solarenvelope.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/solarenvelope.md)."}], "references": ["R.L. Knowles, Sun Rhythm Form, MIT Press, Cambridge, MA, 1981.", "R.L. Knowles, The solar envelope: its meaning for energy and buildings, Energy Build. 35 (2003) 15–25. [doi:10.1016/S0378-7788(02)00076-2](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0378-7788(02)00076-2)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "solarenvelope", "summary": "The Solar envelope study computes, at each cell, the greatest height a building could reach without shading the surrounding buildings during protected hours. This report relates the method to Knowles's solar envelope, gives its protected periods and parameters, and flags an approximation in how solar time is set.", "image": "img/home/solarenvelope.webp", "imageAlt": "Solar envelope heights over a site, lower near neighbours to the north and higher away from them"}}, {"id": "report-climate", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Reading the weather file before designing: monthly climate summaries, degree days and wind roses from EPW data", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Climate analysis", "EPW", "Degree days", "Wind rose", "Typical meteorological year"], "highlights": ["All figures come directly from the hourly records of the project's EPW file; no values are synthesised.", "Degree days use daily mean temperature over complete days only, with bases 18 °C (heating) and 24 °C (cooling) by default.", "Missing humidity, radiation and wind records are excluded and counted, never filled with zeros.", "Wind roses use 16 sectors of 22.5° and treat speeds below 0.5 m/s as calm.", "The analysis describes outdoor weather; it is not an indoor comfort or energy prediction."], "abstract": "Before analysing a design, architects need to understand the climate it will face. SimuSite's Climate analysis study reads the project's hourly weather file in EnergyPlus (EPW) format [1] and reports monthly air temperature (mean, minimum, maximum), relative humidity, global horizontal irradiation, wind speed, a 16-sector wind rose, and heating and cooling degree days. Weather files are usually typical meteorological years (TMY) assembled from multi-year records [2, 3]; SimuSite obtains them for the project's location from the climate.onebuilding.org repository or accepts the user's own file. This report documents the data source, definitions, defaults and missing-data rules, and the verification of calendar handling and units. Degree days are computed from daily mean temperatures over complete days with base temperatures of 18 °C (heating) and 24 °C (cooling) by default; the heating base follows common European and Canadian practice, while the cooling base is a design default that differs from the 18 °C (65 °F) used in some national statistics and is flagged as such.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nClimate-responsive design begins with the weather: when it is cold, hot, humid or windy, and from which direction the wind and sun come [4]. Typical meteorological year files condense long records into one representative year by selecting, month by month, the observed month whose statistics are closest to the long-term distribution [2]. They are the standard input to building simulation and are distributed in the EPW format [1]. A climate summary is the architect's first look at that file and a check that it is the right one.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Data source and parsing\n\nThe project's EPW is downloaded automatically for the nearest station from climate.onebuilding.org, a repository of TMYx and other typical-year files derived from ISD and other national records [3], through SimuSite's weather service, or uploaded by the user. The station name, coordinates, elevation and time zone are read from the LOCATION header, and each of the 8,760 (or 8,784) hourly rows provides dry-bulb temperature, relative humidity, atmospheric pressure, global horizontal irradiance, wind direction and speed, in the field order of the EnergyPlus data dictionary [1]. EPW missing-value codes (for instance 999.9 for temperature or 9999 for radiation) are treated as missing.\n\n### 2.2. Summaries\n\nFor a selected period of months and hours, the study computes per month the number of hours, the mean, minimum and maximum temperature, mean relative humidity, global horizontal irradiation in kWh/m², and mean wind speed. Missing records are excluded from each statistic and their counts reported. The wind rose bins hours with valid speed and direction into 16 sectors of 22.5° centred on the cardinal and intercardinal directions; hours below 0.5 m/s are counted as calm.\n\n### 2.3. Degree days\n\nHeating and cooling degree days are computed from complete days only: for each day with 24 valid hourly temperatures, the daily mean T̄ is formed and HDD += max(0, T_b,h − T̄), CDD += max(0, T̄ − T_b,c). Days with missing hours are not counted, and months with no complete day show no value. The bases default to 18 °C for heating and 24 °C for cooling and can be changed from −20 to 35 °C and −20 to 40 °C.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and definitions of the Climate analysis study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Weather file | EPW, typical year (TMYx) or user file | climate.onebuilding.org [3]; EPW format [1] | Measured data, aggregated into a typical year |\n| Missing values | Excluded and counted | EPW missing codes [1] | Sourced |\n| Degree days | Daily mean over complete days | Standard definition (e.g. CIBSE TM41 [5]) | Sourced |\n| Heating base | 18 °C (editable) | Common European practice; Eurostat uses 18 °C for heating degree days [6] | Sourced default |\n| Cooling base | 24 °C (editable) | Engine default | Flagged: other conventions use 18 °C/65 °F or 21–22 °C; state the base with any result |\n| Wind rose sectors | 16 × 22.5°, calm < 0.5 m/s | Common meteorological practice | Sourced default |\n| Reference pressure | From the file, else from elevation by the standard atmosphere | ASHRAE Fundamentals psychrometrics [7] | Sourced |\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/environment-analysis.cjs`, re-run for this audit, passes its checks of complete EPW chronology in leap and non-leap years, daily-mean degree days, irradiation units, and the exclusion of missing data, including blocking of invalid temperatures. In the end-to-end audit with the Chicago O'Hare TMY3 file [2] the study produced its monthly tables and wind rose without error.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026, starting this study from the Workspace returned \"This study does not run here yet.\"; it runs on its own environment page, which the study card opens, and the checks above were made there and in the test script. A Workspace user who does not follow the link sees no result, which should be made clearer.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nA typical year represents typical conditions and deliberately excludes extremes; it is not suitable for assessing peak heat or design-day cold, and it reflects the climate of its source period, not the future [2, 3]. Users should check that the station is representative of the site (coastal, urban and elevation differences can be large). The cooling base of 24 °C is a reasonable choice for buildings with significant internal gains, but cooling degree days are highly sensitive to the base, so comparisons between sources must use the same one.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Climate analysis study is a faithful, missing-data-aware summary of the weather file. Its only non-standard default is the 24 °C cooling base, which should be stated whenever degree days are reported.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/environment/analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/analysis.js), [mvp/js/environment/plots.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/plots.js), [mvp/js/project-climate.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/project-climate.js). Tests: [tests/environment-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-analysis.cjs), [tests/environment-ui.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-ui.cjs). This article's source: [climate.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/climate.md)."}], "references": ["U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "S. Wilcox, W. Marion, Users Manual for TMY3 Data Sets, NREL/TP-581-43156, National Renewable Energy Laboratory, Golden, CO, 2008.", "Climate.OneBuilding.Org, Repository of free climate data for building performance simulation, [climate.onebuilding.org](https://climate.onebuilding.org/).", "V. Olgyay, Design with Climate: Bioclimatic Approach to Architectural Regionalism, Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, 1963.", "CIBSE TM41, Degree-days: theory and application, Chartered Institution of Building Services Engineers, London, 2006.", "Eurostat, Heating and cooling degree days – statistics, Statistics Explained, European Commission, Luxembourg.", "ASHRAE, ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, chapter 1, Psychrometrics, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2021."], "category": "research", "simulation": "climate", "summary": "The Climate analysis study summarises the project's EPW weather file: monthly temperatures, humidity, radiation, wind roses, and heating and cooling degree days from complete days. This report documents the data source, the definitions and base temperatures, the missing-data rules and their verification.", "image": "img/home/climate.webp", "imageAlt": "Monthly temperature range and wind rose of a project's weather file"}}, {"id": "report-passive", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "A psychrometric screen for passive design opportunities: what the weather allows, and what the thresholds rest on", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Psychrometric chart", "Bioclimatic chart", "Passive design", "Givoni", "Weather screening"], "highlights": ["Each hour is placed by dry-bulb temperature and humidity ratio, computed with the Hyland–Wexler saturation equations.", "The screening band (18–26 °C, RH 30–70 %) and the strategy thresholds are editable design assumptions, not a comfort standard.", "Categories overlap and count weather hours only; they are not predictions of indoor comfort or energy savings.", "The approach follows the bioclimatic charts of Olgyay and Givoni, simplified to transparent rules.", "Psychrometric conversions are checked against an independent reference."], "abstract": "Bioclimatic charts relate the outdoor climate to the design strategies that could make a building comfortable without mechanical systems [1, 2, 3]. SimuSite's Passive design strategies study places every hour of the project's EPW year on a psychrometric chart, by dry-bulb temperature and humidity ratio, and counts the hours that fall in a user-reviewed screening band and in seven overlapping opportunity categories: cold hours, hot hours, cold and sunny (investigate solar heating), hot and sunny (investigate shading), hot and dry (investigate evaporative cooling), and mild nights (investigate night ventilation). This report documents the psychrometric calculations, which follow ASHRAE and Hyland–Wexler [4, 5] and are verified, and the thresholds, which are transparent design assumptions inspired by the Givoni and Milne–Givoni charts [2, 3] rather than values taken from them. The study describes what the weather permits; it does not predict indoor comfort.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nOlgyay's bioclimatic chart [1] and Givoni's building bioclimatic chart [2], later developed by Milne and Givoni [3] and implemented in tools such as Climate Consultant, map zones of the psychrometric chart to passive strategies: internal gains and passive solar heating in cool conditions, shading and natural ventilation in warm ones, evaporative cooling in hot-dry ones, and thermal mass with night ventilation where diurnal swings are large. Counting the hours of a weather year in each zone gives a quick sense of which strategies are worth pursuing. The boundaries of those zones in the literature depend on building type, construction and assumptions about occupants, and differ between authors.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Psychrometrics\n\nFor each hour with a valid relative humidity, the saturation vapour pressure is computed with the Hyland–Wexler equations [5] as given in ASHRAE Fundamentals [4], over ice below the triple point and over water above it. The partial pressure of water vapour is p_v = RH · p_ws, and the humidity ratio W = 0.621945 p_v / (p − p_v), with p the hourly station pressure from the EPW or, if missing, the standard-atmosphere pressure at the station elevation [4]. Hours without humidity are excluded and counted.\n\n### 2.2. Categories\n\nWith a lower and upper temperature (default 18 and 26 °C), the study counts hours:\n\n- in the screening band: lower ≤ T ≤ upper and 30 % ≤ RH ≤ 70 %;\n- cold: T < lower; hot: T > upper;\n- cold and global horizontal irradiance above 150 W/m²: investigate solar access;\n- hot and GHI above 200 W/m²: investigate shading;\n- hot and RH below 40 %: investigate evaporative cooling;\n- between 20:00 and 06:00 with 12 °C ≤ T ≤ 20 °C: investigate night cooling.\n\nCategories overlap; shares are given relative to the hours eligible for each (those with the data it needs).\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Thresholds and constants of the Passive design strategies study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Saturation pressure | Hyland–Wexler over ice / water | Hyland & Wexler [5]; ASHRAE [4] | Sourced, verified |\n| Humidity ratio | 0.621945 p_v / (p − p_v) | ASHRAE Fundamentals [4] | Sourced |\n| Standard-atmosphere pressure | 101,325 (1 − 2.25577 × 10⁻⁵ z)^5.2559 Pa | ASHRAE Fundamentals [4] | Sourced |\n| Screening band | 18–26 °C, RH 30–70 % (editable) | Engine default, of the order of the comfort zones in [2, 3] | Assumption, flagged: not ASHRAE 55 or an adaptive model |\n| Solar heating | Cold and GHI > 150 W/m² | Engine default | Assumption, flagged |\n| Shading | Hot and GHI > 200 W/m² | Engine default | Assumption, flagged |\n| Evaporative cooling | Hot and RH < 40 % | Engine default; direction consistent with [2, 3] | Assumption, flagged |\n| Night cooling | 20:00–06:00, 12–20 °C | Engine default | Assumption, flagged |\n\nThe audit found the physics sourced and correct, and the strategy thresholds to be the engine's own choices. They are reasonable and stated on screen, but they are not taken from Givoni or Milne–Givoni, whose zones are drawn on the chart with different boundaries (and depend on humidity ratio as well as temperature). The interface already states that the band \"is not a PMV/adaptive comfort zone\".\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n`tests/environment-analysis.cjs`, re-run for this audit, passes its checks of the psychrometric functions against an independent reference, of the category counting, and of blocking when humidity is missing, without any indoor comfort claim. The study ran end to end with the Chicago O'Hare TMY3 file in the audit.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026, starting this study from the Workspace returned \"This study does not run here yet.\"; it runs on its own environment page, which the study card opens, and the checks above were made there and in the test script. A Workspace user who does not follow the link sees no result, which should be made clearer.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe screen is honest about what it counts: hours of outdoor weather meeting simple conditions. Whether a strategy works depends on the building: a heavy, well-insulated house exploits night cooling far better than a lightweight office, and passive solar heating needs south glazing and thermal mass. Readers should therefore use the categories to decide what to investigate in more detailed studies (Daylight, Natural ventilation, Solar radiation), not as evidence that a strategy will deliver comfort. A future version could implement the published Givoni–Milne zones explicitly, with their source, alongside the current transparent rules.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Passive design strategies study computes psychrometric quantities correctly and counts weather hours by transparent but assumed thresholds. Those thresholds should be cited as SimuSite's screening rules, not as a published bioclimatic chart.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/environment/analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/analysis.js), [mvp/js/environment/plots.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/environment/plots.js). Tests: [tests/environment-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/environment-analysis.cjs). This article's source: [passive.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/passive.md)."}], "references": ["V. Olgyay, Design with Climate: Bioclimatic Approach to Architectural Regionalism, Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, 1963.", "B. Givoni, Comfort, climate analysis and building design guidelines, Energy Build. 18 (1992) 11–23. [doi:10.1016/0378-7788(92)90047-K](https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-7788(92)90047-K)", "M. Milne, B. Givoni, Architectural design based on climate, in: D. Watson (Ed.), Energy Conservation Through Building Design, McGraw-Hill, New York, 1979, pp. 96–113.", "ASHRAE, ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, chapter 1, Psychrometrics, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2021.", "R.W. Hyland, A. Wexler, Formulations for the thermodynamic properties of the saturated phases of H2O from 173.15 K to 473.15 K, ASHRAE Trans. 89 (2A) (1983) 500–519."], "category": "research", "simulation": "passive", "summary": "The Passive design strategies study plots each hour of the weather file on a psychrometric chart and counts hours in which solar heating, shading, evaporative cooling or night cooling may be worth investigating. This report sets out the psychrometrics, traces the idea to Olgyay and Givoni, and flags the specific thresholds as design assumptions.", "image": "img/home/passive.webp", "imageAlt": "Hourly weather points on a psychrometric chart with the screening band marked"}}, {"id": "report-daylight", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Climate-based daylight screening in the browser: sDA, ASE and daylight factor from a 145-patch sky, checked against Radiance", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Daylight", "Spatial daylight autonomy", "Annual sunlight exposure", "Daylight factor", "Radiance", "Split-flux method"], "highlights": ["Sky light is computed from 145 Tregenza patches with CIE clear and overcast luminance distributions blended by Perez sky clearness.", "Inter-reflected light uses the BRE split-flux formula, so it is uniform within a room.", "After adding angle-dependent glass transmission, the daylight factor agrees with Radiance within max(0.5 points, 25 %) at every test point.", "sDA (300 lux, 50 % of occupied hours) and ASE (1,000 lux, 250 h) follow IES LM-83; since 23 November 2026 blinds close as LM-83-12 describes, and sDA is reported with and without them.", "Without a weather file the study falls back to a synthetic year with random cloud, which should be labelled as such."], "abstract": "Daylight metrics have moved from the static daylight factor to climate-based metrics computed over a year of weather [1, 2]. SimuSite's Daylight study computes, on a grid over the work plane of each level, spatial daylight autonomy (sDA300/50 %), annual sunlight exposure (ASE1000/250 h) as defined in IES LM-83 [3], the daylight factor under a CIE overcast sky [4], and the LEED v4.1 option 2 illuminance check [5]. Sky light is computed by tracing horizontal bearings through the plan to find the visible sky in each of 145 Tregenza patches [6], weighting each patch by a luminance distribution that blends the CIE clear sky [4] and the Moon–Spencer overcast sky [7] according to the hour's Perez sky clearness [8], and passing it through clear glass with Fresnel angular transmission. Inter-reflected light is added by the BRE split-flux formula [9, 10]. This report documents each step, the provenance of all parameters, and the validation of the daylight factor against Radiance [11]: after the glass model was corrected, the engine agrees within max(0.5 percentage points, 25 %) at every point of two test rooms, with a remaining positive bias at the back of rooms caused by the uniform split-flux term. Two issues are flagged: blinds are not modelled, which makes sDA and ASE optimistic relative to LM-83's procedure, and when no weather file is available the study uses a synthetic year with random cloud cover.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nThe daylight factor (DF), the ratio of indoor to outdoor horizontal illuminance under an overcast sky, has been used for decades because it is independent of orientation and climate [1]. Its independence is also its weakness: it says nothing about sunlight, glare or the actual amount of daylight over a year. Climate-based daylight modelling (CBDM) addresses this by computing illuminance for every daylit hour of a weather year [1, 2]. IES LM-83 standardised two such metrics: sDA300/50 %, the share of floor area that receives at least 300 lux for at least half of the occupied hours, and ASE1000/250, the share receiving more than 1,000 lux of direct sunlight for more than 250 occupied hours [3]. LEED v4.1 credits daylight with these metrics or with a simpler point-in-time illuminance check [5]. Reference CBDM tools such as Radiance with the daylight-coefficient or three-phase methods [11] are too slow for interactive browser use; SimuSite therefore uses a simplified but physically structured method and validates it where possible.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Geometry\n\nThe level's walls, windows (sill and head heights), furniture and the outdoors are rasterised. From each sensor point on the work plane (0.8 m by default), horizontal rays are cast on equally spaced compass bearings. Walls 2 m or taller stop a ray; glazing lets it pass between sill and head; past the outermost pane, buildings outside cut the bottom of the visible sky. For each bearing, the range of altitudes at which sky is visible is kept, and daylight coefficients (horizontal illuminance per unit luminance of each patch, ∫∫ sin a cos a da dφ over the visible part) are summed per patch.\n\n### 2.2. Sky and sun\n\nThe sky is divided into Tregenza's 145 patches [6]. For each hour, patch luminances are set so that the sky produces the hour's diffuse horizontal illuminance, with a relative distribution that blends the CIE clear sky (gradation and indicatrix as in the CIE standard general sky [4]) and the Moon–Spencer overcast distribution L(θ) ∝ (1 + 2 sin θ)/3 [7], weighted by the Perez sky clearness ε of the hour [8]. Direct sun is added at its own angle of incidence on the glass if the sun's bearing and altitude fall in the visible range. With an EPW file, direct normal and diffuse horizontal illuminance come from the file (or from irradiance times luminous efficacy where illuminance is missing) [12]. The daylight factor uses the CIE overcast sky with no sun.\n\n### 2.3. Glass\n\nClear glass transmission depends on the angle of incidence. The engine models a single pane of refractive index 1.52 with Fresnel reflection at both faces, multiple internal reflections and absorption along the oblique path, as Radiance's `glass` material does [11], scaled so that transmission at normal incidence equals the user's value (0.6 by default).\n\n### 2.4. Inter-reflections\n\nLight reflected inside the room is estimated with the BRE split-flux formula [9, 10]: IRC = τ W / (A (1 − R)) × (C R_fw + 5 R_cw), with W the glazed area, A the total internal surface area, R its area-weighted mean reflectance, R_fw and R_cw the mean reflectances of the lower and upper parts of the room, and C a coefficient that falls with the angle of external obstruction (39 at 0°, 35 at 10°, 31 at 20°, 25 at 30°, 20 at 40°, 14 at 50°, 10 at 60°, 7 at 70°, 5 at 80°) [9, 10]. The internally reflected component is added uniformly to every point of the room.\n\n### 2.5. Metrics\n\nOver occupied hours (8:00–18:00 by default, as in LM-83 [3]) the study counts, per point, hours at or above 300 lux (sDA) and hours with direct sun at or above 1,000 lux (ASE), and reports the share of regularly occupied floor meeting sDA300/50 % and exceeding ASE1000/250. LEED option 2 checks 300–3,000 lux at 9:00 and 15:00 on the equinox [5].\n\n**Blinds (added 23 November 2026).** LM-83-12 computes sDA with operable blinds: in any analysis hour in which more than 2 % of the analysis points receive direct sunlight of 1,000 lux or more, the blinds close [3, 15]. When BSDF data are not available, LM-83-12 section 2.2.7 models closed white louvre blinds (reflectance above 80 %) as a 20 % diffuse transmittance for both sunlight and skylight, and a shade of unknown transmittance as 5 % diffuse with no specular transmission [3, 16]. The study implements the trigger exactly: for every occupied hour it counts the points whose direct-sun illuminance (the same quantity ASE uses) reaches 1,000 lux, and closes the blinds of the level in that hour if the count exceeds 2 % of the points. With the blinds closed, the illuminance at a point is taken as the blind transmittance times the hour's illuminance without the sun patch. This omits the sunlight the blind diffuses into the room, so the reported value is a lower bound; the sDA without blinds, which the study also reports, is an upper bound, and an LM-83 sDA lies between them. ASE is counted with the blinds open, because it measures the direct sun that triggers them. The blinds are operated as one group per level; LM-83 users often group them by facade or zone [15], which would shut fewer hours.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Daylight study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sky subdivision | 145 patches | Tregenza [6] | Sourced |\n| Clear-sky luminance distribution | CIE clear sky gradation and indicatrix | CIE S 011 [4] | Sourced |\n| Overcast distribution | (1 + 2 sin θ)/3 | Moon & Spencer [7]; CIE overcast [4] | Sourced |\n| Clear/overcast blend | By Perez clearness ε, weight (ε − 1)/5.2 clamped to 0–1 | Perez ε [8]; the linear weighting is the engine's | Flagged: a pragmatic blend, not a published sky model (Perez all-weather would be the reference) |\n| Synthetic clear-sky illuminance (no EPW) | E_b = 127,500 exp(−0.21 m); E_d = 800 + 15,500 √sin a; overcast 300 + 21,000 sin a | IES Lighting Handbook daylight availability formulae [13]; air mass after Kasten & Young [14] | Sourced |\n| Synthetic weather year (no EPW) | Seeded random cloud cover | Engine design | Flagged: not weather data; results must be labelled \"no weather file\" |\n| Glass | n = 1.52, Fresnel, τ_n = 0.6 default | Radiance `glass` [11] | Sourced, validated |\n| Reflectances | Floor 0.2, walls 0.5, ceiling 0.8 | Typical design values for light interiors | Assumption, editable; real finishes should be entered |\n| Split-flux coefficient C | 39 … 5 by obstruction angle | Hopkinson, Longmore & Petherbridge; BRE [9, 10] | Sourced |\n| Work plane | 0.8 m | Common practice for desk height | Sourced default |\n| Occupied hours | 8:00–18:00 | IES LM-83 [3] | Sourced |\n| sDA / ASE thresholds | 300 lux, 50 %; 1,000 lux, 250 h | IES LM-83 [3]; LEED v4.1 [5] | Sourced |\n| Blinds | Close when > 2 % of points get ≥ 1,000 lux direct sun; closed: 20 % (white louvres, default), 5 % (unknown shade) or none | IES LM-83-12, trigger and section 2.2.7 [3, 15, 16] | Sourced trigger and transmittances; closed-blind light omits diffused sunlight, so sDA with blinds is a lower bound (added 23 November 2026) |\n\n## 4. Verification\n\nThe daylight factor was validated against Radiance 5 (gensky −c CIE overcast sky, oconv, rtrace with up to 200,000 ambient samples) through pyradiance, and against an independent numerical integral of the overcast sky through the opening (`tests/validation/daylight_df.py`). Test room 5 × 6 × 2.7 m, window 3 m wide from 0.9 to 2.1 m, glass 0.6, reflectances floor 0.2, walls 0.5, ceiling 0.8, ground 0.15; sensors on the centre line 0.5–5.5 m from the window; one open case and one with a building 8 m high 10 m away. Tolerances fixed in advance: sky component within max(0.2 points, 10 %), DF within max(0.5 points, 25 %).\n\nTable 2. Daylight factor, engine against Radiance, open view (from `mvp/VALIDATION.md`).\n\n| Depth | Sky through glass, engine / Radiance | DF engine / Radiance | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.5 m | 14.73 / 14.82 | 15.36 / 15.31 | +0.05 pts |\n| 1.5 m | 4.46 / 4.46 | 5.08 / 5.05 | +0.03 pts |\n| 2.5 m | 1.42 / 1.41 | 2.05 / 1.98 | +0.07 pts |\n| 3.5 m | 0.57 / 0.56 | 1.20 / 1.07 | +0.13 pts |\n| 4.5 m | 0.27 / 0.27 | 0.90 / 0.66 | +0.25 pts |\n| 5.5 m | 0.15 / 0.15 | 0.78 / 0.53 | +0.24 pts |\n\nThe two references agree with each other within 0.04 points. Before the angle-dependent glass was introduced, the engine over-predicted DF near the window by 20 % (open) and 26 % (obstructed), outside tolerance; it now passes at every point. With the obstruction, the sky component vanishes beyond 2.5 m in both engine and Radiance, and the remaining error is the inter-reflected component, which the engine spreads evenly (0.47–0.74 points) while Radiance falls to 0.18 points at the back. The climate-based metrics (sDA, ASE) have not yet been compared with Radiance's annual methods. In the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 with a Chicago TMY3 file, the study completed on a two-storey test block (sDA 36 %, ASE 7 %, mean DF 1.68 %).\n\n**Blinds.** `tests/daylight-blinds.test.mjs` runs the engine on a 6 × 8 m room with one 4 m window wall in Toronto's generic year, turned to four compass directions. It checks that without blinds no hour is shut and sDA equals the open-window value; that with blinds no point gains daylit hours, ASE is unchanged, and a 5 % shade lowers sDA at least as much as 20 % louvres; and that a window turned to the sun shuts the blinds in many hours while one turned away from it shuts them in far fewer. In that room the blinds closed in 2,874 of 3,532 occupied daylit hours with the window facing the equator, and in 73 hours facing the pole (low summer sun); sDA fell from 46.9 % without blinds to 9.4 % with louvres facing the equator, and was unchanged facing the pole. The large drop facing the equator shows how much the omitted diffused sunlight matters in a sunny room, and why both bounds are reported. On the hospital example's ground floor in the Workspace (`tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs`), sDA was 19 % without blinds and 6 % with louvres.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nFor the daylight factor, the engine is accurate where daylight is strong and biased high where it is weak, because the split-flux method distributes reflected light uniformly. At points whose sky component is below about 1 %, DF can be over-predicted by a factor of two in absolute terms of a few tenths of a point; claims that \"the back of the room reaches 2 % DF\" should be checked with a ray tracer. The sky blend is a reasonable interpolation between two standard skies, but it is not the Perez all-weather luminance model used by Radiance's gendaymtx; ASE and sDA may therefore differ from reference CBDM by more than DF does. LM-83 computes sDA with blinds closed in hours when more than 2 % of sensors receive more than 1,000 lux of direct sun [3]. The engine now applies that rule, but without the sunlight the blinds diffuse, so it reports a range (with blinds, a lower bound; without, an upper bound) rather than an LM-83 value, and neither should be used for credit claims. Finally, the fallback synthetic year produces climate-like but fictitious results; the interface should state \"no weather file\" prominently whenever it is used.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nDaylight in SimuSite combines standard sky models, a validated glass model and the BRE split-flux method. Its daylight factor agrees with Radiance within the stated tolerance; its sDA (reported with LM-83 blinds as a lower bound and without blinds as an upper bound) and ASE are screening estimates and should be confirmed with an annual Radiance simulation for certification.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/daylight.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/daylight.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-deepen-1.cjs), [tests/validation/daylight_df.py](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/daylight_df.py), [tests/validation/daylight_engine.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/daylight_engine.cjs), [tests/daylight-blinds.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/daylight-blinds.test.mjs), [tests/shared-conditions.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/shared-conditions.cjs). This article's source: [daylight.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/daylight.md)."}], "references": ["C.F. Reinhart, J. Mardaljevic, Z. Rogers, Dynamic daylight performance metrics for sustainable building design, LEUKOS 3 (2006) 7–31. [doi:10.1582/LEUKOS.2006.03.01.001](https://doi.org/10.1582/LEUKOS.2006.03.01.001)", "J. Mardaljevic, L. Heschong, E. Lee, Daylight metrics and energy savings, Light. Res. Technol. 41 (2009) 261–283. [doi:10.1177/1477153509339703](https://doi.org/10.1177/1477153509339703)", "Illuminating Engineering Society, IES LM-83-12, Approved Method: IES Spatial Daylight Autonomy (sDA) and Annual Sunlight Exposure (ASE), IES, New York, 2012.", "CIE S 011/E:2003 (ISO 15469:2004), Spatial distribution of daylight – CIE standard general sky, Commission Internationale de l'Éclairage, Vienna, 2003.", "U.S. Green Building Council, LEED v4.1 Building Design and Construction, EQ credit: Daylight, USGBC, Washington, DC, 2019.", "P.R. Tregenza, Subdivision of the sky hemisphere for luminance measurements, Light. Res. Technol. 19 (1987) 13–14.", "P. Moon, D.E. Spencer, Illumination from a non-uniform sky, Illum. Eng. 37 (1942) 707–726.", "R. Perez, R. Seals, J. Michalsky, All-weather model for sky luminance distribution—preliminary configuration and validation, Sol. Energy 50 (1993) 235–245. [doi:10.1016/0038-092X(93)90017-I](https://doi.org/10.1016/0038-092X(93)90017-I)", "R.G. Hopkinson, J. Longmore, P. Petherbridge, An empirical formula for the computation of the indirect component of daylight factor, Trans. Illum. Eng. Soc. 19 (1954) 201–219.", "P.R. Tregenza, Modification of the split-flux formulae for mean daylight factor and internal reflected component with large external obstructions, Light. Res. Technol. 21 (1989) 125–128.", "G.J. Ward, The RADIANCE lighting simulation and rendering system, in: Proc. SIGGRAPH '94, ACM, 1994, pp. 459–472. [doi:10.1145/192161.192286](https://doi.org/10.1145/192161.192286)", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "Illuminating Engineering Society, The Lighting Handbook, 10th ed., chapter on daylighting, IES, New York, 2011.", "F. Kasten, A.T. Young, Revised optical air mass tables and approximation formula, Appl. Opt. 28 (1989) 4735–4738. [doi:10.1364/AO.28.004735](https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.28.004735)", "A. Aman, LM-83 simulation challenges, presentation at the 16th International Radiance Workshop, Portland, OR, 2017. [radiance-online.org](https://www.radiance-online.org/community/workshops/2017-portland-or/presentations/08_AMAN_LM83SimulationChallenges.pdf) (states the trigger: blinds engage if more than 2 % of the analysis area receives direct sunlight of 1,000 lux).", "M. Sadeghipour Roudsari and others, Blinds/shades operation in sDA calculation, Ladybug Tools forum, 2017–2018. [discourse.ladybug.tools](https://discourse.ladybug.tools/t/blinds-shades-operation-in-sda-calculation/2546) (quotes LM-83-12 section 2.2.7 on blind transmittances; a transcription, to be checked against the standard, whose 2023 revision may differ)."], "category": "research", "simulation": "daylight", "summary": "How the Daylight study computes spatial daylight autonomy, annual sun exposure and daylight factor; where its sky models, glass model, reflectances and thresholds come from; its agreement with Radiance for the daylight factor; and the limits of its split-flux inter-reflection and its fallback weather.", "image": "img/home/daylight.webp", "imageAlt": "Daylight autonomy over a floor plan, brighter near windows"}}, {"id": "report-wind", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Pedestrian wind screening with a two-dimensional lattice-Boltzmann model and the Lawson comfort criteria", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Pedestrian wind comfort", "Lattice Boltzmann method", "Lawson criteria", "Atmospheric boundary layer", "Urban microclimate"], "highlights": ["A 2D D2Q9 BGK lattice-Boltzmann flow at about 1.5 m gives speed ratios for each of 8 wind directions.", "EPW wind at 10 m is brought to 1.5 m with ASHRAE power-law profiles (α = 0.14, 0.22, 0.33).", "Comfort classes follow the City of London Lawson criteria (2019): 5 % exceedance thresholds 2.5, 4, 6, 8 m/s and 15 m/s safety.", "The lattice viscosity makes the simulated flow laminar at a Reynolds number of order 10–100, far below real flows.", "The flow model has not been compared with wind-tunnel data; results show patterns, not values to report."], "abstract": "Wind at pedestrian level is a frequent cause of discomfort and occasionally of danger around tall or closely spaced buildings, and many cities require wind microclimate assessments [1, 2]. The standard methods are boundary-layer wind tunnel tests and validated computational fluid dynamics (CFD) [3, 4]. SimuSite's Wind flow study offers a fast screening in the browser: a two-dimensional lattice-Boltzmann (LBM) flow on a horizontal slice at about 1.5 m, with buildings as solid cells, gives the ratio of local to free-stream speed for each of eight wind directions [5, 6]. Combined with the hourly wind of the project's EPW file, transformed from 10 m at the station to 1.5 m at the site with ASHRAE power-law profiles [7], it estimates each location's 95th-percentile speed and rates it with the City of London Lawson criteria [2]. This report documents the solver and its parameters and states its limits plainly: the model is two-dimensional (air cannot pass over roofs), its lattice viscosity corresponds to a Reynolds number of order 10–100 rather than 10⁶–10⁷, and it has not been compared with experimental data. The comfort thresholds and the wind profile are correctly sourced; the flow field is a qualitative indicator of channelling and shelter.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nPedestrian-level wind comfort is assessed by combining the statistical distribution of wind at a reference height with the speed-up factors that the buildings produce at each location, then comparing exceedance probabilities with comfort criteria [1, 3]. Lawson's criteria, in the form adopted by the City of London in 2019, assign each location an activity class by the speed exceeded 5 % of the time [2]. Speed-up factors normally come from wind-tunnel tests or steady RANS or LES CFD following best-practice guidelines such as COST Action 732 and AIJ [3, 4]. Both are beyond early design. A coarse, fast model can still show where channelling and shelter are likely, if its limits are understood.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Flow model\n\nThe site is rasterised on a grid of about 1.5 m at pedestrian height; cells inside buildings (the design and the surrounding buildings) are solid. The lattice-Boltzmann method with the D2Q9 velocity set and the BGK single-relaxation collision operator [5, 6] evolves particle distribution functions; walls use simple bounce-back. The free stream is imposed at the upwind edges as the equilibrium distribution at velocity u₀ = 0.08 (lattice units) in the wind direction; downwind edges are pressure outlets that take the neighbouring cell's velocity at the reference density. The relaxation time τ = 0.7 gives a lattice kinematic viscosity ν = (τ − ½)/3 ≈ 0.067. The solver runs for 3.5 × max(n_x, n_y) steps, i.e. long enough for the free stream to cross the domain about three and a half times. The output at each open cell is the speed ratio |u|/u₀ and a pressure coefficient relative to cells in undisturbed flow.\n\n### 2.2. From weather to local wind\n\nEach hour's EPW wind speed U₁₀ (10 m, open terrain at the station) is transformed to the site at height h = 1.5 m with the power-law profile of ASHRAE Fundamentals [7]: U_h = U₁₀ (δ_met/10)^α_met (h/δ)^α, with station terrain α_met = 0.14, δ_met = 270 m, and site terrain α = 0.14, 0.22 or 0.33 with boundary-layer depths δ = 270, 370 or 460 m for open, suburban and city terrain. The hourly speed at a location is U_h times the speed ratio of the nearest of the eight simulated directions.\n\n### 2.3. Comfort and safety\n\nAt each location the hourly local speeds are sorted and the 95th percentile (exceeded 5 % of the time) is compared with the City of London Lawson thresholds [2]: ≤ 2.5 m/s frequent sitting, ≤ 4 occasional sitting, ≤ 6 standing, ≤ 8 walking, above 8 uncomfortable. A location is unsafe for all users if 15 m/s is exceeded more than 0.022 % of the time (about 2 hours a year) [2].\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Wind flow study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lattice model | D2Q9, BGK, bounce-back | Qian et al.; Chen & Doolen [5, 6] | Sourced method |\n| Relaxation time τ | 0.7 | Engine choice (stability) | Flagged: sets ν and hence a very low Reynolds number |\n| Inlet speed u₀ | 0.08 lattice units | Engine choice (Mach ≈ 0.14) | Numerical setting |\n| Steps | 3.5 × max(n_x, n_y) | Engine choice | Flagged: convergence not checked |\n| Grid | ≈ 1.5 m | Engine choice | Numerical setting; narrow gaps under 2–3 cells are poorly resolved |\n| Wind profile | Power law, α 0.14 / 0.22 / 0.33, δ 270 / 370 / 460 m | ASHRAE Fundamentals 2021 ch. 24 [7] | Sourced |\n| Reference wind | EPW hourly speed and direction | EPW data dictionary [8] | Measured / typical-year data |\n| Comfort classes | 2.5 / 4 / 6 / 8 m/s at 5 % exceedance | City of London Lawson criteria [2] | Sourced |\n| Safety | 15 m/s for > 0.022 % of hours | City of London [2] | Sourced |\n| Directions | 8 | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe unit tests in `tests/climate-studies.cjs` check the Lawson classification (thresholds, 5 % rule, safety rule) and the wind profile. The flow solver itself has not been validated against wind-tunnel data; the validation plan names the surface pressures on a cube in a boundary layer (e.g. Castro and Robins [9]) and the AIJ pedestrian-wind benchmark cases [4] as next steps. In the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on a two-storey block with a Chicago TMY3 file, the study completed (eight directions, 1.5 m grid) and reported a largest speed-up of 1.57 in the windiest 1 % of the ground.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe method captures one real mechanism, the horizontal deflection of wind around and between buildings, and will show where a gap channels the flow and where a courtyard is sheltered. It misses others that often dominate pedestrian wind around taller buildings: the downwash of wind from upper floors down a windward facade, flow over roofs, and three-dimensional corner vortices [1, 3]. Because air cannot go over the buildings, speed-up in narrow gaps is overstated, and shelter in the lee of low buildings is overstated too.\n\nThe Reynolds number of the simulated flow deserves emphasis. With ν ≈ 0.067 lattice units and u₀ = 0.08, a building 20 cells (30 m) wide gives Re = u₀ L / ν ≈ 24. Real flows around buildings have Re of order 10⁶–10⁷ and are turbulent; wind-tunnel practice requires Re high enough for flow separation at sharp edges to be Reynolds-independent [3]. At Re ≈ 24 the flow is laminar, wakes are long and steady, and separation is not that of a turbulent flow. The speed ratios are therefore qualitatively plausible but not quantitatively transferable. A Smagorinsky subgrid model, a finer grid and a higher Reynolds number, or a three-dimensional solver, would be needed for quantitative use, followed by validation [3, 4].\n\nApplying station wind with a power-law profile is standard practice for screening [7], but local terrain roughness and upwind obstacles are represented only by the chosen terrain class.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe comfort statistics, thresholds and wind profiles of the Wind flow study are correctly sourced and implemented; the two-dimensional, low-Reynolds-number flow model is not validated. Results indicate where to look and how options compare qualitatively; they should not be presented as a wind microclimate assessment.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/wind-lbm.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/wind-lbm.js), [emb/lib/climate.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/climate.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/climate-studies.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/climate-studies.cjs). This article's source: [wind.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/wind.md)."}], "references": ["B. Blocken, J. Carmeliet, Pedestrian wind environment around buildings: literature review and practical examples, J. Therm. Envelope Build. Sci. 28 (2004) 107–159. [doi:10.1177/1097196304044396](https://doi.org/10.1177/1097196304044396)", "City of London Corporation, Wind Microclimate Guidelines for Developments in the City of London, City of London, August 2019.", "J. Franke, A. Hellsten, H. Schlünzen, B. Carissimo (Eds.), Best practice guideline for the CFD simulation of flows in the urban environment, COST Action 732, COST Office, Brussels, 2007.", "Y. Tominaga, A. Mochida, R. Yoshie, H. Kataoka, T. Nozu, M. Yoshikawa, T. Shirasawa, AIJ guidelines for practical applications of CFD to pedestrian wind environment around buildings, J. Wind Eng. Ind. Aerodyn. 96 (2008) 1749–1761. [doi:10.1016/j.jweia.2008.02.058](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jweia.2008.02.058)", "Y.H. Qian, D. d'Humières, P. Lallemand, Lattice BGK models for Navier–Stokes equation, Europhys. Lett. 17 (1992) 479–484. [doi:10.1209/0295-5075/17/6/001](https://doi.org/10.1209/0295-5075/17/6/001)", "S. Chen, G.D. Doolen, Lattice Boltzmann method for fluid flows, Annu. Rev. Fluid Mech. 30 (1998) 329–364. [doi:10.1146/annurev.fluid.30.1.329](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.fluid.30.1.329)", "ASHRAE, ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, chapter 24, Airflow around buildings, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2021.", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "I.P. Castro, A.G. Robins, The flow around a surface-mounted cube in uniform and turbulent streams, J. Fluid Mech. 79 (1977) 307–335. [doi:10.1017/S0022112077000172](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022112077000172)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "wind", "summary": "How the Wind flow study estimates speed-up and shelter at pedestrian height with a 2D D2Q9 lattice-Boltzmann solver, and rates comfort with the City of London Lawson criteria from hourly EPW wind. The report gives the sources of the wind profile, pressure and comfort constants, and explains why the flow model is a qualitative screening that has not been validated.", "image": "img/home/wind.webp", "imageAlt": "Wind speed ratio at pedestrian height around buildings, red where the wind speeds up"}}, {"id": "report-vent", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Whole-building natural ventilation with a multizone pressure network: wind, stack and single-sided flow", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Natural ventilation", "Airflow network", "Stack effect", "Wind pressure coefficient", "Single-sided ventilation"], "highlights": ["Each room is a well-mixed zone; windows and doors are orifices with Cd = 0.6 split into vertical strips so stack flow can reverse with height.", "Wind pressure uses the AIVC low-rise pressure coefficients for the chosen terrain and the ASHRAE conversion of the weather-station wind to roof height on the site; buoyancy uses the ideal-gas density of indoor and outdoor air.", "Rooms ventilated from one facade also get the de Gids and Phaff (1982) single-sided flow when it is larger.", "The audit found that the Workspace pressure-coefficient table differed from the AIVC table the method cites, and that the \"neutral plane (stack alone)\" was taken from the combined wind-and-stack solution; both are corrected."], "abstract": "Natural ventilation supplies outdoor air through windows, doors and vents driven by wind pressure on the facades and by the buoyancy of warm indoor air [1–3]. Design guides such as CIBSE AM10 and the AIVC guide describe two levels of calculation: simple correlations for single rooms, and multizone airflow networks in which each room is a node and each opening a nonlinear flow resistance [1, 2, 4]. SimuSite's Natural ventilation study builds such a network for the whole building model, with every floor, stair and void, and solves it for one wind speed, wind direction and pair of indoor and outdoor temperatures. It reports the outdoor-air flow and air change rate of each room, the share of air that arrives second-hand through other rooms, and the direction of flow through every opening. This report documents the equations, the constants and their sources. The orifice equation, the discharge coefficient, the buoyancy terms and the de Gids and Phaff single-sided correlation are correctly sourced. Three departures from the cited sources were found in the audit: the facade pressure-coefficient table in the Workspace engine was not the AIVC table that the method text and the stand-alone ventilation page use, the station wind was applied to the site without the terrain conversion, and the \"neutral plane (stack alone)\" value was taken from the combined wind-and-stack pressures. All three were corrected on 21 November 2026; none invalidated the network method.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nFor a room with openings on one facade, empirical correlations from field measurement give the ventilation rate directly [3, 5]. For cross-ventilation and for multi-storey buildings with stairs and atria, the flows through all openings are coupled: the pressure in each room adjusts until the mass entering equals the mass leaving. Multizone network models solve this balance; they are the basis of programs such as AIRNET, COMIS and CONTAM [4, 6]. They assume each zone is well mixed and each opening behaves as a sharp-edged orifice, and they need wind pressure coefficients on the facades, which in early design come from generic tables rather than from wind-tunnel tests [2, 7].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Zones and links\n\nRooms are taken from the building model on every level. Each exterior window or door becomes a link between its room and outside; each interior door a link between two rooms; each stair or floor void a link between the room below and the room above, or to outside if it reaches the roof. To represent two-way stack flow through a tall opening, each window is divided into six horizontal strips and each door into four, each strip with its own height z [1, 3]. The open area of a window is its width times its height times the opening share set by the user; closed outside doors are given a 1 cm crack.\n\n### 2.2. Pressures\n\nIndoor pressure in room r at height z is P_r − ρ_i g z, where P_r is the unknown room pressure at z = 0 and ρ_i the indoor air density. Outdoor pressure at an opening is C_p ½ ρ_o U_H² − ρ_o g z, with C_p the wind pressure coefficient for the facade, U_H the wind speed at roof height H and ρ_o the outdoor density [2, 7]. Densities follow the ideal-gas law at the given temperatures. The weather file's wind U_met is measured at 10 m at a station in open terrain. Following ASHRAE Fundamentals, it is carried up to the gradient height of open terrain and back down to roof height in the site's terrain: U_H = U_met (270 / 10)^0.14 (H / δ)^α, with H = max(3 m, top of the building), and δ = 270, 370 or 460 m and α = 0.14, 0.22 or 0.33 for open, suburban and urban terrain [7].\n\nC_p depends on the angle between the wind and the facade's outward normal, interpolated in the AIVC low-rise table at 0°, 45°, 90°, 135° and 180° [2]. The shelter row follows the terrain: open terrain uses the exposed row, suburban the row for neighbours half as tall, and urban the row for neighbours as tall. Roof openings of voids use C_p = −0.5.\n\n**Swami–Chandra option (added 23 November 2026).** As an alternative to the table, the Workspace can take facade C_p from the Swami and Chandra (1988) correlation for surface-averaged pressure coefficients on low-rise buildings [9], in the form given in the EnergyPlus Engineering Reference [10]:\n\nC_p = 0.6 ln[1.248 − 0.703 sin(α/2) − 1.175 sin²α + 0.131 sin³(2αG) + 0.769 cos(α/2) + 0.07 G² sin²(α/2) + 0.717 cos²(α/2)]\n\nHere α is the angle between the wind direction and the facade's outward normal, and G is the natural log of the ratio of the facade's width to the width of the adjacent wall. The study takes these widths from the plan's bounding box: a facade facing mostly along the plan's y axis spans its x extent. The correlation was fitted to surface-averaged data for rectangular, exposed low-rise buildings with sides up to 8:1 and has no shelter term [9], so the option is labelled \"exposed\" and the terrain's shelter class is not applied to it. At α = 0 it gives 0.603. For a square plan (G = 0) it gives −0.443 at 90° and −0.364 at 180°, against 0.7, −0.5 and −0.2 in the AIVC exposed row.\n\n### 2.3. Flow equation and solution\n\nThe mass flow through a link is the orifice equation ṁ = C_d A √(2 ρ |Δp|) sign(Δp), with C_d = 0.6 and ρ the density of the air upstream [1, 3]. Below |Δp| = 0.02 Pa the relation is linearised so that the Jacobian stays finite. The mass balance of every room is solved for the room pressures with Newton–Raphson, a damping factor of 0.8 and up to 120 iterations [4]. Flow rates are then converted to volume flows, and the outdoor-air fraction of each room is found by Gauss–Seidel iteration on f_r Q_in,r = Q_out→r + Σ f_s Q_s→r, so that air arriving through a corridor counts as partly used air.\n\n### 2.4. Single-sided ventilation\n\nThe network cannot represent the turbulent pumping that ventilates a room with openings on one facade only. For such rooms the study also computes the de Gids and Phaff correlation, Q = (A/2) √(C₁ U² + C₂ H ΔT + C₃), with C₁ = 0.001, C₂ = 0.0035 and C₃ = 0.01, where A is the open area, H the opening height and U the 10 m wind speed [5]. This is the form given in CIBSE AM10 and BS 5925 [1, 3]. The larger of the network and correlation flows is used.\n\n### 2.5. Outputs\n\nThe study reports for each room the outdoor-air flow in L/s, the air change rate (flow × 3.6 / volume), the outdoor-air fraction, and the building total. It also reports a stack-only neutral plane height, z_n = Σ V_r (−P_r / (Δρ g)) / Σ V_r, from a second solve of the same network with no wind. The legend compares air change rates with a user-set target; the ASHRAE 62.1 natural ventilation procedure governs opening size, not a fixed rate [8].\n\nA \"Through the year\" mode takes the window-open hours of the project weather file (hours with outdoor temperature in a user-set range), groups them by 16 wind directions, 5 speed bands and 3 temperature bands, solves the network once per group, and reports for each room the share of window-open hours at or above the target and the median air change rate. Indoor temperature in each group is the set value or 1 K above outdoor, whichever is higher.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Natural ventilation study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Discharge coefficient C_d | 0.6 | CIBSE AM10; BS 5925 [1, 3] | Sourced (sharp-edged opening) |\n| Flow exponent | 0.5 | Orifice equation [1, 4] | Sourced |\n| Strips per window / door | 6 / 4 | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Linearisation threshold | 0.02 Pa | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Wind profile α, gradient height δ | 0.14 / 0.22 / 0.33; 270 / 370 / 460 m | ASHRAE Fundamentals ch. 24 [7] | Sourced; station-to-site conversion added 21 November 2026 |\n| Reference height | max(3 m, roof) | Engine choice | Reasonable for low-rise tables [2] |\n| Facade C_p (Workspace before 21 November 2026) | 0.6, 0.4, −0.3, −0.45, −0.35 at 0–180° | Not traced | Replaced by the AIVC table |\n| Facade C_p (Workspace and Ventilation page) | Exposed 0.7, 0.35, −0.5, −0.4, −0.2; half-height neighbours 0.4, 0.1, −0.3, −0.35, −0.2; equal-height neighbours 0.2, 0.05, −0.25, −0.3, −0.25 | AIVC guide, low-rise tables [2] | Sourced |\n| Facade C_p, Swami–Chandra option | 0.6 ln[…] by angle and side ratio G (formula in Section 2.2) | Swami and Chandra 1988 [9]; EnergyPlus Engineering Reference [10] | Sourced; exposed, rectangular plans only; G from the plan's bounding box (added 23 November 2026) |\n| Roof void C_p | −0.5 | Engine choice | Assumption, consistent in sign with flat-roof suction [2, 7] |\n| Single-sided C₁, C₂, C₃ | 0.001, 0.0035, 0.01 | de Gids and Phaff [5]; CIBSE AM10 [1] | Sourced |\n| Defaults | U_10 3.5 m/s, from 225°, T_o 20 °C, T_i 24 °C | Engine choice | Editable inputs |\n| Closed-door crack | 1 cm × door width | Engine choice | Assumption |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace test `tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs` checks that the network covers rooms on both levels, that ajar windows let in less air than open ones, that stack alone still gives a positive flow, that air enters on one side and leaves on the other, and that the through-the-year mode returns more than 500 window-open hours. These are qualitative checks. There is no comparison against measured airflows or against CONTAM. A useful next step would be the analytical two-opening stack case, Q = C_d A* √(2 g h ΔT / T), and the cross-ventilation case Q = C_d A* U √ΔC_p, which have closed-form solutions [1, 3], followed by a CONTAM comparison for a multi-storey case [6].\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on a two-storey, twenty-room block with a Chicago TMY3 file (wind from the south at 4.9 m/s), the study reported 13,485 L/s of outdoor air, 32.4 air changes per hour for the whole building and at least 5 air changes per hour in every room, and a neutral plane at −1.0 m. After the corrections of 21 November 2026 (AIVC table, suburban terrain, station-to-site wind, stack-only neutral plane) the same case gives 7,829 L/s, 18.8 air changes per hour, every room still at 5 or more, and a stack-only neutral plane 3.0 m above level 0, inside the two-storey building as expected. The lower flow comes mainly from the suburban wind at roof height, which is about 0.6 times the open-terrain wind at 10 m, and from the smaller sheltered pressure coefficients.\n\n**Swami–Chandra coefficients.** `tests/vent-cp-swami.test.mjs` runs the engine's function in Node and checks it against the printed formula. It gives 0.603 at 0°, −0.443 at 90° and −0.364 at 180° for a square plan, matches the formula at 25 combinations of angle (15–165°) and side-ratio log (−1 to ln 3), and treats ±α and α + 360° alike. For comparison with the AIVC exposed row at 0°, 45°, 90°, 135° and 180°, it gives 0.603, 0.323, −0.443, −0.535 and −0.364, against 0.7, 0.35, −0.5, −0.4 and −0.2. On the hospital example, with the wind from 225° and 20 °C outside and 22 °C inside, the network gave a mean of 2.54 air changes per hour at 2 m/s and 10.22 at 8 m/s with the AIVC suburban row, and 3.56 and 14.24 with Swami–Chandra. The difference is mostly the absence of shelter in the correlation. With the default AIVC option the Workspace result is unchanged from before (1.68 air changes per hour in the hospital example's default run).\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Pressure coefficients.** Generic C_p tables are the largest source of uncertainty in network models, often ±50 % or more on the flow [2, 4]. The stand-alone ventilation page uses the AIVC low-rise table with three shelter classes, which the method text cites [2]. At the time of the audit the Workspace engine used a different single table (0.6, 0.4, −0.3, −0.45, −0.35) with no shelter class and no source in the code; its leeward and side values differed from the AIVC exposed row by up to 0.2. It now uses the AIVC table, with the shelter class taken from the project's terrain. The Swami and Chandra correlation [9], which gives C_p as a function of wind angle and side ratio, is now available as an option (Section 2.2). It suits exposed, rectangular buildings. In sheltered sites the AIVC rows remain the better default.\n\n**Wind profile.** ASHRAE converts the meteorological-station wind to the site by passing through the gradient height: U_H = U_met (δ_met / H_met)^α_met (H / δ)^α [7]. At the time of the audit the engine applied the site exponent directly to the station speed at 10 m, which assumes the station is in the same terrain as the site and, for an airport station and an urban site, overstates the wind at roof height by about 20–40 %. The engine now applies the full conversion, assuming the station is in open terrain, as airport stations usually are.\n\n**Neutral plane.** The code describes the neutral plane as the height where inside and outside pressure match with no wind, but at the time of the audit the value was computed from room pressures solved with wind included. In a strong wind the room pressures are set mainly by the facade pressures, and the computed height fell below the floor, as the audit's −1.0 m showed. The engine now solves the network a second time with no wind and takes the neutral plane from that solution.\n\n**Model limits.** Well-mixed zones cannot show air speed or the distribution of fresh air within a room, and the orifice model does not apply to large openings with bidirectional turbulent exchange [1, 3]. Each solve is a single steady condition. The through-the-year mode averages steady solutions over binned hours; it does not model thermal mass, the indoor temperature response, or occupants closing windows.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe network method, discharge coefficient, buoyancy terms and single-sided correlation of the Natural ventilation study are correctly sourced and implemented. The pressure-coefficient table, the station-to-site wind conversion and the stack-only neutral plane were corrected after the audit. Results are suitable for comparing opening strategies in early design, not for demonstrating compliance.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [emb/modeler.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/modeler.html), [emb/vent.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/vent.html), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs), [tests/vent-cp-swami.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/vent-cp-swami.test.mjs). This article's source: [vent.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/vent.md)."}], "references": ["CIBSE, AM10: Natural ventilation in non-domestic buildings, Chartered Institution of Building Services Engineers, London, 2005.", "M.W. Liddament, A Guide to Energy Efficient Ventilation, Air Infiltration and Ventilation Centre (AIVC), Coventry, 1996.", "British Standards Institution, BS 5925:1991, Code of practice for ventilation principles and designing for natural ventilation, BSI, London, 1991.", "D. Etheridge, Natural Ventilation of Buildings: Theory, Measurement and Design, Wiley, Chichester, 2012. [doi:10.1002/9781119951773](https://doi.org/10.1002/9781119951773)", "W. de Gids, H. Phaff, Ventilation rates and energy consumption due to open windows: a brief overview of research in the Netherlands, Air Infiltration Review, AIVC, 1982.", "W.S. Dols, B.J. Polidoro, CONTAM User Guide and Program Documentation, Version 3.4, NIST Technical Note 1887 Rev. 1, National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, 2020. [doi:10.6028/NIST.TN.1887r1](https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.TN.1887r1)", "ASHRAE, ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, chapter 24, Airflow around buildings, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2021.", "ASHRAE, ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1-2022, Ventilation and Acceptable Indoor Air Quality, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2022.", "M.V. Swami, S. Chandra, Correlations for pressure distribution on buildings and calculation of natural-ventilation airflow, ASHRAE Transactions 94 (1) (1988) 243–266.", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Engineering Reference, version 9.6, AirflowNetwork model, wind pressure calculations (surface-average calculation for low-rise buildings after Swami and Chandra). [bigladdersoftware.com](https://bigladdersoftware.com/epx/docs/9-6/engineering-reference/airflownetwork-model.html)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "vent", "summary": "How the Natural ventilation study estimates the outdoor air each room receives from wind and buoyancy, with a multizone orifice network solved by Newton–Raphson and the single-sided correlation of de Gids and Phaff. The report gives the sources of the discharge coefficient, wind pressure coefficients and wind profile, and records two departures from the cited sources found in the audit and corrected on 21 November 2026.", "image": "img/home/vent.webp", "imageAlt": "Air change rate per room with arrows showing the airflow through windows and doors"}}, {"id": "report-snow", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Snow drift screening from the winter wind: a lattice-Boltzmann speed field, an empirical deposition rule and basic roof loads", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Snowdrift", "Blowing snow", "Snow load", "Lattice Boltzmann method", "Cold-climate design"], "highlights": ["The drifting wind is the most frequent direction and mean speed of the strongest tenth of below-freezing hours in the project EPW.", "A 2D lattice-Boltzmann flow at 1.5 m gives speed ratios; snow depth is scaled by 1.65 − 0.85 × ratio, clamped to 0.1–2.4 times the fall.", "Ground load is depth × density × g with 150 kg/m³ (200 kg/m³ for falls of 40 cm or more); flat roofs carry 0.8 × the ground load, the NBC basic roof factor.", "The deposition rule and its limits have no published source; drift results are qualitative.", "The study is not a code snow-load calculation: codes use statistical ground snow loads, not a single event."], "abstract": "Wind redistributes falling and lying snow around buildings, burying entrances and windward corners of courtyards while scouring others, and loading roofs unevenly [1–3]. Engineering practice handles this with wind-tunnel and water-flume tests, computational models of snow transport, and, for roof loads, the empirical drift provisions of structural codes [3–6]. SimuSite's Snow study is a fast screening for early design. It picks a characteristic drifting wind from the project weather file, computes a two-dimensional lattice-Boltzmann (LBM) flow around the buildings at pedestrian height, and redistributes one user-specified snowfall with an empirical rule: snow deepens where the wind slows and is removed where it speeds up. It reports depth, a drift factor and the implied ground and flat-roof loads. This report documents those steps and their constants. The weather statistic, the density of new snow, the transport threshold and the 0.8 flat-roof factor are sourced or consistent with the literature; the deposition rule (1.65 − 0.85 × speed ratio, clamped to 0.1–2.4) is a heuristic with no published source, and the flow model inherits the limits of the Wind study. The results show the likely pattern, not depths or loads to design to.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nSnow is transported by the wind once the friction velocity exceeds a threshold that depends on the age, temperature and bonding of the snow [7]. Around a building, the flow separates at the windward edges, accelerates around corners and decelerates in the wake; snow is eroded in the accelerated zones and deposited in the decelerated ones [2, 3]. Tominaga's review describes CFD models that couple the airflow to snow transport equations and erosion and deposition fluxes, and notes that even these need careful validation against field or wind-tunnel data [3]. For roof loads, structural codes give the balanced load as a roof factor times a statistical ground snow load, and add empirical drift surcharges at steps and parapets derived from field observations [4–6].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. The drifting wind\n\nThe study reads the project's EnergyPlus Weather (EPW) file and selects the hours with dry-bulb temperature at or below 0 °C and wind speed of at least 0.5 m/s [8]. If fewer than 24 such hours exist, it stops and reports that drifting does not apply. Of the selected hours, those at or above the 90th percentile of wind speed are binned into 16 directions; the most frequent direction and the mean speed of its hours define a single drifting wind. The 10 m speed is brought to 1.5 m with the ASHRAE power-law profile for the site's terrain class (α = 0.14, 0.22 or 0.33) [9].\n\n### 2.2. Flow field\n\nThe same D2Q9 lattice-Boltzmann solver used by the Wind flow study computes the ratio r of local to free-stream speed on a horizontal grid at about 1.5 m, with the design and the surrounding context buildings as solid cells [10]. Its limits (two-dimensional, laminar at a lattice Reynolds number of order 10–100, unvalidated) are documented in the Wind flow report.\n\n### 2.3. Deposition and erosion rule\n\nEach open cell receives a drift factor f = 1 + k₀ (clamp(1.65 − 0.85 r, 0.1, 2.4) − 1), and a depth equal to the snowfall times f. Thus at r = 0.76 the snow lies as it fell, in still air (r = 0) it reaches 1.65 times the fall, and at r ≥ 1.82 it falls to 0.1 times. The factor k₀ = clamp((1.3 U − 2.5)/3, 0, 1), with U the drifting speed at 1.5 m, scales the effect with the wind: it is zero below about 1.9 m/s at 1.5 m and full above about 4.2 m/s. The code describes this as snow moving once the local wind passes about 4 m/s at 1.5 m. Facades take the depth of the adjacent ground cells; roofs take 0.8 times the fall.\n\n### 2.4. Loads\n\nThe density of the fall is 150 kg/m³, or 200 kg/m³ for falls of 40 cm or more, and the load is depth × density × g. The flat-roof load is 0.8 times the open-ground load, the basic roof snow load factor C_b of the National Building Code of Canada for roofs of ordinary size [4]. The study reports the open-ground load, the flat-roof load, the area drifted at least 30 % deeper than the fall, the area scoured below 60 % of the fall, the deepest drift and the mean depth against facades.\n\n### 2.5. Uniform snowfall page\n\nA second tool, linked from the study card, distributes a uniform snowfall over the terrain and roofs by vertical ray sampling and reports areas, volume, mass and water equivalent. Its default density is 100 kg/m³ and it accepts 10–600 kg/m³. It does no drifting.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Snow study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Below-freezing hours | T ≤ 0 °C, U ≥ 0.5 m/s | EPW data [8] | Measured / typical-year data; precipitation is not checked |\n| Drifting wind | Top 10 % of speeds; modal direction of 16; mean speed | Engine choice | Reasonable statistic, not from a standard |\n| Wind profile | α 0.14 / 0.22 / 0.33 | ASHRAE Fundamentals ch. 24 [9] | Sourced |\n| Transport threshold | ≈ 4 m/s at 1.5 m (ramp 1.9–4.2 m/s) | Code comment | Consistent with low thresholds for fresh dry snow [7]; Li and Pomeroy report about 4–11 m/s at 10 m, dry snow averaging 7.7 m/s [7] |\n| Drift rule | f = 1.65 − 0.85 r, limits 0.1–2.4 | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source, not calibrated |\n| Snow density | 150 kg/m³; 200 kg/m³ if ≥ 40 cm | Engine choice | Plausible upper range of new snow (≈ 50–200 kg/m³ [11]); conservative for load |\n| Uniform page density | 100 kg/m³ default | Engine choice | Typical new-snow value [11] |\n| Flat-roof factor | 0.8 | NBC 2020 C_b [4] | Sourced (basic factor; ASCE 7 uses 0.7 C_e C_t I_s [5]) |\n| Default snowfall | 15 cm (uniform page) / user choice | Engine choice | Editable input |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\n`tests/snow-analysis.test.mjs` checks the uniform snowfall tool exactly: areas, volume (3.2 m³), mass (320 kg at 100 kg/m³), water equivalent and the handling of sloped surfaces, gaps and invalid inputs. The Workspace drifting study is exercised by the Workspace tests for completion and output format only. The drift pattern has not been compared with field or wind-tunnel data; candidate benchmarks are the snowdrift measurements around a cube by Beyers and Waechter and the cases collected in Tominaga's review [3, 12].\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 (two-storey block, Chicago O'Hare TMY3 file, 30 cm fall), the study found 1,862 hours at or below 0 °C, a drifting wind from the north-north-west at 9.0 m/s at 10 m, 0.44 kPa on open ground (30 cm × 150 kg/m³ × g, checked by hand), 0.35 kPa on a flat roof, 837 m² drifted at least 30 % deeper, 1,656 m² scoured, a deepest drift of 49 cm and a mean of 43 cm against the facades.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**What it shows.** The rule ties deposition to low wind speed and erosion to high speed, which is the first-order mechanism described in the literature [2, 3]. Lee-side drifts and corner scour will appear in the right places for the given wind direction, within the limits of a two-dimensional flow.\n\n**What it misses.** Real drifting depends on the snow flux arriving from upwind fetch, on the duration of the storm, and on the wind history, not on the local speed alone [1, 3]. Snow does not accumulate without limit in still air, and erosion does not depend linearly on the speed ratio. Drifts on roofs, at roof steps and behind parapets, which govern structural design, are not modelled; the code drift provisions of NBC and ASCE 7 should be used for those [4–6]. Two-dimensional flow over-predicts both channelling and shelter because air cannot pass over the buildings.\n\n**Loads.** Code ground snow loads are statistical values (for example the 1-in-50-year load) that include accumulation over a season and rain on snow [4, 5]. A single fall of user-chosen depth at a fixed density is not equivalent. The study's loads are a way to visualise the consequence of a given fall, and the method text says it is not a snow load calculation.\n\n**Precipitation.** The weather selection uses temperature and wind only; it does not check whether snow fell or lay on the ground in those hours. EPW files carry snow depth and liquid precipitation fields, but these are often missing in typical-year files [8].\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe drifting-wind statistic, terrain profile, density and flat-roof factor of the Snow study are sourced or consistent with published values, and the uniform snowfall tool is verified exactly. The deposition rule is a heuristic and the flow is the unvalidated two-dimensional model of the Wind study. The study is a qualitative screening for entrances, paths and courtyards in early design, not a source of design snow loads.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js), [mvp/js/wind-lbm.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/wind-lbm.js), [mvp/js/snow-analysis.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/snow-analysis.js), [emb/lib/climate.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/climate.js). Tests: [tests/snow-analysis.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/snow-analysis.test.mjs), [tests/free-simulations.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/free-simulations.cjs). This article's source: [snow.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/snow.md)."}], "references": ["R.D. Tabler, Controlling Blowing and Drifting Snow with Snow Fences and Road Design, Final report, NCHRP Project 20-7(147), Transportation Research Board, Washington, DC, 2003.", "M. O'Rourke, Snow loads: guide to the snow load provisions of ASCE 7-10, ASCE Press, Reston, 2012.", "Y. Tominaga, Computational fluid dynamics simulation of snowdrift around buildings: past achievements and future perspectives, Cold Reg. Sci. Technol. 150 (2018) 2–14. [doi:10.1016/j.coldregions.2017.05.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coldregions.2017.05.004)", "National Research Council Canada, National Building Code of Canada 2020, Part 4, Subsection 4.1.6, Loads due to snow and rain, NRC, Ottawa, 2022.", "ASCE, Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria for Buildings and Other Structures, ASCE/SEI 7-22, chapter 7, Snow loads, ASCE, Reston, 2022.", "M.J. O'Rourke, W. Tobiasson, E. Wood, Proposed code provisions for drifted snow loads, J. Struct. Eng. 112 (9) (1986) 2080–2092. [doi:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9445(1986)112:9(2080)](https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9445(1986)112:9(2080))", "L. Li, J.W. Pomeroy, Estimates of threshold wind speeds for snow transport using meteorological data, J. Appl. Meteorol. 36 (1997) 205–213. [doi:10.1175/1520-0450(1997)036<0205:EOTWSF>2.0.CO;2](https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0450(1997)036%3C0205:EOTWSF%3E2.0.CO;2)", "U.S. Department of Energy, EnergyPlus Auxiliary Programs: EnergyPlus Weather File (EPW) Data Dictionary, EnergyPlus documentation, 2023.", "ASHRAE, ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, chapter 24, Airflow around buildings, ASHRAE, Atlanta, 2021.", "S. Chen, G.D. Doolen, Lattice Boltzmann method for fluid flows, Annu. Rev. Fluid Mech. 30 (1998) 329–364. [doi:10.1146/annurev.fluid.30.1.329](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.fluid.30.1.329)", "A. Judson, N. Doesken, Density of freshly fallen snow in the central Rocky Mountains, Bull. Am. Meteorol. Soc. 81 (7) (2000) 1577–1587. [doi:10.1175/1520-0477(2000)081<1577:DOFFSI>2.3.CO;2](https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0477(2000)081%3C1577:DOFFSI%3E2.3.CO;2)", "M. Beyers, B. Waechter, Modeling transient snowdrift development around complex three-dimensional structures, J. Wind Eng. Ind. Aerodyn. 96 (2008) 1603–1615."], "category": "research", "simulation": "snow", "summary": "How the Snow study estimates where one snowfall drifts deep and where the winter wind scours the ground bare, and what it implies for ground and flat-roof loads. The report gives the sources of the drifting-wind statistic, snow density, transport threshold and roof factor, and flags the drift rule itself as an unvalidated heuristic.", "image": "img/home/snow.webp", "imageAlt": "Snow depth around buildings after drifting, deeper in the lee and scoured at corners"}}, {"id": "report-stormwater", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Stormwater runoff and ponding on a site grid: runoff coefficients, D8 routing and fill-and-spill depressions", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Stormwater", "Rational method", "Runoff coefficient", "D8 flow routing", "Depression filling"], "highlights": ["Runoff is rain × a runoff coefficient per cell: planted 0.25, mixed 0.5, paved 0.85, drawn paving 0.9, roofs 0.95, green roofs 0.5.", "Depressions are found with the priority-flood algorithm; water is routed downhill with the D8 single-direction method.", "The roof peak flow uses the rational method Q = C i A.", "An audit test found that a hollow's inflow was taken as the largest single flow branch inside it, so ponded volumes could be ten times too small; the engine now sums every branch that leaves the hollow, and the test is part of the suite.", "The 25, 50 and 100 mm one-hour storms are user choices, not local intensity–duration–frequency data."], "abstract": "Site design must show where surface water will run and where it will collect, both to avoid flooding buildings and to place swales, rain gardens and inlets [1, 2]. Detailed drainage design uses hydrologic and hydraulic models such as SWMM with local rainfall statistics [3, 4]. SimuSite's Stormwater study is a screening on the site grid: one design storm falls in one hour, each cell sheds rain times a runoff coefficient, roofs shed their water to the ground beside them, water is routed downhill with the D8 method on a depression-filled surface, and hollows fill up to their spill level with the water that reaches them [5–7]. It reports runoff volume, flow paths, ponded area and depth, water held in hollows, and the rational-method peak flow from the roofs [1, 8]. This report documents the steps and constants. The runoff coefficients lie within the ranges of standard tables, and the flow routing uses published algorithms. A test written for this audit found one defect: the volume reaching a hollow was taken as the largest flow accumulation inside it, which counts only one branch of the flow network on the filled surface; in a test bowl it gave 7 % of the correct volume. The engine was corrected on 21 November 2026 and now passes the test. The storm depths are not linked to local rainfall statistics.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nThe rational method, introduced by Kuichling in 1889, estimates the peak flow from a small catchment as Q = C i A, where C is a runoff coefficient that lumps infiltration and storage losses, i the rainfall intensity for a duration equal to the time of concentration, and A the area [1, 8]. It remains in use for small urban catchments [2]. For the spatial pattern of flow, terrain analysis routes water over a digital elevation model. The D8 method sends all flow from a cell to its steepest downslope neighbour [5]. Depressions, real or artefacts, must first be handled; the priority-flood algorithm fills them optimally from the edges inward [6, 7], and the filled minus original surface gives the depth a depression can hold before it spills.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Grid, cover and rain\n\nThe study samples the terrain, buildings and site cover on a regular grid (1.5 m in the audit). Building cells are raised 200 m so that roof water runs to the cells beside them. Each open cell receives the ground coefficient of the chosen site cover (mostly planted 0.25, mixed 0.5, mostly paved 0.85); cells with mapped or drawn cover use planting 0.25, paving 0.9 and water 1. Design roofs use 0.95, or 0.5 if a green-roof layer is present; surrounding buildings use 0.95. The rain depth P is 25, 50 or 100 mm, falling in 1 hour. Runoff per cell is P × C × cell area.\n\n**NRCS curve number (added 23 November 2026).** As an alternative to fixed coefficients, the study can compute runoff with the curve number method of USDA NRCS Technical Release 55 [12]. Runoff depth is Q = (P − 0.2S)²/(P + 0.8S) when P exceeds the initial abstraction I_a = 0.2S, and zero otherwise (eq. 2-3), with S = 1000/CN − 10 in inches (eq. 2-4). The user chooses the hydrologic soil group, A to D, from a soil survey. Cover is mapped onto table 2-2a:\n- Planted ground and drawn planting are open space in good condition (grass cover above 75 %): CN 39, 61, 74 and 80 for groups A to D.\n- Paving and roofs are impervious, CN 98.\n- The study's \"mixed\" and \"mostly paved\" ground are composites with 50 % and 85 % connected impervious area. These are computed as TR-55's figure 2-3 draws them, CN = CN_p + f_imp (98 − CN_p). At 85 % this reproduces the \"commercial and business\" row of table 2-2a.\n- Water bodies take all the rain they receive.\n- Green roofs, for which TR-55 gives no value, keep 0.5.\n\nEach cell then gets the coefficient Q/P for the chosen storm, so the routing and ponding below are unchanged. Unlike fixed coefficients, the share of rain that runs off now grows with the storm. With CN 98, 25 mm of rain gives 79 % runoff and 100 mm gives 94 %. With CN 61, a 25 mm storm gives none, because P is below I_a = 32 mm.\n\n### 2.2. Depression filling and flow direction\n\nA priority-flood from the grid edge and from mapped water bodies computes the spill level F of every cell, with an increment of 10⁻⁵ m per step across flats so that they drain [6, 7]. Each cell sends its flow to the neighbour with the steepest descent of F among its eight neighbours (D8) [5]. Water bodies are outlets: what reaches them is counted and removed.\n\n### 2.3. Accumulation and ponding\n\nRunoff is accumulated downstream in reverse flood order. Connected cells where F − z ≥ 0.01 m form hollows. Each hollow's capacity is the sum of (F − z) × area. Its inflow is the sum of the accumulated flow of its cells whose flow leaves the hollow, that is, everything that drains into it, including the rain on the hollow itself. If the inflow is at least the capacity, the hollow fills to its spill level; otherwise the water level is found by bisection so that the stored volume equals the inflow (\"fill and spill\").\n\n### 2.4. Outputs\n\nThe study reports runoff and rain volumes, the share reaching site water, the area ponded 2 cm or deeper, the deepest ponding, the volume held in hollows, the design roof area and coefficient, the rational-method roof peak flow Q = C i A (with i = P per hour), and the share of facade squares with water against them or on a flow path. Flow paths are drawn from channel heads carrying at least 1.2 % of the largest accumulation.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Stormwater study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Runoff coefficient, planted | 0.25 | Rational-method tables: lawns 0.05–0.35 [2, 8] | Within range |\n| Mixed cover | 0.5 | Rational-method tables [2, 8] | Within range for residential areas |\n| Paved (cover / drawn) | 0.85 / 0.9 | Asphalt and concrete 0.70–0.95 [2, 8] | Within range |\n| Roofs | 0.95 | Roofs 0.75–0.95 [2, 8] | Upper end, conservative |\n| Green roof | 0.5 | FLL guideline values for extensive roofs vary with build-up and slope [9] | Plausible, not tied to a build-up |\n| Water | 1 | Definition | — |\n| Design storms | 25 / 50 / 100 mm in 1 h | User choice | Flagged: not linked to local IDF data such as NOAA Atlas 14 [10] |\n| Curve number method (option) | Q = (P − 0.2S)²/(P + 0.8S), S = 1000/CN − 10 (in) | TR-55 eqs. 2-3 and 2-4 [12] | Sourced; checked against TR-55 table 2-1 (added 23 November 2026) |\n| Curve numbers | Open space, good: 39 / 61 / 74 / 80 (A–D); impervious 98; composites at 0, 50, 85 % impervious | TR-55 table 2-2a and figure 2-3 [12] | Sourced; soil group chosen by the user; average antecedent runoff condition |\n| Flat increment | 10⁻⁵ m | Priority-flood with ε [7] | Sourced method |\n| Flow routing | D8 | O'Callaghan and Mark [5] | Sourced method |\n| Hollow threshold | 0.01 m | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Ponding shown | ≥ 0.02 m | Engine choice | Display setting |\n| Hollow inflow | Sum of the flows leaving the hollow | Mass balance | Corrected 21 November 2026, see §4 |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace tests check that the study runs and that its views, animation and flow lines appear; there was no unit test of `stormwater-grid.js`. For this report we wrote one, now `tests/stormwater-grid.test.mjs`. On a 21 × 21 grid of 1 m cells with a conical bowl 5 m in radius and 0.5 m deep, surrounded by ground sloping gently away to the edges, every runoff coefficient set to 1:\n\n- Mass balance holds exactly: runoff equals rain (4.41, 44.1 and 176.4 m³ for 10, 100 and 400 mm).\n- The hollow's capacity is 13.4 m³, against 13.1 m³ for the continuous cone; the difference is the discretisation.\n- The rain falling inside the bowl is 0.69 m³ at 10 mm (69 cells), and the sum of accumulated flow leaving the bowl's cells is 0.69 m³, as it should be. But the hollow's inflow used by the engine was 0.05 m³ at 10 mm, 0.50 m³ at 100 mm and 2.0 m³ at 400 mm. At 400 mm, 27.6 m³ falls in the bowl, so it should fill to spilling, yet the engine reported 2.0 m³ held and the bowl not full.\n\nThe cause is that the priority-flood seeds the flat from every rim cell, so the filled surface inside a hollow drains through many branches; taking the maximum accumulation of any one cell counts a single branch. The correct inflow is the sum of the accumulation of the hollow's cells whose flow leaves the hollow. The engine now uses that sum. After the change the hollow's inflow is 0.69 m³ at 10 mm, equal to the rain on its cells, and at 400 mm the bowl fills to its full capacity and spills; all three checks pass. Flow paths and runoff totals were never affected, and the end-to-end audit below, which has no hollows, gives the same figures before and after.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on a flat site with a two-storey block, a 50 mm storm and mixed cover gave 186 m³ of runoff from 360 m³ of rain, no ponding (as expected on flat ground with drainage to the edges), 257 m² of roof and a roof peak flow of 3.4 L/s (0.95 × 50 mm/h × 257 m² / 3600, checked by hand).\n\n**Curve numbers.** `tests/stormwater-cn.test.mjs` compares the runoff equation with eleven entries of TR-55 table 2-1, from 1 to 10 inches of rain and CN 40 to 90, including cases below the initial abstraction; all agree within 0.006 inch, the table's rounding. It also reproduces the composite of TR-55's own worked example (pervious CN 61 with 20 % impervious area gives 68) and the table 2-2a rows for commercial districts (85 % impervious: 89, 92, 94, 95) and town houses (65 %: 77, 85, 90, 92).\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Storm depth.** A one-hour depth of 25 mm is a moderate storm in many temperate climates, while 100 mm in one hour is extreme almost everywhere outside the tropics. Designers should choose a depth from local intensity–duration–frequency data for the return period and duration of interest; in the United States these are published in NOAA Atlas 14 [10]. Linking the study to such data, or at least showing the return period of the chosen depth, would make the result easier to interpret.\n\n**Runoff coefficients.** The rational method's C values are lumped and depend on soil, slope, antecedent wetness and storm size; tables give ranges, not single values [2, 8]. The values used are reasonable defaults. With the default coefficients, infiltration over time, which SWMM and the NRCS curve number method represent, is not modelled [3, 11]. The curve number option accounts for the initial abstraction and the storm-dependent loss to the soil, for the average antecedent runoff condition of TR-55 [12]. It still has no timing: TR-55's peak-flow and hydrograph methods are not used.\n\n**Routing.** D8 concentrates flow into single lines, which suits channels but exaggerates concentration on planar slopes; multiple-flow-direction methods spread flow more realistically [5]. Without pipes, inlets, kerbs or flow over time, the study shows where water would go if the site had no drainage system, which is the right question for early grading but not a drainage design.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Stormwater study's runoff coefficients are within published ranges, its mass balance is exact, and its routing uses standard algorithms. Ponded volumes were underestimated where a hollow drained through more than one flow branch, by a factor of ten or more in our test; this has been corrected and is covered by a unit test. Storm depths should be chosen from local rainfall statistics.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/stormwater-grid.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/stormwater-grid.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/stormwater-grid.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/stormwater-grid.test.mjs), [tests/stormwater-cn.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/stormwater-cn.test.mjs), [tests/workspace-surfaces.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-surfaces.cjs), [tests/workspace-motion2.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-motion2.cjs). This article's source: [stormwater.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/stormwater.md)."}], "references": ["E. Kuichling, The relation between the rainfall and the discharge of sewers in populous districts, Trans. Am. Soc. Civ. Eng. 20 (1889) 1–56.", "ASCE, WEF, Design and Construction of Urban Stormwater Management Systems, ASCE Manuals and Reports of Engineering Practice No. 77, WEF Manual of Practice FD-20, ASCE, New York, 1992.", "L.A. Rossman, Storm Water Management Model User's Manual, Version 5.1, EPA/600/R-14/413b, U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, Cincinnati, 2015.", "W.C. Huber, R.E. Dickinson, Storm Water Management Model, Version 4: User's Manual, EPA/600/3-88/001a, U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, Athens, GA, 1988.", "J.F. O'Callaghan, D.M. Mark, The extraction of drainage networks from digital elevation data, Comput. Vis. Graph. Image Process. 28 (1984) 323–344. [doi:10.1016/S0734-189X(84)80011-0](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0734-189X(84)80011-0)", "L. Wang, H. Liu, An efficient method for identifying and filling surface depressions in digital elevation models for hydrologic analysis and modelling, Int. J. Geogr. Inf. Sci. 20 (2) (2006) 193–213. [doi:10.1080/13658810500433453](https://doi.org/10.1080/13658810500433453)", "R. Barnes, C. Lehman, D. Mulla, Priority-flood: an optimal depression-filling and watershed-labeling algorithm for digital elevation models, Comput. Geosci. 62 (2014) 117–127. [doi:10.1016/j.cageo.2013.04.024](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cageo.2013.04.024)", "V.T. Chow, D.R. Maidment, L.W. Mays, Applied Hydrology, McGraw-Hill, New York, 1988.", "FLL, Green Roof Guidelines: Guidelines for the Planning, Construction and Maintenance of Green Roofs, Forschungsgesellschaft Landschaftsentwicklung Landschaftsbau, Bonn, 2018.", "S. Perica, D. Martin, S. Pavlovic, I. Roy, M. St. Laurent, C. Trypaluk, D. Unruh, M. Yekta, G. Bonnin, NOAA Atlas 14: Precipitation-Frequency Atlas of the United States, Volume 8, Version 2.0: Midwestern States, NOAA National Weather Service, Silver Spring, 2013.", "USDA Natural Resources Conservation Service, National Engineering Handbook, Part 630 Hydrology, chapter 10, Estimation of direct runoff from storm rainfall, USDA NRCS, Washington, DC, 2004.", "USDA Natural Resources Conservation Service, Urban Hydrology for Small Watersheds, Technical Release 55 (TR-55), 2nd ed., 210-VI-TR-55, Washington, DC, June 1986, chapter 2 (equations 2-1 to 2-4, table 2-1, table 2-2a, figure 2-3)."], "category": "research", "simulation": "stormwater", "summary": "How the Stormwater study estimates where the rain of one design storm runs over the ground and where it can collect, with rational-method runoff coefficients, priority-flood depression filling and D8 flow routing. The report gives the sources of each step, reports a test that found ponded volumes underestimated when a hollow drained through more than one flow branch (since corrected), and explains why the storm is not tied to local rainfall statistics.", "image": "img/home/stormwater.webp", "imageAlt": "Flow paths and ponds on a sloping site after a design storm"}}, {"id": "report-watersound", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Screening the sound of water features and streams: sound power from falling water and simple outdoor propagation", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Water sound", "Soundscape", "Sound power", "Outdoor sound propagation", "Noise mapping"], "highlights": ["A fall's sound power is an acoustic efficiency (3 × 10⁻⁷, 10⁻⁶ or 3 × 10⁻⁶) times the power of the falling water, ρ g Q h; the efficiency is an assumption.", "Fountain and rill presets are octave-band sound powers, A-weighted with the IEC 61672 weights; the presets' source is not named.", "Propagation uses hemispherical spreading (−20 log r − 8 dB) and 5 dB/km air absorption, consistent with ISO 9613.", "Screening (−10 dB behind a building, −8 dB behind terrain) and window insulation (−10 dB open, −25 dB shut) are rules of thumb.", "The study reports levels, not annoyance; water sound is often perceived as pleasant."], "abstract": "Water features are used in landscape design to mask traffic noise and to improve the perceived soundscape, and streams and falls on a site can also be loud enough to matter at bedroom windows [1–3]. SimuSite's Water sound study estimates A-weighted sound levels from the site's water: each waterfall is a point source whose sound power is a fixed fraction of the mechanical power of the falling water, running stream reaches are weaker point sources every 8 m, and fountains and rills are given octave-band sound power spectra. Levels are summed on a grid at 1.5 m, on facade squares and at the outside of each room's windows, with hemispherical spreading, air absorption and simple screening by buildings and terrain, and are compared with the WHO 1999 guideline values for indoor noise [4]. This report documents the model and its constants. The propagation terms and the A-weighting are standard [5, 6]; the source strengths (acoustic efficiency, the riffle formula and the fountain and rill presets) are assumptions with no traceable source, and the screening and indoor reductions are rules of thumb. Results compare places and options; they should not be read as predicted measurements.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nEnvironmental noise is predicted by combining source sound power levels with propagation models such as ISO 9613-2, which accounts for geometric divergence, atmospheric absorption, ground effect and screening [5]. For road, rail and industry, source power databases exist. For water, they do not: the sound of a fall depends on its flow, height, the shape of the lip, the receiving pool and the presence of rocks, and measured levels vary widely [1, 2]. Listening studies show that water sounds with energy at low and mid frequencies (streams, cascades) are preferred, and that a water sound a few decibels below a traffic noise can improve the perceived soundscape [2, 3]. For a designer, the practical questions are how loud a feature will be at a seat, a window or a bedroom, and whether a stream will disturb sleep.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Sources\n\n**Falls.** For each fall of drop h on a water body of discharge Q, the mechanical power is P = ρ g Q h (W), with ρ = 1000 kg/m³. The sound power level is L_W = 10 log₁₀(η P / 10⁻¹² W), with η = 3 × 10⁻⁷ (gentle), 10⁻⁶ (typical) or 3 × 10⁻⁶ (loud), and not less than 70 dB. The source sits halfway down the drop, 0.6 m downstream of the lip. For Q = 0.05 m³/s and h = 2 m, P = 981 W and L_W is 85, 90 or 95 dB.\n\n**Running water.** For a stream with a surface speed v of at least 0.3 m/s, a point source every 8 m along its centre line has L_W = 66 + 10 log₁₀ Q + 10 log₁₀(v/0.5) dB(A), with Q ≥ 0.02 m³/s and v ≥ 0.3 m/s.\n\n**Features.** A fountain is one point source at its centre; a rill or cascade is a line of points every 4 m along the long axis, sharing its power. Their octave-band sound power levels (125 Hz to 4 kHz) are editable; the presets are 50, 55, 50, 52, 55, 52 dB for a fountain (59.4 dB(A)) and 70 dB in every band for a rill (76.3 dB(A)). Bands are combined with the A-weighting corrections −16.1, −8.6, −3.2, 0, +1.2 and +1.0 dB of IEC 61672-1 [6].\n\n### 2.2. Propagation\n\nThe level at a receiver from each source is L_p = L_W − 20 log₁₀ r − 8 − 0.005 r − D_s, with r ≥ 1 m the distance in metres. The term −20 log r − 8 is hemispherical spreading from a point source on a reflecting ground (−20 log r − 11 for free field, +3 dB for the ground) [5, 7]. The 0.005 dB/m air absorption corresponds to about 5 dB/km, the ISO 9613-1 value near 1 kHz at 20 °C and 70 % relative humidity [8]. D_s is 10 dB if a building lies on the straight line between source and receiver, plus 8 dB if the terrain rises above it by more than 0.3 m. On a facade, sources behind the facade plane count 8 dB less. Levels from all sources are added on an energy basis.\n\n### 2.3. Receivers and outputs\n\nThe study computes levels on the ground grid at 1.5 m, on facade squares 0.3 m out, and at a point 0.4 m outside each exterior window at 1.5 m above its floor. For each room it reports the loudest window level and indoor estimates of 10 dB less with windows open and 25 dB less with them shut. It also reports the mean ground level, the share of ground at 55 dB(A) or more, the source list, and, for reference, the WHO 1999 guideline values of 35 dB(A) in living rooms by day and 30 dB(A) in bedrooms at night for continuous noise [4].\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Inputs and constants of the Water sound study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fall acoustic efficiency η | 3 × 10⁻⁷ / 10⁻⁶ / 3 × 10⁻⁶ | Engine choice, labelled as an assumption | Flagged: no measured basis cited |\n| Minimum fall L_W | 70 dB | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Riffle L_W | 66 + 10 log Q + 10 log(v/0.5) | Not traced | Flagged: no source |\n| Fountain / rill presets | Octave bands as in §2.1 | \"A published example\" (unnamed in the code) | Flagged: source should be cited or the presets relabelled |\n| A-weighting | −16.1 … +1.2 dB | IEC 61672-1 [6] | Sourced |\n| Spreading | −20 log r − 8 dB | Hemispherical point source [5, 7] | Sourced |\n| Air absorption | 5 dB/km | ISO 9613-1, ≈ 1 kHz, 20 °C, 70 % RH [8] | Sourced (single band) |\n| Building screening | 10 dB | Rule of thumb | Within the 5–20 dB range of thin barriers [5, 7]; not computed from path difference |\n| Terrain screening | 8 dB | Rule of thumb | Assumption |\n| Facade back-side | −8 dB | Rule of thumb | Assumption |\n| Indoor, windows open / shut | −10 / −25 dB | Rule of thumb | WHO 1999 uses about 15 dB for a partly open window [4]; shut depends on the glazing |\n| Reference levels | 35 dB(A) living, 30 dB(A) bedroom | WHO 1999 [4] | Sourced |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\n`tests/workspace-water.cjs` builds a project with a stream and a fall and checks that the study finds one fall, reports at least six rooms, and gives a loudest window level between 50 and 95 dB(A). The spreading, absorption and A-weighting terms were checked by hand for this report; the fountain and rill presets give 59.4 and 76.3 dB(A), as computed above. No comparison with measured water sound has been made. Measured spectra of fountains and streams in the listening studies of Galbrun and Ali [2] and of Rådsten-Ekman and colleagues [3] could serve to check the presets.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 the audit site had no water, and the study stopped with the message \"No water on the site: draw a water area (Site › Water) or map one first.\", which is the intended behaviour. We then ran `tests/workspace-water.cjs`, which models a stream with a 5.08 m fall at 1.2 m³/s beside a house. The study reported a fall sound power of 108 dB (hand check: 10 log₁₀(10⁻⁶ × 1000 × 9.81 × 1.2 × 5.08 / 10⁻¹²) = 107.8 dB), seven stretches of running water, a mean of 47 dB(A) on the ground, 21 % of the ground at 55 dB(A) or more, and 82 dB(A) at the loudest window. A level of 82 dB(A) at a window is very high; it follows directly from the assumed efficiency and the short distance, and illustrates why the source strength should be treated as uncertain by ±5 dB or more.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Source strength dominates.** A factor of ten in η changes every level by 10 dB, and the three presets span exactly that range. With no measured efficiency for falls of the kind designers draw, the absolute levels are uncertain by at least ±5 dB, and probably more. Relative results, such as which room is loudest or how much a building screens a terrace, are more robust because they depend on geometry.\n\n**Propagation.** The model omits ground absorption (which reduces levels over soft ground at a distance), reflections from facades (which add up to 3 dB in front of a wall), and the frequency dependence of screening and air absorption [5]. Screening is a fixed value rather than a function of the path-length difference over the barrier [5, 7]. These simplifications are acceptable for a screening over short distances, which is where water sound matters.\n\n**Perception.** Sound level is not annoyance. Water sound is usually rated pleasant, and it can mask unwanted noise [1–3]. Conversely, a steady sound of 35–40 dB(A) at a bedroom window with the window open can still disturb sleep for some people. The WHO values are given for reference, and the study states that it reports levels, not annoyance.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Water sound study's propagation terms, A-weighting and reference levels are correctly sourced. Its source strengths are assumptions, as the study itself states for the fall efficiency; the riffle formula and the fountain and rill presets need a cited source or should be labelled as assumptions in the same way. The study is suited to comparing layouts and finding the rooms most exposed to a water feature, not to predicting measured levels.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js), [emb/lib/water.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/water.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-water.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-water.cjs). This article's source: [watersound.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/watersound.md)."}], "references": ["J. Kang, Urban Sound Environment, Taylor & Francis, London, 2007.", "L. Galbrun, T.T. Ali, Acoustical and perceptual assessment of water sounds and their use over road traffic noise, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 133 (1) (2013) 227–237. [doi:10.1121/1.4770242](https://doi.org/10.1121/1.4770242)", "M. Rådsten-Ekman, Ö. Axelsson, M.E. Nilsson, Effects of sounds from water on perception of acoustic environments dominated by road-traffic noise, Acta Acust. united Acust. 99 (2) (2013) 218–225.", "B. Berglund, T. Lindvall, D.H. Schwela (Eds.), Guidelines for Community Noise, World Health Organization, Geneva, 1999.", "ISO 9613-2:1996, Acoustics — Attenuation of sound during propagation outdoors — Part 2: General method of calculation, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 1996.", "IEC 61672-1:2013, Electroacoustics — Sound level meters — Part 1: Specifications, International Electrotechnical Commission, Geneva, 2013.", "D.A. Bies, C.H. Hansen, C.Q. Howard, Engineering Noise Control, 5th ed., CRC Press, Boca Raton, 2017.", "ISO 9613-1:1993, Acoustics — Attenuation of sound during propagation outdoors — Part 1: Calculation of the absorption of sound by the atmosphere, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 1993."], "category": "research", "simulation": "watersound", "summary": "How the Water sound study estimates A-weighted levels on the ground, on the facades and at windows from waterfalls, running streams, fountains and rills. The report separates the parts with a firm basis (spreading, air absorption, A-weighting, WHO reference levels) from the source strengths and screening values, which are assumptions.", "image": "img/home/acoustics.webp", "imageAlt": "Sound levels around a stream and a waterfall, in 5 dB bands"}}, {"id": "report-acoustics", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Reverberation time and speech clarity of every room from statistical room acoustics and the DIN 18041 targets", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Room acoustics", "Reverberation time", "Sabine and Eyring formulas", "Speech transmission index", "DIN 18041"], "highlights": ["Reverberation time in six octave bands (125 Hz–4 kHz) by Eyring (default) or Sabine, with air absorption from ISO 9613-1.", "Targets are the DIN 18041:2016 formulas for groups A1–A4 with a ±20 % band; homes and offices use 0.5 s, a rule of thumb.", "Against an independent ray tracer, Eyring is within −7 % to −1 % for rooms with even absorption; with all absorption on the ceiling it is 11.5 % short.", "Air absorption matches all 36 ISO 9613-1 table values within tolerance.", "Absorption of finishes, furniture and people are typical published values; the STI estimate uses the 1985 weights and ignores background noise."], "abstract": "Reverberation time is the primary design quantity in room acoustics: it governs speech intelligibility in classrooms and meeting rooms, the quality of music, and the general sense of a room being quiet or noisy [1–3]. National standards set targets that depend on use and volume; DIN 18041:2016 is the most widely used for rooms other than auditoria [4]. SimuSite's Indoor acoustics study computes, for every room of the building model, the reverberation time in six octave bands from the room volume and the absorption of its surfaces, furniture, people and the air, using the Eyring or Sabine formula [1, 5, 6]. It compares the mean of the 500 Hz and 1 kHz values with the DIN 18041 target for the room's use, estimates the speech transmission index (STI) from reverberation alone [7, 8], and suggests the area of acoustic panel that would bring a live room to target. This report documents the method, the data and their sources. Air absorption matches ISO 9613-1 [9] at all 36 tabulated points, and Eyring's formula agrees with an independent stochastic ray tracer within −7 % to −1 % for rooms with evenly distributed absorption. The DIN 18041 formulas are implemented correctly. Absorption coefficients are typical textbook values not tied to products, the target for homes and offices is a rule of thumb, and the STI estimate uses the original 1985 weighting and neglects background noise.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nSabine's formula T = 0.161 V / A relates the reverberation time T to the room volume V and the total equivalent absorption area A [5]. Eyring's correction replaces A by −S ln(1 − ᾱ), which is more accurate when the mean absorption coefficient ᾱ is large [6]. Both assume a diffuse sound field. They are the basis of design calculations worldwide and of the targets in standards, and they are known to underestimate the reverberation of rooms whose absorption is concentrated on one surface [1, 2, 10].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Room model\n\nThe study takes each room on each level from the building model: its volume, and the area of each wall, floor, ceiling, window, door and opening to a neighbouring space, with its finish. Finishes have absorption coefficients in six octave bands from 125 Hz to 4 kHz (Table 2). Furniture adds an equivalent absorption area by type (sofa, armchair, bed, upholstered chair, filled bookshelf, rug, or \"hard furniture\"). People can be added at a chosen floor area per person, each with an equivalent absorption area of 0.15–0.45 m² by band. An opening to the next space counts as fully absorbing (α = 1).\n\n### 2.2. Reverberation time\n\nFor each band, A = Σ S_i α_i + A_furniture + A_people, and the air term is 4mV, where m is the power attenuation coefficient of air from ISO 9613-1:1993 equations 3–5 at the chosen temperature and humidity (defaults 20 °C, 50 %) [9]. The speed of sound is c = 331.3 √(1 + t/273.15). The reverberation times are\n\nT_Sabine = (24 ln 10 / c) V / (A + 4mV),   T_Eyring = (24 ln 10 / c) V / (−S ln(1 − ᾱ) + 4mV),\n\nwith ᾱ = A/S capped at 0.99 [1, 5, 6].\n\n### 2.3. Targets\n\nThe study takes the mean of the 500 Hz and 1 kHz values and compares it with a target for the room's use, assigned from the room name or chosen by the user. For DIN 18041:2016 groups, T_target = 0.45 lg V + 0.07 for A1 music (30–1000 m³), 0.37 lg V − 0.14 for A2 speech and lectures (50–5000 m³), 0.32 lg V − 0.17 for A3 teaching and communication (30–5000 m³) and 0.26 lg V − 0.14 for A4 inclusive teaching (30–500 m³) [4]. Homes and offices use 0.5 s; circulation, storage and wet rooms have no target. A room is on target within ±20 % of the target, which is the tolerance band of DIN 18041 at mid frequencies [4].\n\n### 2.4. Speech transmission index\n\nThe modulation transfer function of a diffuse reverberant field is m(F) = [1 + (2πF T/13.8)²]^(−1/2) [7]. For 14 modulation frequencies from 0.63 to 12.5 Hz and seven octave bands from 125 Hz to 8 kHz (the 8 kHz band taking the 4 kHz reverberation time), m is converted to an apparent signal-to-noise ratio, clipped to ±15 dB, mapped to a transmission index and combined with octave weights 0.13, 0.14, 0.11, 0.12, 0.19, 0.17 and 0.14 [7]. The result is rated excellent (≥ 0.75), good (≥ 0.60), fair (≥ 0.45), poor (≥ 0.30) or bad, the IEC 60268-16 qualification bands [8].\n\n### 2.5. Remedy\n\nFor a room that is too live, the extra absorption needed to reach the target by Sabine is A_need − A_now, and the panel area is that divided by 0.95, the difference between a 50 mm mineral-wool panel (α ≈ 0.975 at 500 Hz–1 kHz) and the plaster it replaces.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Constants and data of the Indoor acoustics study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sabine, Eyring formulas | As in §2.2 | Sabine [5]; Eyring [6]; Kuttruff [1] | Sourced |\n| Air absorption | ISO 9613-1 eqs. 3–5 | ISO 9613-1:1993 [9] | Sourced and verified (36/36) |\n| Speed of sound | 331.3 √(1 + t/273.15) | Standard | Sourced |\n| DIN 18041 targets | A1–A4 formulas and volume ranges | DIN 18041:2016 [4] | Sourced and verified |\n| Tolerance | ±20 % | DIN 18041 tolerance band [4] | Sourced (mid frequencies only) |\n| Home and office target | 0.5 s | Rule of thumb | Flagged: DIN 18041 does not cover dwellings; offices fall under group B or A3 |\n| Finish absorption | Table 2 | Typical published values [2, 11] | Plausible, no source in the code, not product data |\n| Furniture absorption | Sofa 0.95 m² at 500 Hz, bed 0.80, armchair 0.35 … | \"Typical values for early design\" | Flagged: no source |\n| Person absorption | 0.15, 0.25, 0.35, 0.42, 0.45, 0.45 m² | Engine choice | Lower end of the 0.4–0.6 m² usually given for a seated adult at mid frequencies [1, 2] |\n| STI MTF | Diffuse-field formula | Houtgast and Steeneken [7] | Sourced |\n| STI octave weights | 0.13 … 0.14 | Houtgast and Steeneken 1985 [7] | Flagged: IEC 60268-16:2020 uses different weights and redundancy factors [8] |\n| Background noise in STI | None | Engine choice | Flagged: STI is optimistic in noisy rooms |\n| Panel α | ≈ 0.975 at 500 Hz–1 kHz | Table 2 | Typical for 50 mm mineral wool [2] |\n\nTable 2. Absorption coefficients of the main finishes (125 Hz–4 kHz).\n\n| Finish | 125 | 250 | 500 | 1k | 2k | 4k |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Plaster on masonry, painted | 0.01 | 0.02 | 0.02 | 0.03 | 0.04 | 0.05 |\n| Gypsum board on studs | 0.29 | 0.10 | 0.05 | 0.04 | 0.07 | 0.09 |\n| Concrete, painted | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.02 | 0.02 | 0.02 | 0.02 |\n| Timber floor on joists | 0.15 | 0.11 | 0.10 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 0.07 |\n| Carpet on concrete | 0.02 | 0.06 | 0.14 | 0.37 | 0.60 | 0.65 |\n| Suspended mineral-fibre ceiling | 0.50 | 0.55 | 0.65 | 0.80 | 0.85 | 0.80 |\n| Acoustic panel, 50 mm mineral wool | 0.20 | 0.55 | 0.95 | 1.00 | 0.95 | 0.90 |\n| Double glazing | 0.10 | 0.07 | 0.05 | 0.03 | 0.02 | 0.02 |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe validation script `tests/validation/room_acoustics.cjs` extracts the engine's functions as they ship and checks them against two references (documented in `mvp/VALIDATION.md`):\n\n- **Air absorption** against the ISO 9613-1 table values for 6 conditions and 6 bands: all 36 within the 3 % or 0.06 dB/km tolerance; the largest difference is +0.7 % at 4 kHz, 15 °C, 20 % RH.\n- **Reverberation time** against an independent stochastic ray tracer (3,000 rays, Lambert-diffuse reflections, air absorption, T30 fitted from −5 to −35 dB). For five rooms with even absorption, Eyring is within −7.0 % to −1.2 % and Sabine within +1.3 % to +10.4 % (Table 3). For an office with an absorbing ceiling (α 0.80) over hard walls and floor (α 0.03), the ray tracer gives 0.39 s, Eyring 0.43 s (+11.5 %) and Sabine 0.49 s (+26 %).\n- **STI**: 1 with no reverberation, 0.74 at 0.5 s, 0.59 at 1 s and 0.44 at 2 s, falling monotonically.\n- **DIN 18041** formulas and volume ranges.\n\nTable 3. Reverberation time against ray tracing (from `mvp/VALIDATION.md`).\n\n| Room (even absorption) | Ray tracing | Eyring | Sabine |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bedroom 4 × 3.5 × 2.7 m, α 0.10, 500 Hz | 0.85 s | 0.83 s | 0.88 s |\n| Office 5 × 4 × 2.7 m, α 0.20, 1 kHz | 0.46 s | 0.43 s | 0.48 s |\n| Classroom 9 × 7 × 3 m, α 0.30, 1 kHz | 0.41 s | 0.38 s | 0.45 s |\n| Hall 20 × 15 × 6 m, α 0.15, 4 kHz | 1.39 s | 1.35 s | 1.43 s |\n| Studio 6 × 6 × 3 m, α 0.05, 500 Hz | 2.30 s | 2.27 s | 2.33 s |\n\nThe Workspace test `tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs` checks that a bare plaster room is too live, that a panelled meeting room is shorter, that a fix is suggested, and that Sabine gives longer times than Eyring.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on a two-storey block with plaster walls, timber floors, plaster ceilings and sparse furniture, 1 of 8 judged rooms was on target; bedrooms and offices had about 2.5 s against 0.5 s, with an STI of about 0.42–0.43, and about 18 m² of acoustic panel was suggested for each. These are the expected results for nearly bare plaster rooms: with ᾱ ≈ 0.03, Sabine gives T ≈ 0.161 V / (0.03 S), about 2 s for a 3 m room.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Diffuse-field limits.** The ray-tracing comparison favours the formulas because its walls scatter diffusely. In real rooms with smooth parallel walls and all the absorption on the ceiling, the grazing sound field between the walls decays slowly, and measured reverberation can greatly exceed both formulas [1, 10]. The study's \"on target\" for such a room should be checked by an acoustician. Below about 200 Hz in small rooms, modes dominate and the 125 Hz value is indicative only [1].\n\n**Data.** The coefficients in Table 2 are of the magnitude found in standard tables [2, 11], but the code gives no source, and products differ. Absorption of furniture and people is uncertain by at least ±30 %. DIN 18041 targets are for occupied rooms; with the default of no people, the comparison is conservative.\n\n**STI.** The 1985 weights differ from the current IEC 60268-16:2020 male-speech weights and the standard adds redundancy corrections between adjacent bands [8]. Neglecting background noise makes the estimate optimistic, while neglecting direct sound makes it pessimistic near the talker. The value is an indicator of how reverberation alone limits clarity.\n\n**Home and office target.** DIN 18041 does not apply to dwellings, and office targets are usually set by group B rules or by standards such as DIN EN ISO 3382-3 for open-plan offices [4, 12]. A value of 0.5 s is a reasonable comfort target for a furnished living room or a small office, but it is a rule of thumb.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Indoor acoustics study implements the published formulas for reverberation time, air absorption and DIN 18041 targets correctly, and is validated against ISO tables and an independent ray tracer for rooms with diffuse fields. The absorption data are typical values without cited sources, the home and office target is a rule of thumb, and the STI is an estimate from reverberation alone. The results are suitable for early design of finishes; rooms with concentrated absorption or critical use need a specialist assessment.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/acoustics.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/acoustics.js), [emb/roomacoustics.html](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/roomacoustics.html), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/validation/room_acoustics.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/validation/room_acoustics.cjs), [tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-vent-acoustics.cjs), [tests/acoustics.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/acoustics.cjs). This article's source: [acoustics.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/acoustics.md)."}], "references": ["H. Kuttruff, Room Acoustics, 6th ed., CRC Press, Boca Raton, 2016.", "M. Long, Architectural Acoustics, 2nd ed., Academic Press, Oxford, 2014.", "M. Vorländer, Auralization: Fundamentals of Acoustics, Modelling, Simulation, Algorithms and Acoustic Virtual Reality, Springer, Berlin, 2008.", "DIN 18041:2016-03, Hörsamkeit in Räumen — Anforderungen, Empfehlungen und Hinweise für die Planung (Acoustic quality in rooms — specifications and instructions for room acoustic design), Beuth, Berlin, 2016.", "W.C. Sabine, Collected Papers on Acoustics, Harvard University Press, Cambridge, MA, 1922.", "C.F. Eyring, Reverberation time in \"dead\" rooms, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 1 (2A) (1930) 217–241. [doi:10.1121/1.1915175](https://doi.org/10.1121/1.1915175)", "T. Houtgast, H.J.M. Steeneken, A review of the MTF concept in room acoustics and its use for estimating speech intelligibility in auditoria, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 77 (3) (1985) 1069–1077. [doi:10.1121/1.392224](https://doi.org/10.1121/1.392224)", "IEC 60268-16:2020, Sound system equipment — Part 16: Objective rating of speech intelligibility by speech transmission index, International Electrotechnical Commission, Geneva, 2020.", "ISO 9613-1:1993, Acoustics — Attenuation of sound during propagation outdoors — Part 1: Calculation of the absorption of sound by the atmosphere, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 1993.", "D. Fitzroy, Reverberation formula which seems to be more accurate with nonuniform distribution of absorption, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 31 (7) (1959) 893–897.", "ISO 354:2003, Acoustics — Measurement of sound absorption in a reverberation room, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 2003.", "ISO 3382-3:2022, Acoustics — Measurement of room acoustic parameters — Part 3: Open plan offices, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 2022."], "category": "research", "simulation": "acoustics", "summary": "How the Indoor acoustics study computes each room's reverberation time in six octave bands with the Sabine and Eyring formulas, air absorption from ISO 9613-1, a reverberation-only speech transmission index, and targets from DIN 18041. The report gives the sources of the absorption data and targets, summarises the validation against ISO tables and an independent ray tracer, and flags the values that are rules of thumb.", "image": "img/home/acoustics.webp", "imageAlt": "Rooms coloured by reverberation time against their target"}}, {"id": "report-spacesyntax", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Visibility graph analysis of floor plans: integration, connectivity and intelligibility after Turner and DepthmapX", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Space syntax", "Visibility graph analysis", "Visual integration", "Intelligibility", "DepthmapX"], "highlights": ["Nodes on a 0.2–1.5 m grid are joined where they see each other at eye height (1.6 m) or knee height (0.5 m).", "Integration [HH] is the reciprocal of relative asymmetry normalised by the Hillier and Hanson diamond D-value; R3 uses depths up to three.", "Intelligibility is the R² of connectivity against global integration.", "The formulas match the published definitions and the DepthmapX conventions; there is no numerical comparison with DepthmapX yet.", "Configurational measures correlate with movement and wayfinding in many studies but do not predict occupant numbers."], "abstract": "Space syntax describes the configuration of buildings and cities by the relations of each space to all others, and has repeatedly been found to correlate with pedestrian movement, wayfinding and co-presence [1–4]. Visibility graph analysis (VGA), introduced by Turner and colleagues, applies the method to a fine grid of points in a plan, joining points that are mutually visible [2]. SimuSite's Space syntax study performs VGA on each floor of the building model: a grid of nodes inside the walls, joined where a straight sightline at eye or knee height is unobstructed, followed by a breadth-first search from every node. It reports visual integration [HH] (Hillier and Hanson's normalisation), local integration within three steps (R3), connectivity and intelligibility, and runs agents that walk towards what they see, after Turner and Penn [5]. This report documents the graph construction and formulas and their sources. The integration and D-value formulas are those published by Hillier and Hanson and used in DepthmapX [1, 6]; the implementation has not yet been compared numerically with DepthmapX on a reference plan. Results describe the spatial structure of a plan; their relation to use is statistical and depends on programme and occupants.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nIn The Social Logic of Space, Hillier and Hanson represented buildings as graphs of convex spaces and measured each space's mean depth from all others, normalised so that buildings of different size can be compared [1]. Benedikt's isovists, the region visible from a point, provided a complementary description of space as experienced [7]. Turner and colleagues combined the two: in a visibility graph, every point of a fine grid is a node, two nodes are joined if each is in the other's isovist, and the graph measures of space syntax are computed on it [2]. VGA avoids the subjective step of drawing convex spaces or axial lines and is implemented in the open-source DepthmapX software [6]. In buildings, visual integration has been found to correlate with observed movement and with where people gather, and the correlation between local and global measures, intelligibility, with the ease of wayfinding [3, 4, 8].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Floor and obstacles\n\nThe plan of one level is rasterised. The occupied floor is everything inside the walls, with doorways up to about 1.1 m bridged, minus furniture footprints. At eye height (1.6 m), walls and tall furniture block sight and low furniture is seen over; at knee height (0.5 m), all furniture blocks, which approximates what can be walked across. Glass can be set to be seen through.\n\n### 2.2. Graph\n\nNodes are placed on a square grid over the floor. The spacing and the number of sight rays are chosen from a list for each detail level (draft 0.6–1.5 m, standard 0.3–0.75 m, fine 0.2–0.5 m, with 240–720 rays) as the finest that fits a time budget on the user's computer. From each node, the free distance along each of the n_r bearings is found by ray casting against the obstacles. Nodes a and b are joined if b lies within the free distance of a along the bearing towards b and vice versa.\n\n### 2.3. Measures\n\nConnectivity is the number of nodes joined to a node. A breadth-first search from each node s gives the visual depth d(s, t) to every reachable node t, the number k of nodes reached and the mean depth MD = Σ d / (k − 1). Following Hillier and Hanson [1]:\n\n- Relative asymmetry RA = 2 (MD − 1) / (k − 2).\n- The diamond D-value D_k = 2 [k (log₂((k + 2)/3) − 1) + 1] / [(k − 1)(k − 2)].\n- Real relative asymmetry RRA = RA / D_k, and integration [HH] = 1 / RRA.\n\nIntegration R3 applies the same formulas to the nodes within three steps, with k the count of those nodes, as DepthmapX does for radius-limited integration [6]. Intelligibility is the squared Pearson correlation between connectivity and global integration across all nodes [1, 8]. The study also reports visual steps from any chosen spot.\n\n### 2.4. Agents\n\nTwo hundred agents start at random nodes and repeatedly choose a destination at random among the nodes they can see within a 170° field of view, walk three grid steps towards it and choose again, for 1,500 steps, after Turner and Penn's agent model in DepthmapX [5, 6]. The count of agents entering each node (gate count) is reported. A deterministic random seed makes runs repeatable.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings and formulas of the Space syntax study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Graph | Mutual visibility on a grid | Turner et al. 2001 [2] | Sourced |\n| Eye / knee height | 1.6 m / 0.5 m | DepthmapX practice of \"see\" and \"move\" graphs [2, 6] | Sourced convention |\n| Node spacing | 0.2–1.5 m by detail and time budget | Engine choice | Numerical setting; results depend on spacing |\n| Sight rays per node | 240–720 | Engine choice | Numerical setting; at 720 rays a 1° sector, so nodes far away can be missed or added |\n| RA, D-value, RRA, integration | As in §2.3 | Hillier and Hanson [1] | Sourced and checked against the published formula |\n| Local integration | Radius 3 | DepthmapX convention [6] | Sourced |\n| Intelligibility | R² of connectivity vs integration | Hillier and Hanson [1]; Hillier [8] | Sourced |\n| Agents | 200 agents, 170° view, 3 steps, 1,500 steps | Turner and Penn [5]; DepthmapX defaults [6] | Sourced convention |\n| Doorway bridging | ≈ 1.1 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace tests check that the study runs from the menu, that its views render and that the agents move. The formulas were checked line by line against Hillier and Hanson for this report [1]. A numerical comparison with DepthmapX on a reference plan, which would show the effect of the ray discretisation on connectivity and integration, has not been done and is the obvious next step.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on level 1 of the audit block (231 m² of floor, nodes every 60 cm), the study built a graph of 644 nodes and reported a mean visual integration of 5.18, an intelligibility R² of 0.73, the most integrated tenth at 8.19 and above, and 2,122 agent crossings at the busiest node.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**What the measures mean.** Integration measures how few visual steps separate a point from all others; well-integrated spaces are those from which the rest of the plan is easily seen or reached. Studies of urban areas and of buildings such as museums and offices have found that integration explains a substantial part of the variance in observed movement where movement is not driven by strong attractors [3, 4, 9]. Where function dominates, for example a lift lobby or a reception desk, the correlation weakens. Integration therefore describes a plan's potential for movement and visibility, not a forecast of occupancy.\n\n**Normalisation.** Integration [HH] is designed to compare plans of different size, but its values still depend on the grid spacing because k and mean depth do. Values should be compared only between plans analysed at the same spacing and height. The study reports the spacing in its results so that this can be checked.\n\n**Discretisation.** Mutual visibility is tested against a polar ray map with a finite number of rays; at long range a 1° sector is wide, so a distant node just past a door jamb can be counted as visible or not. DepthmapX tests visibility exactly against the geometry [6]. The effect is mostly on connectivity of nodes in large open spaces.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Space syntax study implements visibility graph analysis and the Hillier and Hanson measures as published, with DepthmapX conventions for heights, radius and agents. It has not yet been compared numerically with DepthmapX. Its results are appropriate for comparing plan options by their spatial structure, with the understanding that configuration influences, but does not determine, how a building is used.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/spacesyntax.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/spacesyntax.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-motion2.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-motion2.cjs), [tests/workspace-p0.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-p0.cjs), [tests/workspace-interior.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-interior.cjs). This article's source: [spacesyntax.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/spacesyntax.md)."}], "references": ["B. Hillier, J. Hanson, The Social Logic of Space, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1984.", "A. Turner, M. Doxa, D. O'Sullivan, A. Penn, From isovists to visibility graphs: a methodology for the analysis of architectural space, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 28 (1) (2001) 103–121. [doi:10.1068/b2684](https://doi.org/10.1068/b2684)", "B. Hillier, A. Penn, J. Hanson, T. Grajewski, J. Xu, Natural movement: or, configuration and attraction in urban pedestrian movement, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 20 (1) (1993) 29–66. [doi:10.1068/b200029](https://doi.org/10.1068/b200029)", "A. Penn, Space syntax and spatial cognition: or why the axial line?, Environ. Behav. 35 (1) (2003) 30–65. [doi:10.1177/0013916502238864](https://doi.org/10.1177/0013916502238864)", "A. Turner, A. Penn, Encoding natural movement as an agent-based system: an investigation into human pedestrian behaviour in the built environment, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 29 (4) (2002) 473–490. [doi:10.1068/b12850](https://doi.org/10.1068/b12850)", "T. Varoudis, depthmapX: multi-platform spatial network analysis software, Space Syntax Laboratory, University College London, 2012–. https://github.com/SpaceGroupUCL/depthmapX", "M.L. Benedikt, To take hold of space: isovists and isovist fields, Environ. Plann. B 6 (1) (1979) 47–65. [doi:10.1068/b060047](https://doi.org/10.1068/b060047)", "B. Hillier, Space is the Machine: A Configurational Theory of Architecture, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1996.", "A. Turner, Analysing the visual dynamics of spatial morphology, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 30 (5) (2003) 657–676. [doi:10.1068/b12962](https://doi.org/10.1068/b12962)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "spacesyntax", "summary": "How the Space syntax study builds a visibility graph over each floor and derives visual integration, local integration (R3), connectivity and intelligibility, following Turner, Doxa, O'Sullivan and Penn (2001) and the conventions of DepthmapX. The report gives the formulas and their sources, describes the resolution settings, and explains what configurational measures can and cannot predict.", "image": "img/home/spacesyntax.webp", "imageAlt": "Floor plan coloured from blue (segregated) to red (integrated) by visual integration"}}, {"id": "report-movementdepth", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Movement and depth: combining agent gate counts with visual integration on a visibility graph", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 20 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Agent-based model", "Gate counts", "Visibility graph analysis", "Natural movement", "Space syntax"], "highlights": ["The study runs on the same visibility graph as the Space syntax study, with 200 agents and 1,500 steps.", "Agents choose a visible destination within a 170° field of view and walk three steps before choosing again, after Turner and Penn (2002).", "The index is 0.65 √(normalised gate count) + 0.35 × normalised integration, each normalised between its 1st and 99th percentiles.", "The weighting and scaling are SimuSite's choices with no published source; the two components are sourced.", "The map ranks places within one plan; it is not a pedestrian count forecast."], "abstract": "Space syntax research distinguishes \"natural movement\", the part of pedestrian flow that is generated by the configuration of space itself, from movement drawn by attractors [1, 2]. Turner and Penn showed that simple agents that walk towards randomly chosen visible points, on a visibility graph, produce gate counts that correlate with observed movement in a gallery about as well as visual integration does [3]. SimuSite's Movement + depth study runs such agents on the visibility graph of each floor and combines their crossings with visual integration into one index, shown on a blue–white–pink scale in the manner of DepthmapX [4]. This report documents the agent model and the combination. The agent rules and integration follow the published methods (see the Space syntax report for the graph and integration). The combination, 0.65 times the square root of the normalised gate count plus 0.35 times normalised integration, is a presentation choice of SimuSite with no published basis; it should be read as a ranking of places within a plan.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nHillier and colleagues found that, in urban grids, the integration of street segments explains much of the variation in pedestrian counts, and called this natural movement [1]. Inside buildings, Turner and Penn's exosomatic visual architecture (EVA) agents, which pick destinations among the points they can see, reproduce aggregate movement patterns in the Tate Britain galleries with correlations similar to those of integration [3]. Agent gate counts and integration are therefore two estimates of the same configurational tendency, one from a stochastic walk and one from graph depth.\n\n## 2. Method\n\nThe study builds the visibility graph of the chosen level as described in the Space syntax report (nodes inside the walls, joined where mutually visible at eye or knee height) and computes visual integration [HH] [5]. It then runs 200 agents for 1,500 steps. Each agent starts at a random node with a random heading; it chooses a destination among nodes visible from its current node, at least two grid spacings away and within ±85° of its heading (after 30 failed attempts the field-of-view rule is relaxed), walks three grid steps towards it, and chooses again. Agents that would leave the floor turn around. Each time an agent enters a new node, the node's gate count increases by one [3, 4]. A fixed random seed makes runs repeatable.\n\nThe combined index at node k is\n\nI_k = 0.65 √(clamp((g_k − g₁)/(g₉₉ − g₁), 0, 1)) + 0.35 clamp((i_k − i₁)/(i₉₉ − i₁), 0, 1),\n\nwhere g is the gate count, i the integration, and the subscripts 1 and 99 their 1st and 99th percentiles. The study shows the index, integration and gate counts as separate views, and plays back the walks of the first 48 agents.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Movement + depth study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Visibility graph and integration | As in the Space syntax report | Turner et al. [5]; Hillier and Hanson [6] | Sourced |\n| Field of view | 170° | Turner and Penn [3]; DepthmapX default [4] | Sourced |\n| Steps before a new choice | 3 | Turner and Penn [3]; DepthmapX default [4] | Sourced |\n| Agents, steps | 200, 1,500 | Engine choice | Numerical setting; gate counts are noisy with few agents |\n| Minimum destination distance | 2 grid spacings | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Weights | 0.65 gate, 0.35 integration | Engine choice | Flagged: no published basis |\n| Gate scaling | Square root | Engine choice | Flagged: compresses high counts; presentation choice |\n| Normalisation | 1st–99th percentile | Engine choice | Robust to outliers |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace tests check that the agents walk and that the view and replay controls work. The agent rules were checked against the description of Turner and Penn [3]. Gate counts have not been compared with DepthmapX's agent analysis on the same plan, nor with observed movement. In the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026, the study ran on level 1 of the audit block (644 nodes at 60 cm) and recorded 2,122 crossings at the busiest node.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe two components are well founded, but the index that combines them is not a published measure. The weights favour the agents; the square root makes moderately used areas stand out more than a linear scale would. Different weights would change which mid-ranked areas appear pink. For decisions, the separate views of integration and gate counts are easier to interpret and to defend with the literature [3, 5].\n\nAgent results depend on the number of agents and steps: with 200 agents and 1,500 steps on a few hundred nodes, counts are reasonably stable for the busiest areas but noisy in rarely visited corners. Agents have no destinations, purposes or knowledge of the building; real occupants walk to lifts, desks and exits. The study shows the movement that the layout alone encourages, which is useful for placing shared functions, displays or seating, but it does not forecast how many people will pass a point. The Everyday circulation study models purposeful trips between rooms.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe agent model and integration used by the Movement + depth study are sourced and implemented as published; the combined index has weights chosen for presentation rather than from evidence, and it should be read as a relative ranking. Reporting the two components alongside the index, as the study does, keeps the result traceable.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/spacesyntax.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/spacesyntax.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-motion2.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-motion2.cjs), [tests/workspace-p0.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-p0.cjs). This article's source: [movementdepth.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/movementdepth.md)."}], "references": ["B. Hillier, A. Penn, J. Hanson, T. Grajewski, J. Xu, Natural movement: or, configuration and attraction in urban pedestrian movement, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 20 (1) (1993) 29–66. [doi:10.1068/b200029](https://doi.org/10.1068/b200029)", "B. Hillier, Space is the Machine: A Configurational Theory of Architecture, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1996.", "A. Turner, A. Penn, Encoding natural movement as an agent-based system: an investigation into human pedestrian behaviour in the built environment, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 29 (4) (2002) 473–490. [doi:10.1068/b12850](https://doi.org/10.1068/b12850)", "T. Varoudis, depthmapX: multi-platform spatial network analysis software, Space Syntax Laboratory, University College London, 2012–. https://github.com/SpaceGroupUCL/depthmapX", "A. Turner, M. Doxa, D. O'Sullivan, A. Penn, From isovists to visibility graphs: a methodology for the analysis of architectural space, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 28 (1) (2001) 103–121. [doi:10.1068/b2684](https://doi.org/10.1068/b2684)", "B. Hillier, J. Hanson, The Social Logic of Space, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1984."], "category": "research", "simulation": "movementdepth", "summary": "How the Movement + depth study combines the crossings of exploratory agents with visual integration into one map of where movement gathers and how deep each spot lies. The report gives the sources of the agent model and integration, and flags the 0.65/0.35 weighting and the square-root scaling as SimuSite's own choices with no published basis.", "image": "img/home/movementdepth.webp", "imageAlt": "Floor plan shaded blue, white and pink by the combined movement and depth index"}}, {"id": "report-evac", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Everyday circulation between rooms: shortest walking routes, ant-colony walks and use-weighted trip patterns", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Pedestrian circulation", "Shortest path", "Ant colony optimisation", "Trip distribution", "Space planning"], "highlights": ["Routes are found with A* on a 0.4 m navigation grid (8 neighbours, no corner cutting), then pulled taut along lines of sight.", "An ant colony (pheromone exponent 1, heuristic weight 1.6, evaporation 0.12) walks every pair of rooms over 12 rounds; settled walks show the preferred routes.", "Each pair of rooms is weighted by the product of the rooms' use weights (living 20, kitchen 12, bath or entry 6.5, office 3.5, bedroom 1.5); these weights have no published source.", "Walking speed for the replay is 1.3 m/s, consistent with measured comfortable adult gait speeds.", "The study is a picture of likely routes for layout design, not an evacuation analysis or a count forecast."], "abstract": "Circulation, the movement of people between the spaces of a building, determines corridor widths, the placement of shared rooms and the distances people walk each day [1, 2]. Formal methods range from adjacency matrices and trip-distribution models in space planning to agent-based pedestrian simulation [2, 3]. SimuSite's Circulation study (labelled Everyday circulation in the Workspace; its identifier \"evac\" is historical) takes the rooms on one floor, finds the shortest walkable route between every pair with the A* algorithm on a navigation grid [4], and runs an ant colony optimisation over the same grid so that walks start exploratory and settle onto good routes [5, 6]. Pairs are weighted by how often their rooms are used, and the metres walked through each spot are mapped as a density. This report documents the method and its constants. The path-finding and ant-colony algorithms are standard and the walking speed is consistent with measured values [7]; the room-use weights are assumptions without a source, and the result shows the pattern of routes, not numbers of people.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nIn space planning, the expected number of trips between two spaces is often modelled as a product of their activity levels, as in the gravity models of transport planning, and layouts are judged by the total distance weighted by trips [2, 3]. Shortest paths are the first-order prediction of where people walk; real paths deviate from them because people prefer fewer turns, wider spaces and familiar routes [8]. Ant colony optimisation, introduced by Dorigo and colleagues, finds short paths in a graph by stochastic walkers that lay pheromone in proportion to path quality, with evaporation; it converges on short routes while keeping some variety [5, 6]. The study uses it to show the spread of plausible routes rather than to find the shortest path, which A* already gives.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Grid\n\nThe walls of the level, with their door and opening gaps, and furniture edges are rasterised at 0.1 m. A navigation grid of 0.4 m cells is blocked where any wall pixel touches the cell; diagonal steps are not allowed to cut a wall corner. Each room is represented by a point (its label position), snapped to the nearest free cell.\n\n### 2.2. Shortest routes\n\nFor each pair of rooms, A* with eight neighbours finds the shortest grid path [4], which is then pulled taut by removing intermediate points wherever a straight line of sight exists on the fine raster. The study reports the average and longest route and the walking time at 1.3 m/s.\n\n### 2.3. Ant colony\n\nFor each pair, an ant colony runs for 12 rounds. In each round up to 5 ants walk from one room towards the other, never revisiting a cell. From cell i, the probability of stepping to a free neighbour j is proportional to τ_j^α exp(β (h_i − h_j)/c_ij), where τ is the pheromone, h the true walking distance to the goal from a Dijkstra distance field, c_ij the step length, α = 1 and β = 1.6 [5, 6]. After each round, pheromone evaporates by ρ = 0.12 (to a floor of 0.05) and each successful ant adds 40/L to the cells of its path, where L is its length. Walks are smoothed with Chaikin's corner cutting [9], given a small sideways wobble that is larger in early rounds, and replayed at 1.3 m/s.\n\n### 2.4. Trip weights\n\nEach room has a use weight from its name: living room 20, kitchen, dining, break room or lobby 12, bathroom, entrance, stair or lift 6.5, office, study, laundry or storage 3.5, bedroom 1.5, and 6.5 otherwise. A pair's weight is the product of its rooms' weights, and the number of ants per round on each pair is proportional to that weight, with the busiest pair receiving the full number and a cap of 1,200 walks in total. The user can switch weighting off so that all pairs are walked equally.\n\n### 2.5. Density\n\nThe metres walked through each cell of a density grid are accumulated by bilinear deposits every 5 cm along each walk. The busiest spot and the number of crossings there are reported.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Circulation study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Navigation grid | 0.4 m, 8 neighbours, no corner cutting | Engine choice | Numerical setting; 0.4 m is close to a person's body depth |\n| Shortest path | A* with taut-string smoothing | Hart, Nilsson and Raphael [4] | Sourced |\n| ACO rule | τ^α · heuristic^β, α = 1, β = 1.6 | Ant System form [5, 6] | Sourced form; values are engine choices within the usual range |\n| Evaporation ρ | 0.12 | Engine choice | Within the 0.1–0.5 range used in ACO studies [6] |\n| Deposit | 40 / L | Ant System Q/L rule [5] | Sourced form |\n| Rounds, ants | 12, up to 5 per round | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Room-use weights | 20 / 12 / 6.5 / 3.5 / 1.5 | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source |\n| Pair weight | Product of room weights | Gravity-type trip model [3] | Plausible form; no distance decay |\n| Walking speed | 1.3 m/s | Measured comfortable gait 1.27–1.46 m/s for adults [7] | Consistent |\n| Smoothing | Chaikin, 3 iterations | Chaikin [9] | Sourced |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace tests check that the study is in the menu, runs and animates. There is no test of the routes against a reference. The A* implementation can be checked on simple layouts by hand; the ant colony is stochastic but seeded, so a run repeats exactly.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on level 1 of the audit block (nine places), the study reported an average walk of 13.4 m, a longest walk of 21.0 m (16 s at 1.3 m/s), 420 walks totalling 6,652 m, and about 13 ant crossings at the busiest spot. It found routes for 21 of the 36 pairs; the remaining 15 pairs had no walkable route on the navigation grid. We did not determine whether this was due to doors missing in the generated audit model or to door gaps too narrow for the 0.4 m grid; the study should say which rooms are unreachable so that the user can tell.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Weights.** The use weights encode a plausible ranking of how often rooms in a home are visited, but they are not derived from time-use or observation data, and they mix homes and offices. Because the pair weight is a product, the living room dominates: a living-kitchen pair weighs 240, a bedroom-bathroom pair under 10. For offices or schools, users should set the weights or switch weighting off. Trip-distribution models in practice also include a distance decay, which the study omits [3].\n\n**Routes.** Shortest paths on a grid are a reasonable first approximation in small buildings where people know the layout. People in unfamiliar buildings, or with mobility constraints, follow different routes [8]. The ant colony adds variety, but the variety reflects the algorithm's randomness, not observed behaviour.\n\n**Not evacuation.** Despite its identifier, the study does not model egress, congestion or flow capacity. The Fire evacuation study, documented separately, uses the SFPE hydraulic model for that purpose.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Circulation study uses standard path-finding and ant-colony algorithms and a realistic walking speed. Its room-use weights are unsourced assumptions that strongly shape the density map. The study is useful for seeing which corridors and doors carry the everyday routes of a layout; it does not predict occupant numbers or evacuation performance.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/circulation-engine.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/circulation-engine.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-p0.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-p0.cjs), [tests/workspace-motion2.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-motion2.cjs). This article's source: [evac.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/evac.md)."}], "references": ["E. Neufert, P. Neufert, Architects' Data, 4th ed., Wiley-Blackwell, Chichester, 2012.", "R.L. Francis, L.F. McGinnis, J.A. White, Facility Layout and Location: An Analytical Approach, 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, Englewood Cliffs, 1992.", "J. de D. Ortúzar, L.G. Willumsen, Modelling Transport, 4th ed., Wiley, Chichester, 2011.", "P.E. Hart, N.J. Nilsson, B. Raphael, A formal basis for the heuristic determination of minimum cost paths, IEEE Trans. Syst. Sci. Cybern. 4 (2) (1968) 100–107. [doi:10.1109/TSSC.1968.300136](https://doi.org/10.1109/TSSC.1968.300136)", "M. Dorigo, V. Maniezzo, A. Colorni, Ant system: optimization by a colony of cooperating agents, IEEE Trans. Syst. Man Cybern. B 26 (1) (1996) 29–41. [doi:10.1109/3477.484436](https://doi.org/10.1109/3477.484436)", "M. Dorigo, T. Stützle, Ant Colony Optimization, MIT Press, Cambridge, MA, 2004.", "R.W. Bohannon, Comfortable and maximum walking speed of adults aged 20–79 years: reference values and determinants, Age Ageing 26 (1) (1997) 15–19. [doi:10.1093/ageing/26.1.15](https://doi.org/10.1093/ageing/26.1.15)", "R.G. Golledge, Path selection and route preference in human navigation: a progress report, in: A.U. Frank, W. Kuhn (Eds.), Spatial Information Theory, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 988, Springer, Berlin, 1995, pp. 207–222.", "G.M. Chaikin, An algorithm for high-speed curve generation, Comput. Graph. Image Process. 3 (4) (1974) 346–349."], "category": "research", "simulation": "evac", "summary": "How the Circulation study shows where people tend to walk between the rooms of one floor, with A* shortest routes on a navigation grid, an ant colony that settles on good routes, and pair weights from how often each room is used. The report gives the sources of the algorithms and the walking speed, and flags the room-use weights as unsourced assumptions.", "image": "img/home/evac.webp", "imageAlt": "Walks between rooms drawn as bands along the corridors, darker where more people pass"}}, {"id": "report-isovist", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Isovists with height: what can be seen from a viewpoint at the eye heights of a child, a wheelchair user and an adult", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Isovist", "Visibility analysis", "Natural surveillance", "Eye height", "Child supervision"], "highlights": ["720 sight lines (0.5° apart) from the viewpoint; anything as tall as the eye stops a line.", "A lower object at distance d hides the floor from d to d·E/(E − h), and a target at height z from d to d·(E − z)/(E − h).", "The same view is compared at eye heights of 1.0 m (child), 1.2 m (wheelchair user) and 1.6 m (adult).", "The geometry is exact for vertical-sided obstacles; eye heights are representative values consistent with anthropometric data.", "The study tests lines of sight, not attention or lighting."], "abstract": "An isovist is the set of all points visible from a given point in space [1, 2]. Isovists have been used to describe spatial experience, to quantify natural surveillance in the sense of Newman's defensible space and of crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED), and to check supervision in nurseries, classrooms and care settings [3–5]. Most isovist tools work in plan and treat every obstacle as a full-height wall. SimuSite's Isovist study adds height: from a viewpoint at a chosen eye height, it casts 720 sight lines and treats furniture lower than the eye as hiding only a strip of floor behind it, computed by similar triangles. It reports the floor seen and the blind spots within the viewer's room, compares the result at the eye heights of a child, a wheelchair user and a standing adult, and checks whether marked hazards are visible. This report documents the geometry and its sources. The visibility rule is exact for obstacles with vertical sides and horizontal tops; the eye heights are representative values consistent with anthropometric references [6]. The study answers whether a line of sight exists; whether someone will look is outside its scope.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nTandy introduced the isovist in landscape survey, and Benedikt formalised isovists and isovist fields as a description of the space visible from points of a plan, with measures such as area, perimeter and occlusivity [1, 2]. Turner and colleagues later linked isovists into visibility graphs [7]. In design for safety, a supervisor's isovist shows which parts of a room they can see from where they usually stand; CPTED guidance asks for clear sightlines across spaces and to entrances [4, 5]. Because what an adult sees over a 1.2 m shelf differs from what a child or a seated person sees, the eye height matters for these questions.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Obstacles and the viewer's room\n\nThe plan of one level is converted to line segments with heights: walls and glazing at their full height, furniture as the outlines of their footprints at their heights. The viewer's room is found on a raster: walls of 2 m or more and glazing close it, doorways up to about 1.1 m are bridged by a morphological closing with a 0.55 m disc, and the region is grown back to the walls. If the floor still runs out to the edge of the drawing, the whole drawing counts and the result is marked as an approximation.\n\n### 2.2. Sight lines with height\n\nFrom the viewpoint at eye height E, 720 rays are cast, 0.5° apart. Along each ray, the obstacles crossed are taken in order of distance d. An obstacle of height h ≥ E stops the ray. A lower obstacle hides the floor from d to d·E/(E − h), where the sightline over its top comes back down to the floor. When looking for a target at height z (for example the head of a seated child at 0.6 m), the hidden strip ends at d·(E − z)/(E − h), and obstacles lower than z hide nothing. For example, a 0.9 m shelf 3 m away hides 9 m of floor where a seated child could be from a 1.0 m eye, but only 1.3 m from a 1.6 m eye. Imported models are handled as a height map traversed with the Amanatides–Woo grid walk [8], with the same rule.\n\n### 2.3. Outputs\n\nEach floor cell of the room is seen if it lies within the free length of the nearest ray and outside every hidden strip. The study reports the floor seen, the share of the room that is blind, the same share at eye heights of 1.0, 1.2 and 1.6 m, and, for each marked hazard (a point at a height), whether the straight sightline from the eye to it passes above every obstacle except the last 15 cm, which is the hazard's own object. A walk around the room shows the isovist at each point.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Isovist study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Isovist | Region visible from a point | Tandy [1]; Benedikt [2] | Sourced |\n| Sight lines | 720 (0.5°) | Engine choice | Numerical setting; at 10 m, adjacent rays are 9 cm apart |\n| Hidden strip | d·(E − z)/(E − h) | Similar triangles | Exact for vertical-sided obstacles with flat tops |\n| Child eye height | 1.0 m | Representative value | Consistent with standing eye height of children about 5–7 years old [6] |\n| Wheelchair eye height | 1.2 m | Representative value | Consistent with seated eye heights of adult wheelchair users, about 1.1–1.3 m [6, 9] |\n| Adult eye height | 1.6 m | Representative value | Close to the average standing eye height of adults (about 1.5 m for women, 1.6 m for men) [6] |\n| Seated child target | 0.6 m | Representative value | Assumption, consistent with sitting height of young children [6] |\n| Doorway bridging | ≈ 1.1 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Room-closing wall height | 2 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Hazard's own object | Last 15 cm of the sightline | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace interior tests check that the study runs from a viewpoint, reports the seen area and the three eye heights, and that hazards are counted. The similar-triangles rule was checked by hand for this report on the example in §2.2. There is no comparison with a three-dimensional ray tracer; for the obstacles the study supports (vertical sides, flat tops), the plan computation is exact up to the ray and raster resolution.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026, a viewpoint in a 25 m² meeting room at 1.0 m eye height looking for a seated child saw 24.9 m² of floor, with 0.4 % blind at every eye height, and the one marked hazard was not visible from the viewpoint. The near-identical results at all three heights are expected in a room with little furniture.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**What the geometry captures.** The study handles the case plan-based isovists miss: low furniture that an adult sees over and a child does not. That is the situation in nurseries, classrooms and shops, where shelf heights are a design decision. The strip rule shows directly how much difference a lower shelf or a raised viewpoint makes.\n\n**What it does not.** Obstacles with sloped or open shapes (chairs, tables with open space under them, plants) are treated as solid blocks of their height, which overstates occlusion. The eye height of real people varies widely around the representative values. Visibility also depends on light, contrast and attention; a line of sight is necessary for supervision and natural surveillance but not sufficient [4, 5]. Isovists from one viewpoint do not describe how a person moving through the room sees it; the walk view partly addresses that.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Isovist study's visibility rule is exact geometry for the obstacles it models, its eye heights are representative values consistent with anthropometric data, and its concept follows Benedikt. It is suited to checking supervision sightlines and blind spots in early layout, with the understanding that it tests lines of sight rather than what people will notice.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/isovist.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/isovist.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-interior.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-interior.cjs). This article's source: [isovist.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/isovist.md)."}], "references": ["C.R.V. Tandy, The isovist method of landscape survey, in: H.C. Murray (Ed.), Methods of Landscape Analysis, Landscape Research Group, London, 1967, pp. 9–10.", "M.L. Benedikt, To take hold of space: isovists and isovist fields, Environ. Plann. B 6 (1) (1979) 47–65. [doi:10.1068/b060047](https://doi.org/10.1068/b060047)", "O. Newman, Defensible Space: Crime Prevention Through Urban Design, Macmillan, New York, 1972.", "T.D. Crowe, Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design, 2nd ed., Butterworth-Heinemann, Boston, 2000.", "P. Cozens, T. Love, A review and current status of crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED), J. Plan. Lit. 30 (4) (2015) 393–412. [doi:10.1177/0885412215595440](https://doi.org/10.1177/0885412215595440)", "A.R. Tilley, Henry Dreyfuss Associates, The Measure of Man and Woman: Human Factors in Design, revised ed., Wiley, New York, 2002.", "A. Turner, M. Doxa, D. O'Sullivan, A. Penn, From isovists to visibility graphs: a methodology for the analysis of architectural space, Environ. Plann. B Plann. Des. 28 (1) (2001) 103–121. [doi:10.1068/b2684](https://doi.org/10.1068/b2684)", "J. Amanatides, A. Woo, A fast voxel traversal algorithm for ray tracing, in: Proceedings of Eurographics '87, Amsterdam, 1987, pp. 3–10.", "U.S. Access Board, ADA Accessibility Guidelines; Advisory on reach ranges and eye level of wheelchair users, in: 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design, U.S. Department of Justice, Washington, DC, 2010."], "category": "research", "simulation": "isovist", "summary": "How the Isovist study finds the floor area seen and the blind spots within a room from a chosen viewpoint, with 720 sight lines and a similar-triangles rule for furniture lower than the eye. The report gives the geometry, the sources of the isovist concept and the eye heights, and describes the limits of a plan-based visibility check.", "image": "img/home/isovist.webp", "imageAlt": "The area visible from a viewpoint in a room, with blind spots behind furniture"}}, {"id": "report-views", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Screening quality views to the outdoors against the LEED v4.1 criteria with horizontal sight lines", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Views to the outdoors", "LEED Quality Views", "View factor", "Occupant wellbeing", "Visibility analysis"], "highlights": ["Spots every 0.08–1 m over the occupied floor cast 180–1,440 horizontal sight lines at a seated eye height of 1.07 m (LEED's 42 in).", "Criteria follow LEED v4.1 Quality Views: line of sight for 75 % of the floor, and 2 of 4 view qualities for 75 %.", "Distant objects count from 7.5 m past the glass (LEED's 25 ft); near views are within 3 × the window head height.", "The view-factor thresholds (0.07, 0.2, 0.4, 0.75 of a 90° cone) are SimuSite's proxy for the Heschong Mahone scale and have not been checked against it.", "\"Movement\" outside counts only when the user declares it (until 21 November 2026 it was assumed by default, which made criterion 2 nearly always met)."], "abstract": "Access to views of the outdoors is associated with occupant satisfaction, wellbeing and, in some studies, performance [1–3]. Rating systems reward it: LEED v4.1 Quality Views asks for a direct line of sight to the outdoors from 75 % of regularly occupied floor area, and for 75 % to have views with at least two of four qualities [4]. SimuSite's Quality views study screens a floor plan against these criteria. From a grid of spots over the occupied floor, it casts horizontal sight lines at seated eye height; walls and tall furniture stop them, and glass lets them pass above its sill. For each spot it records whether a view out exists and evaluates the four criteria: views in directions at least 90° apart; at least two of nature or sky, movement, and objects at least 7.5 m away; a window within three times its head height; and a view factor of 3 or more. This report documents the method and its constants. The criteria, distances and eye height follow LEED v4.1 [4]; the view factor is computed from a geometric proxy whose thresholds have not been checked against the Heschong Mahone definition [2], and \"movement\" is assumed present unless the user switches it off. The study is a design screening, not a LEED submission.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nResearch on office workers by the Heschong Mahone Group found that the size and content of the view from a desk were associated with better performance on some tasks and fewer reported health symptoms, and introduced a view factor scale from 1 to 5 describing view size and quality [2]. Other studies associate views of nature with recovery and lower stress [1, 3]. LEED v4.1 Quality Views turned these findings into four criteria that a floor area can meet, with a line of sight drawn at 42 inches (1.07 m), a seated eye height [4]. The European daylight standard EN 17037 assesses views separately with criteria on horizontal sight angle, outside distance and layers of the view [5].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Occupied floor and spots\n\nThe plan of one level is rasterised as in the Space syntax and Isovist studies. The occupied floor is everything inside the walls minus furniture; rooms the user excludes (corridors, storage) are not counted. Spots are placed on a grid whose spacing (0.08–1 m) and ray count (180–1,440) are chosen by detail level and the time budget.\n\n### 2.2. Sight lines\n\nFrom each spot, horizontal rays at the eye height (1.07 m seated by default, or 1.6 m standing) run through the plan. Walls of 2 m or more stop a ray, as does anything as tall as the eye. Glazing lets a ray through if its sill is below the eye. When a ray crosses a pane into the outdoors, it records the distance to the pane, how far the view runs beyond it, and whether the sky shows above whatever stands outside.\n\n### 2.3. Criteria at a spot\n\n- **Line of sight**: at least one ray reaches the outdoors.\n- **Criterion 1**: views out in at least two directions 90° or more apart.\n- **Criterion 2**: at least two of: sky or nature (sky seen, or nature declared present by the user), movement (counted only when the user declares a street or path with people in view), and objects at least 7.5 m beyond the glass (a ray runs at least that far outside).\n- **Criterion 3**: a view through glazing no farther than three times the window head height (2.4 m by default, so 7.2 m).\n- **Criterion 4**: a view factor of 3 or more. For each ray through glass, the vertical angle subtended by the window from sill to head as seen from the spot, each part capped at 45°, is divided by 90°. The best 90° horizontal sector is found, and its mean fraction F is mapped to a view factor of 1 to 5 with thresholds 0.07, 0.2, 0.4 and 0.75.\n\nA spot has a quality view if it has a line of sight and meets at least two criteria. The floor meets the credit if both the line of sight and quality views reach 75 % of the counted floor area; 90 % for both is reported as exemplary performance [4].\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Quality views study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Eye height | 1.07 m seated (1.6 m standing) | LEED v4.1: 42 in [4] | Sourced |\n| Thresholds | 75 % line of sight and 75 % quality; 90 % exemplary | LEED v4.1 [4] | Sourced |\n| Two of four criteria | As in §2.3 | LEED v4.1 [4] | Sourced |\n| Far objects | 7.5 m beyond the glass | LEED v4.1: 25 ft (7.6 m) [4] | Sourced (rounded) |\n| Near window | 3 × head height | LEED v4.1 [4] | Sourced |\n| Head height | 2.4 m default | Engine choice | Editable input |\n| Blocking walls | ≥ 2 m | Engine choice | Assumption; LEED counts partitions at their height |\n| View factor proxy | Vertical window angle in the best 90° sector | Engine choice | Flagged: proxy, not the HMG procedure |\n| View factor thresholds | 0.07, 0.2, 0.4, 0.75 | Engine choice | Flagged: not checked against HMG 2003 [2] |\n| Movement | Off unless declared | Engine choice | Changed 21 November 2026 from assumed present |\n| Nature | Off unless declared; sky detected | Engine choice | Reasonable |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace interior tests check that the study runs, reports the criteria and switches between its views. The criteria logic was checked against the LEED v4.1 credit text for this report [4]. The view-factor proxy has not been compared with view factors assessed with the Heschong Mahone method on real rooms.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on level 1 of the audit block (237 m² of occupied floor, spots every 50 cm, 360 rays each), 86 % of the floor had a line of sight out and 84 % had quality views, so the credit thresholds were met. By criterion: directions 90° apart 25 %, two view types 86 %, within three head heights 84 %, view factor 3 or more 15 %. Criterion 2 equalled the line-of-sight share because movement was assumed and every view out of this block reaches beyond 7.5 m or shows the sky. With movement off by default, the same case gives 2 % for criterion 2 (sky and a far view seldom fall in the same view at seated eye height on this site), 31 % of the floor with quality views, and the credit not met; the other three criteria are unchanged. Declaring movement brings back the earlier figures. The difference shows how much this one declaration decides the result.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Movement default.** With movement assumed, any view that runs beyond 7.5 m or shows the sky meets criterion 2. Most views out of a building do, so criterion 2 was nearly always met. The default is now off, the conservative choice: the user declares movement where a street or path with people is actually in view, and the method text states it as an assumption.\n\n**View factor.** Heschong Mahone's view factor combines the size of the view, judged from the angular extent of the window and what is seen through it, with its content [2]. The proxy measures only angular size through the window, with thresholds that are SimuSite's choice. It is a reasonable indicator of how much window a spot sees but should not be reported as an HMG view factor without calibration.\n\n**Plan-based method.** Sight lines are horizontal at one height. A view that is blocked at 1.07 m by a desk screen but open above it, or a view down to a street from an upper floor, is not represented. Glass is treated as transparent; frits, tints or blinds are not modelled. The study also ignores whether the view is pleasant; EN 17037 and LEED both rate content only coarsely [4, 5].\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Quality views study follows the LEED v4.1 criteria, distances and eye height. Two elements need attention before results are used in a submission: the view-factor proxy and its thresholds, and the user's declarations of nature and movement outside, which the geometry cannot check. As a screening of how plan depth, partition layout and window placement affect view access, the study is appropriate.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/views.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/views.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-interior.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-interior.cjs). This article's source: [views.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/views.md)."}], "references": ["R. Kaplan, The nature of the view from home: psychological benefits, Environ. Behav. 33 (4) (2001) 507–542. [doi:10.1177/00139160121973115](https://doi.org/10.1177/00139160121973115)", "Heschong Mahone Group, Windows and Offices: A Study of Office Worker Performance and the Indoor Environment, Technical Report P500-03-082-A-9, California Energy Commission, Sacramento, 2003.", "R.S. Ulrich, View through a window may influence recovery from surgery, Science 224 (4647) (1984) 420–421. [doi:10.1126/science.6143402](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.6143402)", "U.S. Green Building Council, LEED v4.1 Building Design and Construction, EQ Credit: Quality Views, USGBC, Washington, DC, 2019 (updated 2021).", "CEN, EN 17037:2018, Daylight in buildings, European Committee for Standardization, Brussels, 2018."], "category": "research", "simulation": "views", "summary": "How the Quality views study checks, for spots over the occupied floor, whether there is a line of sight to the outdoors through vision glazing and which of the four LEED v4.1 view criteria are met. The report gives the sources of each criterion and the seated eye height, and flags the view-factor thresholds as needing confirmation. The audit also found that movement outside was assumed by default; this was changed so that the user declares it.", "image": "img/home/views.webp", "imageAlt": "Floor plan coloured where occupants have quality views to the outdoors"}}, {"id": "report-wayfinding", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Where visitors need signs: decision points, sightlines and a Signage Priority Score on the corridor network", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Wayfinding", "Signage", "Decision points", "Legibility", "Healthcare planning"], "highlights": ["Routes run from lift lobbies, stair landings and entrances to each public room's door over the real floor; lifts cost the equivalent of 40 m plus 4 m per floor, stairs 12 m per floor.", "The public floor is thinned to its centre lines (Zhang–Suen) to find junctions, turns and lift or stair exits as candidate sign points.", "The Signage Priority Score weighs choice complexity 25, traffic 20, hidden destination 20, ambiguity 15 and wrong-turn cost 20, and is zeroed where a sign already covers the point; High ≥ 60, Medium ≥ 35.", "A sign counts as seen when at least 2 of the 8 one-metre spots before the point see it within 15 m and 60° of square-on.", "The principles are from the wayfinding literature, but the weights, thresholds and costs are engine choices that have not been calibrated against observed behaviour."], "abstract": "Wayfinding, finding one's way to a destination in an unfamiliar building, depends on the configuration of the plan, on what can be seen from each decision point, and on signage [1–4]. Hospitals, universities and transport buildings spend heavily on signs and still generate frequent requests for directions [5]. SimuSite's Wayfinding study helps decide where signs are most needed. It computes shortest routes from the places visitors start (lift lobbies, stair landings and entrances) to the door of each public room, extracts the corridor network, and identifies decision points where routes split. Each point receives a Signage Priority Score from six factors with written formulas: the number of ways on, the number of routes passing, whether the destination is out of sight, how ambiguous the choice is, the cost of a wrong turn, and whether an existing sign already covers it. Proposed sign layouts are then evaluated by the share of route decisions they guide. This report documents the method and its sources. Route finding, skeletonisation and line-of-sight tests are standard; the factors reflect principles from the wayfinding literature [1–4, 6]; the weights, thresholds and costs are SimuSite's choices and have not been compared with observed wayfinding. The study is a structured way to prioritise sign locations, not a prediction of navigation success.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nWeisman identified four classes of environmental variables that affect wayfinding: visual access, architectural differentiation, signs and room numbering, and plan configuration [1]. Passini and Arthur described wayfinding as a sequence of decisions made at decision points, each needing information about the way on, and argued that signs should be placed where decisions are made and should show the destination or the way to it [2, 3]. O'Neill showed that plan complexity reduces wayfinding performance and that signage improves it [4]. In multi-level buildings, vertical transitions are a particular source of disorientation [6]. Space syntax research adds that visual integration of a plan correlates with how easily people find their way [7]. These findings suggest that signs are most needed at decision points with many options, heavy use and poor visual access to destinations.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Floor, starts and destinations\n\nEach floor is rasterised: walls block except at doors, furniture blocks walking, and rooms are labelled. Rooms named as staff-only (utility, plant, storage and similar) are not walked through and are not destinations. Starts are lift lobbies (just outside the lift door), stair landings, the main entrances on the ground floor, or points the user places. Destinations are the public rooms, grouped by name so that \"Patient room 301\" and \"Patient room 302\" form one group. For rooms on other floors, routes go to a lift or stair paired between floors, with a lift costed as 40 m of walking plus 4 m per floor travelled (about 30 s of waiting and ride) and a stair as 12 m per floor.\n\n### 2.2. Routes and network\n\nShortest routes on the grid use eight directions without corner cutting, and are pulled taut along lines of sight, as in the Circulation study. The public floor is thinned to its centre lines with the Zhang–Suen algorithm [8], spurs shorter than the corridor's width are pruned, and the skeleton becomes a graph whose nodes are junctions of three or more ways, ends, and sharp turns (more than a set angle with runs of at least 2 m on both sides). Junctions, turns and the exits from lifts and stairs are the candidate decision points.\n\n### 2.3. Signage Priority Score\n\nFor each candidate point, from the routes passing through it:\n\n- **A, choice complexity**: (branches − 1)/3, plus 0.2 if two branches leave within 45° of each other, capped at 1; 0.33 at a turn.\n- **B, traffic**: the number of routes passing, normalised by the largest on the floor.\n- **C, hidden destination**: the share of passing routes whose destination door, or a later point of the route, is not in sight from 5 m before the point.\n- **D, ambiguity**: 1 minus the largest share of destinations down one branch, plus 0.25 if two branches lead to rooms of the same group, capped at 1.\n- **F, wrong-turn cost**: the mean of twice the length (up to 40 m) of the branches a route does not take, divided by 40 m.\n- **E, existing coverage**: 1 if an existing sign within 8 m is seen from the main approach, facing it within 60°.\n\nThe score is 100 × (25A + 20B + 20C + 15D + 20F)/100 × (1 − E). Points score High at 60 or more, Medium at 35 or more, and Low otherwise; a point on a route scoring at least the threshold (35 by default) with no existing coverage gets a recommended sign. A High point whose way on is mostly out of sight is reported as unresolved.\n\n### 2.4. Sign layouts and their evaluation\n\nA proposed sign stands on the wall ahead of arriving people when one is within 6 m, or hangs over the corridor, faces the main approach and lists, for each way on, the destinations down it with arrows relative to the walker. A route sees a sign if at least 2 of the 8 one-metre spots before the decision point see it, within 15 m, ahead of the walker and within 60° of square-on. A decision is guided when a sign there is seen and lists the way taken. Three layouts are generated (minimum, recommended, maximum) and compared by sign count, decisions guided, routes fully guided and High points covered. Room identification signs beside doors are checked for being seen within 30° of square-on from 2–10 m.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Wayfinding study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Decision points as sign locations | Junctions, turns, vertical exits | Passini; Arthur and Passini [2, 3] | Sourced principle |\n| Factor A, choice complexity | (branches − 1)/3 + 0.2 close | Principle from Weisman, O'Neill [1, 4] | Formula is an engine choice |\n| Factor C, visual access | Destination seen from 5 m before | Weisman [1] | Formula is an engine choice |\n| Factor D, ambiguity | Share and same-group penalty 0.25 | Arthur and Passini [3] | Formula is an engine choice |\n| Factor F, wrong-turn cost | 2 × branch length, cap 40 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Weights A, B, C, D, F | 25, 20, 20, 15, 20 | Engine choice | Flagged: not calibrated; editable |\n| Classes | High ≥ 60, Medium ≥ 35 | Engine choice | Flagged: not calibrated |\n| Lift and stair costs | 40 m + 4 m/floor; 12 m/floor | Engine choice (\"about 30 s of wait and ride\") | Assumption |\n| Sign seen | 2 of 8 spots, ≤ 15 m, ≤ 60° | Engine choice | Plausible; legibility distance depends on letter height [9] |\n| Existing-sign reach | 8 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Skeleton | Zhang–Suen thinning | Zhang and Suen [8] | Sourced |\n| Routes | Grid shortest paths, taut | Standard | Sourced |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe repository has four tests for this study. `tests/wayfinding-signage.cjs` and `tests/wayfinding-layouts.cjs` run the engine on synthetic floors and check, among other things, that layouts A, B and C have increasing sign counts, that the recommended layout guides at least 80 % of decisions and covers every High point, that a hung sign shows mirrored arrows on its back face computed from the geometry, that a sign moved into a room is flagged with a visibility issue and passes again when moved back, and that adding the recommended signs reduces the recommendations and raises clarity. The Workspace tests check the study in the application. In our run of `tests/wayfinding-layouts.cjs` on 21 November 2026, all eight checks passed; on the test's hospital floor the minimum and balanced layouts each used 4 signs and the maximum layout 11, all three guided 43 of 43 decisions and 22 of 22 routes, and adding the balanced layout reduced the points needing a sign from 2 to 0. These are consistency tests: they confirm that the score and the evaluation behave as designed, not that the design matches how people find their way.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on the two-storey audit block, the study found 24 routes from 3 starts (1 stair, 2 entrances), 3 decision points on visitor routes (0 High, 1 Medium, 2 Low), recommended 1 sign, reported no unresolved points and no route without a valid path, and estimated that existing signage covered 25 % of the choices.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Grounded principles, uncalibrated numbers.** Each factor corresponds to a variable that the literature associates with wayfinding difficulty [1–4, 6, 7]. The functional forms, weights and class thresholds are reasonable but arbitrary; another reasonable choice could reorder Medium points. The weights are exposed so that a wayfinding consultant can adjust them, and the study reports the factors behind each score. Calibration would require comparing scores with observed hesitation, errors or requests for directions in existing buildings, for example by the methods of Hölscher and colleagues [6].\n\n**What a plan cannot show.** Landmarks, colour, lighting, the content and legibility of signs, and the familiarity of visitors all affect wayfinding and are not modelled [3, 5]. The visibility test assumes a sign is legible within 15 m, which depends on letter height; signage guidance relates letter height to viewing distance [9].\n\n**Groups from names.** Destination groups and staff-only rooms are read from room names. Inconsistent naming will distort ambiguity scores and routes; the user can correct groups.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Wayfinding study turns established wayfinding principles into an explicit, adjustable score and evaluates sign layouts with geometric tests. Its route and network methods are standard and its tests confirm internal consistency. Its weights, thresholds and costs are uncalibrated engine choices, so its output should be used to prioritise and discuss sign locations with a wayfinding designer, not to claim a level of wayfinding performance.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/wayfinding-signage.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/wayfinding-signage.js), [mvp/js/interior/wayfinding.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/wayfinding.js), [emb/lib/plansim.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/plansim.js). Tests: [tests/wayfinding-signage.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/wayfinding-signage.cjs), [tests/wayfinding-layouts.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/wayfinding-layouts.cjs), [tests/workspace-wayfinding-signage.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-wayfinding-signage.cjs), [tests/workspace-wayfinding-layout.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-wayfinding-layout.cjs). This article's source: [wayfinding.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/wayfinding.md)."}], "references": ["J. Weisman, Evaluating architectural legibility: way-finding in the built environment, Environ. Behav. 13 (2) (1981) 189–204. [doi:10.1177/0013916581132004](https://doi.org/10.1177/0013916581132004)", "R. Passini, Wayfinding in Architecture, Van Nostrand Reinhold, New York, 1984.", "P. Arthur, R. Passini, Wayfinding: People, Signs, and Architecture, McGraw-Hill, New York, 1992.", "M.J. O'Neill, Effects of signage and floor plan configuration on wayfinding accuracy, Environ. Behav. 23 (5) (1991) 553–574. [doi:10.1177/0013916591235002](https://doi.org/10.1177/0013916591235002)", "J.R. Carpman, M.A. Grant, Design That Cares: Planning Health Facilities for Patients and Visitors, 3rd ed., Jossey-Bass, San Francisco, 2016.", "C. Hölscher, T. Meilinger, G. Vrachliotis, M. Brösamle, M. Knauff, Up the down staircase: wayfinding strategies in multi-level buildings, J. Environ. Psychol. 26 (4) (2006) 284–299. [doi:10.1016/j.jenvp.2006.09.002](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2006.09.002)", "S. Haq, C. Zimring, Just down the road a piece: the development of topological knowledge of building layouts, Environ. Behav. 35 (1) (2003) 132–160.", "T.Y. Zhang, C.Y. Suen, A fast parallel algorithm for thinning digital patterns, Commun. ACM 27 (3) (1984) 236–239. [doi:10.1145/357994.358023](https://doi.org/10.1145/357994.358023)", "D. Gibson, The Wayfinding Handbook: Information Design for Public Places, Princeton Architectural Press, New York, 2009."], "category": "research", "simulation": "wayfinding", "summary": "How the Wayfinding study finds the decision points on visitor routes from lifts, stairs and entrances, scores each with a Signage Priority Score from six stated factors, and evaluates proposed sign layouts by how many route decisions they guide. The report gives the sources of the route and network methods and of the wayfinding principles, and makes clear that the score's weights and thresholds are SimuSite's own and have not been calibrated against observed wayfinding.", "image": "img/home/wayfinding.webp", "imageAlt": "Corridor network with decision points ranked high, medium and low for signage"}}, {"id": "report-privacy", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Visual privacy from the street and neighbouring buildings: sightline screening and a window risk score", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Visual privacy", "Overlooking", "Sightlines", "Window design", "Residential design"], "highlights": ["Onlookers stand along the street at 1.6 m eye height every 1.5 m, along mapped roads and paths every 2 m, and at every storey of buildings within 40 m every 3 m.", "A floor spot 1.2 m up is seen when a straight line from an onlooker passes through a window between its sill and head and over all furniture and site buildings.", "Rooms need high, medium, low or no privacy from their names; high-need rooms are flagged when more than 10 % of their floor is seen, medium-need when more than 25 %.", "Window score = 100 × need weight × (0.6 × share of onlookers who see in + 0.4 × share of the room's floor seen); Medium at 15, High at 40.", "The geometry is exact for straight sightlines; the need classes, limits, weights and thresholds are heuristics without a published source."], "abstract": "Privacy, the selective control of access to the self, is a basic need in dwellings and in clinical and other sensitive rooms [1, 2]. Overlooking from streets and neighbouring windows is a common planning concern, and many local planning guides set separation distances between facing windows of habitable rooms [3]. SimuSite's Privacy study screens a floor plan for visual exposure. Onlookers are placed along the street, along the site's mapped roads and paths, and at each storey of nearby buildings; for every spot of the floor at 1.2 m, the study tests whether a straight line from each onlooker reaches it through a window, between the window's sill and head, and over furniture, site buildings and terrain. It reports the share of each room's floor that is seen, flags rooms whose exposure exceeds a limit for their privacy need, and gives each outside window a risk score. This report documents the geometry and the scoring. The sightline test is exact geometry; the privacy concept is well established; the need classes, exposure limits, score weights and thresholds are heuristics chosen for the study and are not taken from a standard. Curtains, frosting, reflections, planting and behaviour are not modelled.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nAltman defined privacy as a dynamic process of regulating interaction with others, and identified visual exposure as one of its channels [1]. Hall's proxemics related distance to the kinds of interaction it allows [4]. In housing design, visual privacy is protected by window placement, sill heights, screening and distances between facing windows; London's housing guidance, for example, cites a yardstick of 18–21 m between habitable rooms while warning against applying it rigidly [3]. In healthcare, privacy in examination and patient rooms is a matter of dignity and is addressed by design guidance [5]. A designer needs to know which rooms are seen, from where and through which windows.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Onlookers\n\nOnlookers stand on chosen sides of the plan along a line a set distance from the walls (4.0 m in the audit), every 1.5 m, at 1.6 m eye height above the ground beneath them. Where the site has mapped roads and paths near the plan, onlookers stand on the pavements of each road (half its width plus 1 m out, on both sides) and along each path, every 2 m. In buildings within 40 m, onlookers stand at each storey (every 3 m from 1.5 m) on the faces turned towards the plan, every 3 m and 0.3 m out from the face. A hypothetical building opposite can also be placed.\n\n### 2.2. Sightlines\n\nFor each floor spot on a grid at 1.2 m above the floor (a seated or lying person's head height in the room) and each onlooker, the straight line between them is intersected with the walls of the plan. It must cross an outside wall at an opening whose sill is below and whose head is above the line where it crosses, and it must pass over every piece of furniture taller than the line, and over the site's buildings and the terrain. The study records which onlookers see each spot and through which window.\n\n### 2.3. Room exposure and need\n\nEach room's privacy need is set from its name: high for bedrooms, bathrooms, nurseries, examination, consulting, treatment and patient rooms and changing rooms; medium for living rooms, studies and offices; none for circulation, garages and storage; low otherwise. The user can change any room. A high-need room is flagged for screening when more than 10 % of its floor is seen, a medium-need room when more than 25 % is seen.\n\n### 2.4. Window risk score\n\nFor each window in an outside wall, with need weight w (1, 0.7, 0.35 or 0.1 for high, medium, low and no need):\n\nscore = 100 × w × (0.6 × seen + 0.4 × floor),\n\nwhere seen is the share of onlookers in front of the window who see in through it, each weighted 1 within 8 m and 8/d beyond, and floor is the share of the room's floor that they see through it. Windows are Medium risk at 15 and High at 40.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Privacy study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Onlooker eye height | 1.6 m | Representative standing eye height [6] | Consistent |\n| Spot height in rooms | 1.2 m | Engine choice | Assumption (seated head height) |\n| Street line | User distance (4 m in audit), every 1.5 m | Engine choice | Editable input |\n| Neighbours | Within 40 m, every 3 m per storey | Engine choice | Assumption; consistent in scale with separation guidance [3] |\n| Sightline test | Straight lines through sill–head | Geometry | Exact |\n| Room need classes | From room names | Engine choice | Heuristic; editable |\n| Exposure limits | 10 % (high), 25 % (medium) | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source |\n| Need weights | 1 / 0.7 / 0.35 / 0.1 | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source |\n| Score weights | 0.6 seen, 0.4 floor | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source |\n| Distance weighting | 1 within 8 m, 8/d beyond | Engine choice | Plausible (detail visible falls with distance [4]); not calibrated |\n| Risk classes | Medium ≥ 15, High ≥ 40 | Engine choice | Flagged: no published source |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\n`tests/privacy-windows.test.mjs` tests the window score on constructed cases: the street sees into a bedroom; a store room behind the same glass scores lower; a window with no one in front of it scores 0; a sill at 1.8 m, above the 1.6 m eye, lowers the score; a site building or a terrain bank between the street and the window lowers it; people in a nearby building see in; mapped roads replace the street lines; windows in inside walls are not scored; and the risk classes switch at their thresholds. The sightline test can be checked by hand for any onlooker and spot. There is no validation against people's judgements of privacy.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on the two-storey audit block with onlookers on the street sides, 71.4 % of the floor was seen from outside and 99.4 % of the floor of private rooms; 4 rooms and 4 windows needed screening; of the outside windows, 4 were High, 4 Medium and 1 Low risk. The high exposure is expected for a block with large windows close to a street and no curtains.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Geometry versus perception.** Being in a line of sight is necessary for being seen but not sufficient: at distance, through reflective glass in daylight, or with the room dark, little may be seen. At night with lights on, much more is seen. The distance weighting is a rough stand-in for the loss of detail with distance [4]; a more principled approach would use the visual angle subtended by a person.\n\n**Heuristic thresholds.** No standard defines how much of a bedroom may be seen from the street. The 10 % and 25 % limits and the score classes encode a reasonable design stance and make results comparable between options; they are exposed so that a designer can state and adjust them.\n\n**Names.** Need classes from names fail for generic names (\"Room 1\"), as in the audit model, where most rooms were classed as low need; the user should check them.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Privacy study's sightline geometry is exact for straight lines and openings, and it accounts for site buildings and terrain. Its need classes, limits, weights and thresholds are stated heuristics. It is suitable for comparing window and layout options and for finding which windows need screening, not for demonstrating compliance with a separation standard.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/privacy.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/privacy.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/privacy-windows.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/privacy-windows.test.mjs), [tests/workspace-interior.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-interior.cjs). This article's source: [privacy.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/privacy.md)."}], "references": ["I. Altman, The Environment and Social Behavior: Privacy, Personal Space, Territory, Crowding, Brooks/Cole, Monterey, CA, 1975.", "A.F. Westin, Privacy and Freedom, Atheneum, New York, 1967.", "Mayor of London, Housing Supplementary Planning Guidance, Greater London Authority, London, March 2016.", "E.T. Hall, The Hidden Dimension, Doubleday, Garden City, NY, 1966.", "Department of Health, Health Building Note 00-01: General design guidance for healthcare buildings, Department of Health, London, 2014.", "A.R. Tilley, Henry Dreyfuss Associates, The Measure of Man and Woman: Human Factors in Design, revised ed., Wiley, New York, 2002."], "category": "research", "simulation": "privacy", "summary": "How the Privacy study finds which parts of each room can be seen from the street and from neighbouring buildings through the windows, and ranks each outside window by a risk score. The report gives the geometry, the sources of the privacy concepts, and flags the room-need classes, limits, weights and thresholds as SimuSite's heuristics.", "image": "img/home/privacy.webp", "imageAlt": "Floor plan shaded by how much of each room is seen from outside, with windows marked high, medium or low risk"}}, {"id": "report-cameras", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Security camera coverage from DORI pixel densities, sightlines with height and greedy placement", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Video surveillance", "DORI", "IEC 62676-4", "Coverage optimisation", "Sensor placement"], "highlights": ["Detail levels follow IEC 62676-4 DORI: detect 25, observe 62.5, recognise 125 and identify 250 pixels per metre.", "Each camera's range is the distance at which its horizontal pixels across its field of view still give the required density at the watched height.", "Dome (100°), bullet (85°) and fisheye (360°) cameras are modelled with 2560 horizontal pixels; prices of $169.99, $199.99 and $589.99 are examples.", "Candidate positions on walls (16 directions) and the ceiling are chosen greedily with swaps, a standard approach to the maximum coverage problem.", "Lighting, lens distortion, image quality and camera models are not represented; results are geometric coverage."], "abstract": "Video surveillance design must decide how many cameras are needed, where they go and what detail they provide [1]. IEC 62676-4 expresses detail as pixel density on the subject and defines four levels, detection, observation, recognition and identification (DORI) [2]. SimuSite's Camera coverage study combines these with the floor plan: each camera type's range is the distance at which it still delivers the required pixels per metre at the height being watched, sightlines from the mounting height are cut by walls and hidden behind lower furniture, and candidate positions on the walls and ceiling are selected greedily, with swaps, to cover the most floor for a given number of cameras or to reach a coverage target [3, 4]. This report documents the method and its constants. The DORI densities are taken from IEC 62676-4 and the range formulas are standard pinhole and equidistant-fisheye geometry; the greedy selection is a well-understood heuristic for the maximum coverage problem. The camera specifications and prices are example values, not products. Results are geometric coverage; lighting, lens distortion, compression and installation constraints decide image quality in practice.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nThe pixel density on a target, in pixels per metre at the target's distance, is the accepted measure of whether a camera can serve a purpose: seeing that someone is present, what they are doing, who a known person is, or the identity of a stranger [1, 2]. IEC 62676-4 sets these at 25, 62.5, 125 and 250 px/m [2]. The placement of cameras to cover a floor is a version of the art gallery problem, which is NP-hard in general [5]; practical methods discretise candidate positions and target points and solve a maximum coverage problem, for which the greedy algorithm achieves at least 1 − 1/e of the optimum [3, 4, 6].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Range from pixel density\n\nFor a rectilinear camera with N horizontal pixels and horizontal field of view φ, the pixel density at slant distance s on the optical axis is N / (2 s tan(φ/2)); the maximum slant distance for a density p is s = N / (2 p tan(φ/2)). For a 360° fisheye, the study spreads the N pixels over 180° (an equidistant projection across the image circle), giving s = N / (π p). The horizontal range at the watched height z_t from a camera at height z_c is √(s² − (z_c − z_t)²). For example, a 2560-pixel dome with a 100° field at the recognition level (125 px/m) reaches s = 8.6 m; mounted at 2.7 m watching a 1.0 m target, the horizontal range is 8.4 m. The fisheye reaches s = 6.5 m.\n\n### 2.2. Sightlines\n\nFrom the camera position at its mounting height, rays are cast as in the Isovist study: walls of 2 m or more stop the view (they are taken to reach the ceiling), and lower objects hide the floor behind them down to the watched height. Rays are clipped at the floor being watched so that cameras do not count floor seen through glass or doors. The field of view limits the rays of dome and bullet cameras.\n\n### 2.3. Floor and candidates\n\nThe floor to watch is everything inside the walls, less furniture, with doorways up to about 1.1 m bridged. Candidate positions are points 0.15–0.35 m inside the walls about every 0.8 m, for dome and bullet cameras in 16 directions each, and a 1.25 m grid on the ceiling for fisheyes. Each candidate's coverage is listed on a grid of watch points every 25 cm.\n\n### 2.4. Selection\n\nIn budget mode, the study adds cameras one at a time, each time picking the candidate that covers the most uncovered watch points, and after each addition tries single swaps until no swap covers more [3, 4]. In target mode, it adds cameras until the target share is reached, then tops up for slivers the coarse watch points missed. Coverage is reported on the floor cells, with the number of cameras seeing each spot, the coverage obtained for each number of cameras, and the cost at the example prices.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings of the Camera coverage study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Detect, observe, recognise, identify | 25, 62.5, 125, 250 px/m | IEC 62676-4 [2] | Sourced |\n| Rectilinear range | N / (2 p tan(φ/2)) | Pinhole camera geometry [1] | Sourced (on axis; less at the image edge) |\n| Fisheye range | N / (π p) | Equidistant projection [7] | Approximation; real fisheye lenses vary |\n| Horizontal pixels | 2560 for all types | Engine choice | Example value (a common 4-megapixel sensor width) |\n| Fields of view | Dome 100°, bullet 85°, fisheye 360° | Engine choice | Example values |\n| Prices | $169.99, $199.99, $589.99 | Engine choice, labelled example prices | Flagged: not linked to products |\n| Mounting height | 2.7 m default | Engine choice | Editable input |\n| Watched height | 1.0 m default | Engine choice | Editable input; face height is usually 1.5–1.7 m |\n| Walls stopping the view | ≥ 2 m | Engine choice | Assumption |\n| Candidates | Wall every 0.8 m × 16 directions; ceiling 1.25 m grid | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Selection | Greedy with swaps | Maximum coverage heuristics [3, 4, 6] | Sourced method |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe Workspace interior tests check that the study places cameras, reports coverage and cost, and responds to the detail level and camera types. The range formulas were checked by hand for this report (§2.1). No comparison with a commercial camera design tool or with measured images has been made.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit of 20 November 2026 on level 1 of the audit block (230.9 m² of floor), four cameras at the recognition level, mounted at 2.7 m and watching at 1.0 m, covered 62.7 % of the floor; the study chose four fisheyes, with 12.8 m² seen by two or more, at an example cost of $2,359.96 (4 × $589.99). The partial coverage is expected: the block has many small rooms, and each room needs its own camera.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Range is the optimistic case.** The rectilinear formula gives the density on the optical axis; at the edges of a wide field of view, distances are longer and densities lower. Lens distortion, especially in fisheyes, makes density vary across the image [7]. Low light, backlighting, motion blur and compression reduce effective detail [1]. Range should therefore be read as an upper bound, and identification in particular should be confirmed with the chosen camera.\n\n**Watched height.** The default of 1.0 m suits detecting and observing people, including children; recognition and identification depend on faces, which are usually at 1.5–1.7 m. A higher watched height lengthens the horizontal range for a given slant distance.\n\n**Optimisation.** Greedy selection with swaps finds good, not necessarily optimal, sets. For the small instances of a single floor, it is close to optimal in practice [4], and the coverage-against-cameras curve it produces is useful for deciding how many cameras are worth buying. Budget mode maximises coverage for a number of cameras, not coverage per dollar.\n\n**Privacy and law.** Coverage is only one consideration. Data-protection rules and privacy expectations constrain where cameras may look; the study does not assess these.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Camera coverage study uses the IEC 62676-4 DORI densities, standard range geometry and an established coverage heuristic. Its camera specifications and prices are examples that should be replaced by the products under consideration. Results show geometric coverage at a stated detail level and are suitable for comparing layouts and estimating camera counts in early design.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/cameras.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/cameras.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/workspace-interior.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-interior.cjs), [tests/workspace-fixes-1115.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-fixes-1115.cjs). This article's source: [cameras.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/cameras.md)."}], "references": ["H. Kruegle, CCTV Surveillance: Video Practices and Technology, 2nd ed., Butterworth-Heinemann, Burlington, MA, 2007.", "IEC 62676-4:2014, Video surveillance systems for use in security applications — Part 4: Application guidelines, International Electrotechnical Commission, Geneva, 2014.", "R. Church, C. ReVelle, The maximal covering location problem, Pap. Reg. Sci. Assoc. 32 (1) (1974) 101–118. [doi:10.1007/BF01942293](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01942293)", "A.T. Murray, K. Kim, J.W. Davis, R. Machiraju, R. Parent, Coverage optimization to support security monitoring, Comput. Environ. Urban Syst. 31 (2) (2007) 133–147.", "J. O'Rourke, Art Gallery Theorems and Algorithms, Oxford University Press, New York, 1987.", "G.L. Nemhauser, L.A. Wolsey, M.L. Fisher, An analysis of approximations for maximizing submodular set functions—I, Math. Program. 14 (1978) 265–294. [doi:10.1007/BF01588971](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971)", "J. Kannala, S.S. Brandt, A generic camera model and calibration method for conventional, wide-angle, and fish-eye lenses, IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell. 28 (8) (2006) 1335–1340. [doi:10.1109/TPAMI.2006.153](https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2006.153)"], "category": "research", "simulation": "cameras", "summary": "How the Camera coverage study finds how much floor a set of cameras sees at a required level of detail, using the DORI pixel densities of IEC 62676-4 to set each camera's range, height-aware sightlines, and greedy placement with swaps. The report gives the sources of the detail levels and the optimisation, and flags the camera specifications and prices as example values.", "image": "img/home/cameras.webp", "imageAlt": "Floor plan coloured by how many cameras see each spot, with camera positions and their fields of view"}}, {"id": "report-fire-evacuation", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Fire evacuation in the Workspace: the sources of the egress parameters and an audit of the shipped engine", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 20 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Evacuation modelling", "Required safe escape time", "Hydraulic model", "Pre-movement time", "Progressive horizontal evacuation"], "highlights": ["Movement follows the SFPE hydraulic model: speed and specific flow fall with density; doors and stairs have specific flow limits.", "Detection, alarm and pre-movement times follow PD 7974-6 by occupancy scenario; occupant loads follow IBC Table 1004.5.", "A full verification article reports 18 component tests after IMO MSC.1/Circ.1533 within 0–3 % (one at 5.6 %) and a 3-storey SFPE comparison within 5.6 %.", "The engine's unit tests and the Workspace tests passed in this audit, except one check of an old page address.", "Staffing, preparation times and occupant groups dominate the uncertainty; results compare designs and are not predictions or code checks."], "abstract": "The Fire evacuation study estimates the required safe escape time (RSET) of a building, either with everyone leaving at once or by a hospital's progressive horizontal evacuation followed by descent. It is a grid-based stochastic model with flow limits from the hydraulic model of the SFPE Handbook [1], detection, alarm and pre-movement times from PD 7974-6 [2], occupant loads from IBC Table 1004.5 [3], and occupant groups whose speeds come from published measurements [4, 5]. Because the study already has a detailed verification article, \"How accurate is the fire evacuation simulation?\", published on this blog on 10 November 2026, this report is brief. It summarises the sources of each parameter, records the results of the November 2026 audit of all SimuSite simulations, and directs readers to the article for the verification tests, sensitivity analyses and the hospital case study.", "body": "## 1. Method in brief\n\nThe plan of each floor becomes a walking grid. Occupants are placed by room use at the IBC occupant load factors, or entered by the user, in groups (adults, older adults, children, walking-aid users and wheelchair users). After detection and alarm, each occupant waits a pre-movement time drawn from a distribution for the scenario, then walks the shortest available route to an exit and an assembly point. Speeds fall with local density, and doors and stairs pass people at their specific flows, following the hydraulic model [1]. In hospital mode, inpatients are moved by staff, first horizontally through smoke doors into a neighbouring compartment and then down the stairs, with preparation times by care level. Each scenario is run many times with random fire locations and blocked exits, and the median and 10th and 90th percentiles of RSET are reported.\n\n## 2. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Main parameters of the Fire evacuation study.\n\n| Parameter | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Speed–density and specific flow relations; door and stair flow limits | SFPE Handbook, 5th ed., hydraulic model [1] | Sourced |\n| Detection, alarm, pre-movement by scenario | PD 7974-6:2019 [2] | Sourced |\n| Occupant load factors | IBC 2021 Table 1004.5 [3] | Sourced |\n| Speeds of people with reduced mobility | Measured values in the literature [4, 5] | Sourced; see the verification article |\n| Inpatient mobility shares, staff numbers, preparation times | Engine defaults, editable | Assumptions, labelled as such in the results |\n| Exit found blocked | 15 % of runs (one exit) | Assumption, labelled |\n\n## 3. Verification\n\nThe verification article reports 18 component tests after IMO MSC.1/Circ.1533 [6] and the verification categories of NIST TN 1822 [7] and ISO 20414 [8]: speeds, flows and pre-movement distributions agree with analytical values within 0–3 %, and a three-storey building agrees with an SFPE hand calculation within 5.6 %. It also finds that the 0.5 m grid keeps only 1 m of a 1.8 m clear corridor (0.25 m keeps 1.5 m), that the 90th percentile needs about 50 runs to settle, and that staffing, patient preparation and occupant groups dominate the uncertainty.\n\n## 4. Audit of 21 November 2026\n\n`tests/egress-engine.test.mjs` passed all five of its tests: compartments, stairs and population are found in a ward block; horizontal relocation through the smoke door is repeatable; the fire floor and those above leave by the stair; occupants are placed by room use with stages in order; and inpatients take far longer than everyone walking. `tests/workspace-fire-evacuation.cjs` passed its six checks of the study in the Workspace (building scene, everyone-out run, horizontal-then-down run with a median of 17.2 min, 3D view, playback and saving) and then timed out on a final check that an old page address redirects to the Workspace, which is a navigation test rather than an engine test.\n\nIn the end-to-end run of 20 November 2026 on the two-storey audit block, the study reported a median RSET of 17.1 min (10th percentile 12.7 min, 90th percentile 21.7 min) for 52 people with 26 inpatients moved by 7 staff. The result is dominated by the staff-assisted movement of inpatients, as the verification article explains. The study states its assumptions in its results and does not check code compliance; the separate Code check does.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nThe Fire evacuation study is the most thoroughly verified of SimuSite's simulations. Its movement and timing parameters are sourced, its engine passes its tests, and its uncertain inputs are labelled as assumptions. For the evidence, read the verification article.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/interior/egress.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/egress.js), [mvp/js/interior/egresscode.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/interior/egresscode.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/egress-engine.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/egress-engine.test.mjs), [tests/workspace-fire-evacuation.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-fire-evacuation.cjs). This article's source: [fire-evacuation.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/fire-evacuation.md)."}], "references": ["S.M.V. Gwynne, E.R. Rosenbaum, Employing the hydraulic model in assessing emergency movement, in: M.J. Hurley (Ed.), SFPE Handbook of Fire Protection Engineering, 5th ed., Springer, New York, 2016.", "BSI, PD 7974-6:2019, The application of fire safety engineering principles to fire safety design of buildings — Part 6: Human factors: Life safety strategies — Occupant evacuation, behaviour and condition, BSI, London, 2019.", "International Code Council, 2021 International Building Code, Table 1004.5, Maximum floor area allowances per occupant, ICC, Washington, DC, 2020.", "K.E. Boyce, T.J. Shields, G.W.H. Silcock, Toward the characterization of building occupancies for fire safety engineering: capabilities of disabled people moving horizontally and on an incline, Fire Technol. 35 (1999) 51–67.", "A.R. Larusdottir, A.S. Dederichs, Evacuation of children: movement on stairs and on horizontal plane, Fire Technol. 48 (2012) 43–53.", "International Maritime Organization, MSC.1/Circ.1533, Revised guidelines on evacuation analysis for new and existing passenger ships, IMO, London, 2016.", "E. Ronchi, E.D. Kuligowski, P.A. Reneke, R.D. Peacock, D. Nilsson, The process of verification and validation of building fire evacuation models, NIST Technical Note 1822, National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, 2013. [doi:10.6028/NIST.TN.1822](https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.TN.1822)", "ISO 20414:2020, Fire safety engineering — Verification and validation protocol for building fire evacuation models, International Organization for Standardization, Geneva, 2020."], "category": "research", "simulation": "fire-evacuation", "summary": "A short data report on the Fire evacuation study that lists the sources of its walking speeds, flows, pre-movement times and occupant loads, records the results of the November 2026 audit, and points to the full verification article, which tests the engine against IMO MSC.1/Circ.1533 and an SFPE hand calculation.", "image": "img/home/fire-evacuation.jpg", "imageAlt": "Evacuation routes from rooms to exits and assembly points on two floors"}}, {"id": "report-vehicle-swept-path", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Vehicle swept paths from a kinematic bicycle model with pure-pursuit steering and computed trailer off-tracking", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 21 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Swept path analysis", "Off-tracking", "Design vehicle", "Pure pursuit", "Site access"], "highlights": ["The rear-axle centre is the reference point, so the least turning radius is R_min = wheelbase / tan(maximum steering angle).", "Steering follows pure pursuit with a look-ahead equal to the wheelbase (1.5–12 m), limited to the maximum angle and to the rate set by the lock-to-lock time at the stated speed.", "A trailer's heading follows its hitch: φ′ = [sin(θ − φ) + h (tan δ / L) cos(θ − φ)] / L_t per metre, so off-tracking is computed.", "Against analytical steady turns, the engine is within 0.4–0.9 mm for a car and 0.5 mm for a semi-trailer's axle radius.", "The presets are typical dimensions, not manufacturer data or AASHTO or TAC design vehicles; the WB-20-type preset should be checked against the Green Book before use."], "abstract": "Swept path analysis checks whether a design vehicle can follow a route, through a site entrance, a loading bay or a fire access road, and how much space its body and wheels sweep [1, 2]. Commercial tools simulate the vehicle with kinematic models and standard design vehicles. SimuSite's Vehicle swept path study does the same for early design: it rounds the corners of a drawn route by arcs no tighter than the vehicle can turn, drives a kinematic bicycle model along it with pure-pursuit steering limited in angle and rate [3, 4], computes the heading of any trailer from its hitch [5], and checks the clearance of the body outlines to every obstacle at every step. It reports feasibility with the stations of any infeasible stretch, the tightest radius, the least clearance and where it occurs, the swept area, and exports to DXF. This report documents the equations and their sources. The kinematics are standard and the engine reproduces analytical steady-turn geometry to within a millimetre in its tests. The vehicle presets are typical dimensions rather than the design vehicles of AASHTO or TAC, and should be replaced by the vehicle the project must serve.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nAt low speed, tyres roll without significant slip, and a vehicle's motion is described by kinematics alone: the rear axle moves along its heading, and the heading changes at a rate set by the steering angle and the wheelbase (the bicycle model) [4]. The rear wheels track inside the front wheels on a turn, and the trailer of an articulated vehicle tracks further inside its tractor; this off-tracking determines the width a turn needs [1, 5]. Road design guides publish design vehicles and their minimum turning paths [1, 2]. Swept path software combines these models with a driver model that steers the vehicle along a path.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Reference path\n\nThe route is a polyline drawn on the plan. Each corner between two segments driven in the same direction is replaced by a circular arc of radius 1.25 × R_min by default (less where the segments leave no room, or a radius set by the user), where R_min = wheelbase / tan(δ_max) is the radius of the rear-axle centre at full lock. A corner between a forward and a reversing segment is a cusp, where the vehicle stops and may turn the wheel at standstill.\n\n### 2.2. Vehicle and steering\n\nThe vehicle is a kinematic bicycle model with the rear-axle centre at (x, y) and heading θ: per metre travelled, x′ = cos θ, y′ = sin θ and θ′ = tan δ / L, where L is the wheelbase and δ the steering angle [4]. Steering follows pure pursuit: the target is the route point a look-ahead distance L_d ahead (the wheelbase, between 1.5 and 12 m), and the steering angle is that of the arc through the target, δ = atan(2 L sin α / L_d), where α is the angle to the target [3]. The angle is limited to ±δ_max and its rate to 2 δ_max / t_lock per second at the stated speed (8 km/h by default). Where the vehicle cannot stay within 0.30 m of the route, a corner is tighter than R_min, or a trailer folds past its articulation limit, the stretch is reported infeasible with its stations; the route is never forced.\n\n### 2.3. Trailer\n\nFor an articulated vehicle with the hitch at distance h ahead of the tractor's rear axle and a trailer wheelbase L_t from kingpin to trailer axle, the trailer heading φ changes per metre as\n\nφ′ = [sin(θ − φ) + h (tan δ / L) cos(θ − φ)] / L_t,\n\nwhich is the standard kinematic trailer equation [5]. In a steady turn with the hitch over the rear axle, the trailer axle runs on a circle of radius √(R² − L_t²) inside the tractor's rear axle radius R.\n\n### 2.4. Outlines and clearance\n\nAt each step the body outlines of the tractor and trailer and the four wheel positions are recorded. The swept envelope is rasterised on a fine grid (0.02–0.05 m), and every body outline is tested against every obstacle outline (buildings, walls and drawn obstacles) for the least gap. Stretches in collision or nearer than the clearance threshold (0.30 m by default) are reported. An option drives around obstacles with a planned path.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Settings and vehicle data of the Vehicle swept path study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vehicle model | Kinematic bicycle | Rajamani [4] | Sourced; valid at low speed |\n| Steering controller | Pure pursuit | Coulter [3] | Sourced |\n| Look-ahead | L, limited to 1.5–12 m | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Trailer equation | As in §2.3 | Kinematic trailer model [5] | Sourced |\n| Corner rounding | 1.25 × R_min | Engine choice, \"as CAD swept-path tools do\" | Editable |\n| Tracking tolerance | 0.30 m | Engine choice | Numerical setting |\n| Clearance threshold | 0.30 m | Engine choice | Common practice; editable |\n| Speed | 8 km/h | Engine choice | Typical manoeuvring speed |\n| Passenger car | L 2.9 m, width 1.85 m, δ 35° (R_min 4.14 m) | Typical dimensions | Plausible; AASHTO P vehicle is 5.8 m long [1] |\n| Box, medium and heavy rigid trucks, van, pickup, bus, fire truck, mixer | Typical dimensions | Engine choice; the mixer \"after the reference drawing\" | Flagged: not manufacturer or code design vehicles |\n| WB-20-type semi-trailer | Tractor L 6.2 m, kingpin to axle 12.4 m, length 22.7 m, δ 28.2° | \"After the WB-20 in the reference drawing\" | Flagged: the drawing is not named; check against AASHTO [1] |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\n`tests/swept-path.cjs` compares the engine with closed-form geometry. In our run on 21 November 2026 all eight checks passed:\n\n- A passenger car on a 10 m radius: rear axle within 0.4 mm of the circle; outer front corner radius 11.584 m (±0.9 mm of the analytical value); inner side 9.075 m (±0.4 mm); swept width 2.51 m; the rasterised envelope matches within its 0.02 m cell.\n- A 30 m straight: a band 1.85 m wide, equal to the car's width.\n- A 2 m corner: reported infeasible over the right stations; with no radius set, the corner is rounded to 5.2 m and driven.\n- A semi-trailer on a 30 m radius: trailer axle on 27.317 m (within 0.5 mm of √(30² − 12.4²)), off-tracking 2.68 m; a 10° articulation limit is flagged.\n- Clearance to a box of 0.575 m is reported feasible, 0.175 m is flagged under 0.30 m, and an overlap is reported as a collision from 6.20 to 13.00 m (hand calculation 6.15–13.05 m).\n- A forward-then-reverse route has one cusp; two cars crossing meet at 6.9 s; the same input gives the same output.\n\n`tests/workspace-swept-path.cjs` passed its checks in the Workspace: a box truck along a drawn route (feasible, tightest radius 6.63 m against the vehicle's least 5.50 m, least clearance 0.77 m, swept area 371.4 m²), detection of a route through a building and a drive around it, a 3 m corner reported infeasible, two vehicles meeting, and JSON, DXF and SVG exports. In the end-to-end audit on 20 November 2026, with no route drawn, the study asked the user to draw one, as intended.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Kinematics only.** At the low speeds of site access and loading, tyre slip is small and kinematic models are the basis of design-vehicle turning templates [1, 2]. At higher speeds, or for long trailers with steered axles, dynamic effects and steering systems not represented here matter.\n\n**Design vehicles.** The presets are labelled as typical dimensions. For a road authority or fire department submission, the vehicle must be the one specified: the AASHTO Green Book design vehicles in the United States [1], the TAC design vehicles in Canada [2], or the local fire apparatus. The WB-20-type preset's overall length of 22.7 m and its outer front corner radius at full lock of about 14.7 m are of the same order as the AASHTO WB-20 interstate semi-trailer, but its source drawing is not named and the dimensions and turning radius should be checked against the Green Book before use [1].\n\n**Driver model.** Pure pursuit with a look-ahead of one wheelbase cuts corners slightly, as drivers do; the corner rounding at 1.25 R_min reflects a driver who does not turn at full lock. Swept paths from commercial tools use similar conventions, and results can differ by tens of centimetres depending on them.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Vehicle swept path study uses standard kinematic models and a standard steering controller, and reproduces analytical turning geometry to within a millimetre. Its vehicle presets are typical dimensions and must be replaced by the project's design vehicle for any submission. It is well suited to testing site access and loading layouts in early design.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/swept-path.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/swept-path.js), [mvp/js/vehicles.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/vehicles.js), [mvp/js/route-plan.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/route-plan.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/swept-path.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/swept-path.cjs), [tests/workspace-swept-path.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-swept-path.cjs). This article's source: [vehicle-swept-path.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/vehicle-swept-path.md)."}], "references": ["AASHTO, A Policy on Geometric Design of Highways and Streets, 7th ed. (\"Green Book\"), American Association of State Highway and Transportation Officials, Washington, DC, 2018.", "Transportation Association of Canada, Geometric Design Guide for Canadian Roads, TAC, Ottawa, 2017.", "R.C. Coulter, Implementation of the pure pursuit path tracking algorithm, Technical Report CMU-RI-TR-92-01, Robotics Institute, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, 1992.", "R. Rajamani, Vehicle Dynamics and Control, 2nd ed., Springer, New York, 2012. [doi:10.1007/978-1-4614-1433-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-1433-9)", "SAE International, SAE J695, Turning Ability and Off Tracking — Motor Vehicles, SAE, Warrendale, PA."], "category": "research", "simulation": "vehicle-swept-path", "summary": "How the Vehicle swept path study drives a design vehicle along a drawn route with a rate-limited pure-pursuit steering controller, computes the off-tracking of trailers, and checks clearance to buildings and other obstacles. The report gives the equations and their sources, reports exact checks against analytical turning geometry, and flags the vehicle presets as typical dimensions that must be checked against the design vehicle of the jurisdiction.", "image": "img/home/vehicle-swept-path.jpg", "imageAlt": "The swept envelope of a truck turning into a service yard, with wheel tracks and clearance warnings"}}, {"id": "report-parking-layout", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Generating parking layouts: stall and aisle modules, a seeded search over row directions and exact stall assignment", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 21 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Parking design", "Layout generation", "Accessible parking", "Dynamic programming", "Site planning"], "highlights": ["Default stalls are 2.5 × 5.0 m with a 6.0 m aisle at 90°; compact 2.3 × 4.6 m and large 2.7 × 5.5 m are optional; angled stalls use 4.8 m (60°) or 4.0 m (45°) one-way aisles.", "Row directions come from the area's longest edges and the structural grid; offsets are searched with a fixed seed, so a layout repeats exactly.", "Stall types are assigned exactly per row by count, priority or dynamic programming to target shares; every stall is checked again at the end.", "The accessible access aisle is 1.525 m, the 60 in of the 2010 ADA Standards (it was 1.5 m at the time of the audit), and each option notes when it has fewer accessible or van-accessible stalls than ADA Table 208.2 asks for its total.", "Tests match hand counts exactly (86 stalls in a 60 × 40 m lot at 90°, 38 at 60°, 30 at 45°)."], "abstract": "Surface and structured parking occupies a large share of many sites, and its layout determines both capacity and circulation [1, 2]. Parking design manuals give stall and aisle dimensions for each stall angle, and accessibility standards set the size, number and location of accessible stalls [1, 3]. SimuSite's Parking layout study generates layouts for a drawn area, surface or underground. It lays double-loaded modules of stall, aisle and stall across the area at candidate row directions and offsets, cuts aisles to the free floor and connects them with cross aisles, keeps only aisles reached from an entrance, assigns stall types along each row exactly, and checks every stall at the end. It reports the stall count by type, the area per stall and three distinct options, and exports to DXF. This report documents the method and its dimensions. The geometry is verified against hand counts. The default dimensions are consistent with European practice and somewhat smaller than common North American practice; the accessible access aisle is marginally narrower than the ADA minimum, and the number of accessible stalls is set by the user rather than by the ADA scoping table. The dimensions are editable assumptions, not a bylaw.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nA parking module is the width of one aisle with a row of stalls on each side; its width and the stall pitch along the aisle depend on the stall angle, stall size and aisle width [1, 2]. Layout design chooses the row direction and position in the site to maximise usable stalls subject to circulation. The problem is a form of rectangle packing with constraints, generally solved by designers with templates and by software with heuristics [2]. Accessible stalls in the United States must follow the 2010 ADA Standards: an 8 ft (2,440 mm) wide car stall with a 60 in (1,525 mm) access aisle, or a van stall with a wider stall or aisle, in numbers set by Table 208.2, on the shortest accessible route to the entrance [3].\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Modules and candidates\n\nFor a stall of width w and length l at angle α to the aisle, the stall is drawn as a parallelogram w / sin α wide along the aisle and l sin α + w cos α deep. Aisles are 6.0 m for 90° stalls and for two-way traffic, 4.8 m one-way at 60° and 4.0 m one-way at 45°. Candidate row directions are those of the area's longest edges, the structural column grid when underground, and one the user sets; each is tried at several offsets across the module (and at offsets that put column lines between modules). The search uses a seeded random generator (mulberry32), so the same input gives the same layouts.\n\n### 2.2. Aisles, connections and reach\n\nFor each candidate, the aisle strips are cut to the free floor (inside the area by the setback, clear of holes, obstacles and columns), and pieces shorter than two stalls are dropped. Cross aisles join neighbouring aisle lines at the ends of their overlap. Aisles within reach of an entrance, directly or by a connector across the rows, are kept; only rows along reached aisles receive stalls.\n\n### 2.3. Stall assignment\n\nAlong each row, the free runs are found. Stall types are assigned per run exactly for the run lengths in one of three modes: (A) the maximum count; (B) the user's first type (for example large) first, then the rest; (C) a mix closest to target shares, by dynamic programming across runs over the counts of the tracked types. Accessible stalls with their access aisles are placed first, in the runs nearest an entrance. At the end every stall is checked again for being inside the area, clear of obstacles, aisles and other stalls, and on a reached aisle; a stall that fails is marked invalid and not counted. The best distinct candidates are shown as options A, B and C.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Dimensions of the Parking layout study (all editable).\n\n| Parameter | Value | Reference practice | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Standard stall | 2.5 × 5.0 m | European guidance commonly uses about 2.5 × 5.0 m [4]; US guidance often 2.6–2.75 × 5.5 m [1] | Assumption, consistent with European practice |\n| Compact stall | 2.3 × 4.6 m | Compact stalls in US practice about 2.3–2.4 × 4.6–4.9 m [1] | Assumption |\n| Large / SUV | 2.7 × 5.5 m | US standard stall sizes [1] | Assumption |\n| Aisle at 90°, two-way | 6.0 m | European guidance about 6.0–6.5 m [4]; US about 7.0–7.3 m [1] | Assumption, at the narrow end |\n| One-way aisle at 60° / 45° | 4.8 m / 4.0 m | Of the order given in design manuals [1, 4] | Assumption |\n| Accessible stall and aisle | 2.5 m + 1.525 m | ADA 2010: 2,440 mm stall + 1,525 mm aisle [3] | Meets ADA dimensions (aisle corrected from 1.5 m on 21 November 2026) |\n| Van-accessible stall and aisle | 2.5 m + 2.5 m | ADA 2010: 2,440 mm stall + 2,440 mm aisle (or 3,350 + 1,525) [3] | Meets ADA dimensions |\n| Accessible count | 2 by default; ADA Table 208.2 and 208.2.4 count checked on each option | ADA Table 208.2 scales with total [3] | User-set; a note shows any shortfall against Table 208.2 |\n| Search | Seeded, several offsets per direction | Engine choice | \"The best found, not a proven optimum\" |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\n`tests/parking.cjs` compares the generator with hand layouts. In our run on 21 November 2026 all checks passed:\n\n- A 60 × 40 m area at 90°: 86 stalls by hand and by the generator (rows of 24, 19, 19 and 24); the free search also finds 86 in 30 positions, with 45 % of the area in stalls.\n- At 60°, a pitch of 2.887 m and a depth of 5.580 m give 38 stalls, equal to the hand count; at 45°, 3.536 m and 5.303 m give 30, equal to the hand count. These pitches and depths are w / sin α and l sin α + w cos α exactly.\n- An L-shaped area with a hole gives options of 62, 59 and 51 stalls, every stall inside, clear and reached, with the 2 accessible stalls 9 m and 6 m from the entrance.\n- Mode B fills the rows with large stalls first (64); mode C reaches 21 compact stalls of 72 (29.2 % for a 30 % target) and rescales target shares that add up to 160 %.\n- Underground, 60 stalls avoid all 24 columns and the core.\n\n`tests/workspace-parking.cjs` passed its Workspace checks (43 stalls in 1,288 m² with 2 accessible, 30.0 m² per stall; three options; editing stall types; DXF layers; a delivery van driven down an aisle without hitting a stall; and 42 underground stalls clear of 66 columns). In the end-to-end audit on 20 November 2026, with no area drawn, the study asked the user to draw one, as intended.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Dimensions are local.** Stall and aisle sizes vary between countries, cities and operators; North American practice uses larger stalls and wider aisles than European practice [1, 4]. The defaults give more stalls than a typical US bylaw would allow and should be set to local requirements before counts are reported.\n\n**Accessible parking.** At the time of the audit the access aisle was 1.5 m, 25 mm short of the 2010 ADA Standards; it is now 1.525 m. The number of accessible stalls stays a user setting, because local rules differ, but each option now compares it with ADA Table 208.2 for the option's total, and with 208.2.4, which asks for one in six of them, and at least one, to be van-accessible [3]; a shortfall is listed in the option's notes. A unit test, `tests/parking-ada.test.mjs`, checks the aisle and the table's steps (1 space up to 25, 2 up to 50, ..., 2 % from 501 to 1,000, 20 plus one per 100 above 1,000). The two parking test files above pass after the change. The study places accessible stalls nearest an entrance, which follows the location requirement in spirit; it does not check slopes or the accessible route.\n\n**Optimality.** A seeded search over directions and offsets finds good layouts but not proven optima; exact assignment within each row means the remaining loss is in the choice of rows. The study says so in its results.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Parking layout study's geometry and stall assignment are verified exactly against hand layouts. Its dimensions are editable assumptions consistent with European practice; for US projects, the stall and aisle values should be set to the bylaw, and the accessible count raised to what the option's notes ask for under ADA Table 208.2.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/parking.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/parking.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/parking.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/parking.cjs), [tests/workspace-parking.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-parking.cjs), [tests/parking-ada.test.mjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/parking-ada.test.mjs). This article's source: [parking-layout.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/parking-layout.md)."}], "references": ["Urban Land Institute, National Parking Association, The Dimensions of Parking, 5th ed., Urban Land Institute, Washington, DC, 2010.", "A.P. Chrest, M.S. Smith, S. Bhuyan, M. Iqbal, D.R. Monahan, Parking Structures: Planning, Design, Construction, Maintenance and Repair, 3rd ed., Springer, Boston, 2001.", "U.S. Department of Justice, 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design, sections 208 and 502, Washington, DC, 2010.", "FGSV, Empfehlungen für Anlagen des ruhenden Verkehrs (EAR 05), Forschungsgesellschaft für Straßen- und Verkehrswesen, Cologne, 2005 (amended 2012)."], "category": "research", "simulation": "parking-layout", "summary": "How the Parking layout study fills a drawn area with double-loaded rows of stalls, chooses among row directions and offsets with a seeded search, assigns stall types exactly per row, and checks every stall. The report gives the stall and aisle dimensions and their relation to published practice and the ADA Standards, reports the hand-checked tests, and flags the dimensions as editable assumptions rather than a bylaw.", "image": "img/home/parking-layout.jpg", "imageAlt": "A parking area filled with rows of stalls and aisles, with accessible stalls near the entrance"}}, {"id": "report-structure", "kind": "blog", "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T00:00:00-08:00", "content": {"format": "article", "journal": "SimuSite Research Notes", "journalNote": "Technical note · not peer reviewed", "articleType": "Method and data report", "title": "Structural frame screening from the drawn walls: layout rules, NBC 2020 load combinations and a linear 3D frame analysis", "authors": [{"name": "SimuSite Studio", "aff": "a"}], "affiliations": [{"key": "a", "text": "SimuSite, building performance software for architects. Prepared with AI assistance; human review pending."}], "history": ["Published online 21 November 2026", "Simulation audit of 21 November 2026", "Not submitted to a journal; not peer reviewed"], "keywords": ["Structural layout", "Matrix structural analysis", "Load combinations", "National Building Code of Canada", "Early design"], "highlights": ["Walls of stone, masonry or concrete 150 mm or thicker carry the floors; a column grid fills the rest at the chosen span.", "Materials follow Canadian standards: steel 350W (E 200 GPa), 30 MPa concrete (E 24.6 GPa = 4500√f′c), glulam 24f-E (E 12.4 GPa, f_b 30.6 MPa).", "Live loads follow NBC 2020 (residential 1.9, office and education 2.4, retail 4.8, industrial 6.0 kPa) and combinations follow NBC Table 4.1.3.2-A, without earthquake.", "The solver reproduces cantilever and simply supported beam results exactly and closes equilibrium to 10⁻¹⁵.", "Flagged: roof snow fixed at 1.0 kPa, wind pressure 0.6 kPa, wall strengths and soil bearing 150 kPa. Two audit findings were corrected: the frame now takes the live load of the chosen occupancy, and no wind sway is reported where bearing walls hold the floors."], "abstract": "Architects make structural decisions in early design, spans, grids, wall thicknesses and the choice of system, long before an engineer sizes members [1]. SimuSite's Structure study supports those decisions. From the drawn walls it identifies bearing walls, lays out a column grid for a chosen system (concrete flat plate, steel frame or mass timber), sizes columns, beams and footings with stated rules, and analyses the frame as a linear three-dimensional model under the load combinations of the National Building Code of Canada 2020 [2, 3]. It reports column loads, bending moments, a capacity ratio, storey drift under wind and suggestions such as braces or transfer walls. This report documents the layout rules, loads, materials and solver, and their sources. Material properties, occupancy live loads and load combinations follow Canadian standards; the solver reproduces closed-form beam results and closes equilibrium to machine precision. Several inputs are assumptions: a fixed roof snow load of 1.0 kPa, a wind pressure of 0.6 kPa, wall strengths and a soil bearing pressure. The audit found two defects, both corrected on 21 November 2026: the frame analysis used the residential live load whatever the building's use, and a wind sway of h/2,762,239 was reported because bearing walls act as rigid horizontal supports. The study is a screening for early design; it excludes earthquake, second-order effects and code checks, and an engineer designs the structure.", "body": "## 1. Introduction\n\nApproximate structural design, from span-to-depth rules and tributary areas, has long been part of architectural practice and teaching [1]. Matrix structural analysis of a 3D frame is standard engineering practice and can now run in a browser [4]. Combining the two allows a layout to be generated from the architecture and checked for load paths, member sizes and lateral stiffness, provided its assumptions are visible.\n\n## 2. Method\n\n### 2.1. Layout\n\nWalls of stone, masonry or concrete 150 mm or thicker are taken as bearing walls; glass, gypsum, plaster and thinner walls are partitions. The user can change any wall's role. Grid lines follow bearing wall lines first, then partition lines that split long bays, and lines are added where no wall exists so that bays do not exceed the chosen span (7.5 m by default). Columns stand at grid intersections not on bearing walls. Bearing walls are checked for thickness against their line load; a wall over nothing on the floor below is flagged.\n\n### 2.2. Systems and sizing\n\n| System | Dead load (kPa) | Depth rule | Column design stress |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Concrete flat plate | 24 × max(0.2, span/30) + 1.5 | Slab span/30, at least 200 mm | 20 MPa (35 MPa concrete with 2 % steel) |\n| Steel frame | 3.0 + 1.5 | Beams span/22 under a 140 mm composite deck | 190 MPa |\n| Mass timber | 1.5 + 1.5 | Glulam beams span/18 under 175 mm CLT | 12 MPa |\n\nThe 1.5 kPa term is superimposed dead load (finishes and partitions). Columns are sized from their factored load and the column design stress. Pad footings are √(N / 1.4 / q_a) on a side, with the factored load divided by 1.4 to approximate service load and q_a = 150 kPa by default; strip footings under walls are sized the same way.\n\n### 2.3. Loads and combinations\n\nLive loads follow NBC 2020 Table 4.1.5.3 by occupancy: residential and hotel 1.9 kPa, office and education 2.4 kPa, retail 4.8 kPa, industrial 6.0 kPa [2]. The occupancy is chosen on the study card (residential by default) and sets the floor live load of the frame analysis. Roof snow is 1.0 kPa. Wind is a uniform pressure (0.6 kPa by default) on each facade, in both directions along both axes. Load combinations follow NBC Table 4.1.3.2-A [2]: 1.4D; 1.25D + 1.5L + (1.0S or 0.4W); 1.25D + 1.5S + (1.0L or 0.4W); 1.25D + 1.4W + (0.5L or 0.5S); and service combinations for drift. Earthquake (combination 5) is not included.\n\n### 2.4. Frame analysis\n\nThe structure becomes a 3D frame of beam elements (six degrees of freedom per node) with slab strips for flat plates and braces as axial members [4]. Supports are at the footings and along bearing walls. The linear static system is solved directly; results include axial force, shear, torsion and bending moment in every member, displacements, normal and von Mises stress, a capacity ratio for screening, and storey drift under service wind, compared with h/400. Where any floor rests on a bearing wall, the wall restrains it horizontally and the floor diaphragm ties the rest of the frame to it, so the frame cannot sway; the study then reports that the walls hold the building against wind and does not give a drift. Material properties are given in Table 1.\n\n## 3. Parameters and their sources\n\nTable 1. Materials, loads and settings of the Structure study.\n\n| Parameter | Value | Source | Audit status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Steel | Grade 350W, E 200 GPa, ρ 7,850 kg/m³ | CSA G40.21; CSA S16 [5] | Sourced |\n| Concrete | f′c 30 MPa, E 24.6 GPa, ρ 2,400 kg/m³ | CSA A23.3: E_c = 4500√f′c [6] | Sourced |\n| Glulam | Douglas fir 24f-E, E 12.4 GPa, f_b 30.6 MPa | CSA O86 [7] | Sourced |\n| W-shape and tube sections | Nominal dimensions, fillets ignored | Steel handbooks [5] | Sourced (slightly conservative) |\n| Live loads | 1.9 / 2.4 / 4.8 / 6.0 kPa | NBC 2020 Table 4.1.5.3 [2] | Sourced |\n| Live load in the frame analysis | The chosen occupancy's value (residential by default) | NBC 2020 Table 4.1.5.3 [2] | Corrected 21 November 2026; before, residential 1.9 kPa for every building |\n| Load combinations | NBC Table 4.1.3.2-A, without E | NBC 2020 [2] | Sourced |\n| Roof snow | 1.0 kPa | Engine choice | Flagged: not from the site's ground snow load; NBC S = I_s [S_s(C_b C_w C_s C_a) + S_r] [2] |\n| Wind | Uniform 0.6 kPa | Engine choice | Flagged: not from NBC q, C_e, C_g, C_p [2] |\n| Superimposed dead load | 1.5 kPa | Common allowance [1] | Plausible |\n| Span-to-depth rules | Slab /30; steel /22; glulam /18 | Rules of thumb [1] | Plausible for preliminary sizing |\n| Wall strengths | 1.5 MPa masonry or stone; 8 MPa concrete | Engine choice | Flagged: no source |\n| Soil bearing q_a | 150 kPa | Engine choice | Flagged: site-specific; requires geotechnical data |\n| Service load | Factored / 1.4 | Engine choice | Approximation |\n| Drift limit | h/400 | Common serviceability limit | Plausible; practice ranges about h/400–h/600 [8] |\n\n## 4. Verification and validation status\n\nThe solver contains analytical self-checks, and three test files exercise the layout and analysis. In our runs on 20–21 November 2026 all passed:\n\n- `tests/structure-frame.cjs`: a cantilever's base moment 30.00 kN·m and tip deflection 29.37 mm equal PL and PL³/3EI; a simply supported beam's mid-span moment 22.50 kN·m and end shear 15.00 kN equal wL²/8 and wL/2; a laid-out building of 24 columns and 34 beams (492 unknowns) closes equilibrium to 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ with a wind drift of h/292, reduced to h/4,829 by 8 X braces carrying axial force only; a flat plate with 34 slab strips and 20 wall supports is in equilibrium.\n- `tests/structure-layout.cjs`: bearing and partition roles from wall materials and thicknesses, grid lines on bearing walls first, columns off bearing walls, an unsupported upper wall flagged, beams in stock sizes, and footing sizes from the stated formulas.\n- `tests/workspace-structure-analysis.cjs`: a hospital frame of 198 columns and 345 slab strips (drift h/1,102 against h/400), every result type drawn, load combinations, member selection, braces and CSV export.\n\nIn the end-to-end audit on the two-storey audit block (concrete flat plate, 8 of 26 walls bearing), the study laid out a 6 × 3 grid with bays of 4.0–7.0 m and 300 × 300 mm columns, and reported a heaviest factored ground-floor column load of 638 kN, a largest factored moment of 117 kN·m, a highest capacity ratio of 57 % and a wind sway of h/2,762,239. After the correction the study reports instead that the bearing walls hold the floors against wind, with the sway not computed. The three test files above were run again after the corrections and all checks pass.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\n**Wind sway with bearing walls.** The audit's drift of h/2,762,239 means the frame barely moved, because bearing walls are modelled as supports that restrain the floors horizontally. Walls do provide lateral stiffness, but not infinite stiffness, and masonry walls need to be checked as shear walls. The study now suppresses the drift where bearing walls are supports and states that the walls carry the wind, and that their racking is not checked; the frame-only drift of h/292 in the test, before bracing, shows the order of magnitude when walls do not help. Modelling the walls as shear panels with their own stiffness would let the study report a real drift.\n\n**Loads.** The live loads by occupancy are correct. At the time of the audit the frame analysis used the residential value for every building, which understated loads by 21 % for offices and by 60 % for retail; the occupancy is now chosen on the study card. The roof snow load and the wind pressure are fixed values; in Canada they should come from the NBC climatic data for the site [2], and elsewhere from the local code. SimuSite's Snow and Wind studies use the project weather file; linking the roof snow load to the site's design ground snow load would remove an inconsistency.\n\n**Scope.** The study is linear, first-order and static, without earthquake, connection design, punching shear, vibration or fire. Its capacity ratio is a screening of stress against strength, not a code member check. The method text states that an engineer designs the structure.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe Structure study's materials, occupancy live loads and NBC load combinations are correctly sourced, and its solver is verified against closed-form solutions. Several loads and strengths are fixed assumptions that should be site-specific, and the lateral stiffness of bearing walls is not modelled, so no drift is given when they carry the wind. As a tool for testing grids, spans and systems against the drawn architecture in early design, it is useful; its numbers should be confirmed by an engineer.", "backmatter": [{"title": "CRediT authorship contribution statement", "text": "SimuSite Studio: Methodology, Software, Validation, Writing – original draft. The text was drafted with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) from the engine source and the test results named in it. Human subject-matter review is pending."}, {"title": "Declaration of competing interest", "text": "The authors develop SimuSite, the software described in this article. No external funding was received."}, {"title": "Data availability", "text": "Engine source: [mvp/js/structure-layout.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/structure-layout.js), [mvp/js/structure-frame.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/structure-frame.js), [emb/lib/structural-solver.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/emb/lib/structural-solver.js), [mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/js/workspace-chrome.js). Tests: [tests/structure-layout.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/structure-layout.cjs), [tests/structure-frame.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/structure-frame.cjs), [tests/workspace-structure-analysis.cjs](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/tests/workspace-structure-analysis.cjs). This article's source: [structure.md](https://github.com/plotcloudstudio/plotcloud/blob/main/mvp/research/reports/structure.md)."}], "references": ["E. Allen, J. Iano, The Architect's Studio Companion: Rules of Thumb for Preliminary Design, 6th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, 2017.", "National Research Council Canada, National Building Code of Canada 2020, Division B, Part 4, Structural design, NRC, Ottawa, 2022.", "National Research Council Canada, Structural Commentaries (User's Guide – NBC 2020: Part 4 of Division B), NRC, Ottawa, 2022.", "W. McGuire, R.H. Gallagher, R.D. Ziemian, Matrix Structural Analysis, 2nd ed., Wiley, New York, 2000.", "Canadian Institute of Steel Construction, Handbook of Steel Construction, 12th ed., CISC, Markham, 2021 (with CSA S16-19 and CSA G40.21).", "CSA Group, CSA A23.3:19, Design of concrete structures, CSA, Toronto, 2019.", "CSA Group, CSA O86:19, Engineering design in wood, CSA, Toronto, 2019.", "L.G. Griffis, Serviceability limit states under wind load, Eng. J. AISC 30 (1) (1993) 1–16."], "category": "research", "simulation": "structure", "summary": "How the Structure study lays out a structural system from the drawn walls, sizes columns, beams and footings with stated rules, and analyses the resulting frame as a linear 3D model under NBC 2020 load combinations. The report gives the sources of the materials, loads and combinations, reports the solver's analytical checks, and flags the assumed loads and strengths and a wind-sway result that is not meaningful when bearing walls are treated as lateral supports.", "image": "img/home/structure.webp", "imageAlt": "A building's structural frame with columns, beams and slab strips coloured by bending moment"}}];
